        <head>PICTURED ROME</head>
        <p>PICTURED ROME</p>
        <p>SANKEY STREET, NEXT TO G.P.O.</p>
        <p>MONDAY, TUESDAY &amp; :WEDN ESDAY, FEB. 3RD, 4TH, &amp;STH. "THE BLACK MASK."</p>
        <p>THURSDAY, FRIDAY AND SATURDAY, FEB. 6TH, 7TH &amp; FEB. BTH. "THE SURGEON."</p>
        <p>RYAL THEATRE OF VAR lETIES SCOTLAND ROAD, WARRINGTON. 6.SO—TWICE NIGHTLY.-9. MONDAY, FEBRUARY 3RD. THE " ROYAL " STOCK CO. IN THE POWERFUL DRAMA, "FOR MONEY.-NOT LOVE." Full Matinee Thursdays, at 2.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>CHURCH SOCIETY.- LECIT JRES IN THE PARISH CHURCH DURING LENT. NEXT FRIDAY. FEBRUARY 7TH.</p>
        <p>w ARRINGTON</p>
        <p>REV. BERNARD HEYWOOD, Vicar of Swinton</p>
        <p>AT 3 P.M. FOR WOMEN. "PRAYER." AT 8 P.M. FOR MEN. "MARRIAGE AND DIVORCE."</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LITERARY AND PHILOSOPFIIOAL SOCIETY. PROFESSOR L. T. HOBHOUSE, Professor of Sociology in the University of London, Fellow ,of Weston College, Oxford, will give an Address, entitled: "BIOLOGY AND POLITICS" at the CO-OPERATIVE HALL, CAIRO STREET, on MONDAY NEXT, FEBRUARY 3rd, at 8 p.m. CHAIRMAN: 0. LOFTHOUSE. Esq., President. ADMISSION FREE.</p>
        <p>W ARRINGTON PERMANENT BENEFIT BUILDING SOCIETY. ANNUAL AUDIT. Members are kindly requested to send in their Pass Books to Mr Edwin Bradshaw, Chartered Accountant, 4, Egypt-street, Warrington, one of the Society's Auditors, not later than Saturday, the Bth February. ALFRED H. OLIVE, Secretary. 6, Egypt-street. Warrington.</p>
        <p>CRISIS IN THE CHINESE OPIUM TRAFFIC. LECTURE. (Illustrated by Limelight). IN THE FRIENDS' MEETING HOUSE, BUTTERMARKET STREET, ON TUESDAY, FEBRUARY 4TH, 1913, BY MR. NOEL BRAMHALL (of the Anti-Opium League.) Chair to be taken at 8 p.m. by • THE REV. 'W. COOPER. ADMISSION FttEE. ALL WELCOME.</p>
        <p>' '; 7 ent)36":atal OF --WARD:iictltOlVl— tippi- TENDERS are invited for the supply of Uniform Clothing, Hats, (Ice., 1.6 the several Depart- ments of the Corporation for the ensuing twelve months. Particulars, &amp;a., may be oLtained on and after Monday next, the 3rd instant, at the Borough Treasurer's Office, Bank House, Sankey Street, and tenders, securely fastened and endorsed ." Tenders for Clothing "should be sent in to the undersigned, not later than twelve o'clock noon on the 12th Ve'o., 1913. The contractor whose tendkr is accepted will be required to observe the recognised customs and conditions as to the rate of wages and working hours prevailing within the district. The lowest or any tender not necessarily accepted. JOHN FAIRHuRsT, Borough Treasurer. Municipal Offices, Warrotgton. Ist Feeruary, 1913.</p>
        <p>COUNTY • BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. TO STORES CONTRAC: ORS, EA RTHEN- WARE PIPE MANUFAGTURERS, QUARRY OWNERS, MERCHANTS, TEAM. OWNERS &amp;c. The Paving aid Sewerage Comerii tee of the Council of the County Borough of Warrington is prepared to receive TENDERS for the supply of the Committee's requirements during the tmolve months ending March 31st 1914, viz,: CONCRETE FLAGS. RAWTENSTALL OR YORK FLAGS. RAWTENSTALL HERBS. BLUE BRICKS. CHEQUERED TILES. SEWER PIPES. BENDS, JUNCTIONS &amp;c. MACADAM AND CHIPPING'S. LIM 14.:SIONE CHIPPING'S. COPPER SLAG. PORTLAND CEMENT. CAST IRON GULI,TES. BRUSHES, HAMMERS, SCUTCHES &amp;c. SCAVENGERS' BROOMS, SHOVELS, SCUFFLES AND SCOOPS STREET SWEEPING MACHINE ROTARY BROOMS. Also for Carting for the Highway and Scavenging Departments for the tweve months. Parties wishing for Tender Forms will please specify those materials only for which they propose to Tender. • Specifications and samples may be seen and forms of tender and all fur( her information obtained at ,the Office of the Borough Surveyor, Town Hall, on and after Tuesday next, the 4th inst., at which plane Fenders, sealed in the envelopes supplied, must be delivered not later than 10 o'clock a.m. on Saturday the 15th day of February, 1913. • Any Contractor. whose Tender may be accepted will be required to observe the recognised customs and conditions as to the rate of wages and working hours prevailing within the Borough. The Committee reserve the right to divide the contracts in any way they may think fit, and do not bind themselves to accept the lowest or any Tender. ANDREW M. HER, Borough Engineer and Surveyor. Borough Engineer and Surveyor's Office, January 31st, 1913.</p>
