      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WAF:HUIGTON Experierae:—</head>
        <p>A WAF:HUIGTON Experierae:—</p>
        <p>lir. J. Price lives at 46, Queen-street, Warring- tcn. He says : " Four years ago I ha.' an awful attack of lumbago. I got up ()Ike morning in terrible pain—l could scarcely get out of bed and dress myself, and the effort to get to work caused me to perspire, such agony was I in. Then I set out for home, but how I reached there I do not know, and the perspiration fairly rolled off me. "I had no use in the lower pArt of my body, my legs being powerless. The kidney excretions were unnatural. I got about with two sticks, and a neighbour seeing me and asking what was the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Backache • • .v • lib t: &gt;VI kb Kidney</head>
        <p>Backache • • .v • lib t: &gt;VI kb Kidney</p>
        <p>COMING OF AGE.</p>
        <p>JUVENILE RECHABITES' CELEBRATIONS.</p>
        <p>Interesting Presentations.</p>
        <p>One of the most interesting events in the annals of the John Dimond tent 911, of the Juvenile Order of Rechabites, Warrington District, took place on Monday evening when the 21st anniversary of the tent was celebrated by a large number of members in the Friars Green schoolroom. The proceedings took the form of a tea and entertainment, at which, prizes were pre- sented to a number of children by Mrs Dodds of Penketh. Presentations were also made to Bros. Wright and Massey of barometers inscribed to the effect that they were given in appreciation of the 21 years service they had rendered as officers of the tent. For having passed through the various offices of the order Bro. Arthur Hamblett received a Chief Ruler's sash. The tea, which was well served, was partaken of by about IEO members and amongst others who assisted at the tables were the wives of the superintendents. At the entertainment Bro. W Wright presided and he was sunported by Mrs Dodds and members of the Order. In his opening remarks the CHAIRMAN said he was acting in the absence of Bro Lowe who was their superintendent. As one of the workers, he (the speaker), thought that for discipline, instruction and general conduct of the members the Fist year had been one of the best they had experieccel for a long time. Twenty- one iyears last November the John Dimond Juvenile Tent was instituted in connection with Charity Tent. At that time they found that. the early work of the Rechabites had practically died out in Warrington. It was therefore decided by the Charity Tent members to form and institute a juvenile tent. That institution took place about the 10th November, in the Brick-street Sunday school and about 20 members joined. The John Dimond Juvenile Tent had been interesting and progressive and they continued to grow until that tent held the premier position in the 3nvenile District, and to-day they had a member- ship of :100. Applause.) They had had more than that, but a number had been transferred to the Adult Tent, and that had somewhat lessened their ranks. From a humble beginning, they continued to advance and at the present time they had 12 or 13 juvenile tents in Warrington with over 1,000 members. (Applause.) Looking bark on such a record one saw that good work had been accomplished and it was a source of pride that that there were at least three present. that night who bad seen the whole 21 years with the tent. Two of them were superintendents at the commencement and were still in that position to-day. (Applause.) He was sure there was nothing which gave a man or woman greater pleasure than to look back on 21 years' service rendered in the noble cause of Temperance. (Hear,bear) The influence of that Rechabite movement had spread until, he helieved,it was the main temperance influence in the town to-day, (Hear, hear.) He hoped it would continue and increase in power. They were not as yet quite on the mountain top ; the drinking customs of our land were not entirely broken down. There was a great deal of sin and poverty and they would have to do much before they could siy that the temperance victory was won. They were out to send their temper- ance principles into hundreds of homes in Warrington in order that the people would be lifted up to a higher plane which would all he for the good of the whole town. 'Applause.) They had with them that night their friend and sister Mrs Dodds. They knew that she was not merely an ornament to their order but she was a helpful and willing worker in numerous respects Mrs Dodds, by her personal influence and character was a great pillar in their temperance movement. Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PRIZE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>The children who received prizes at the hands of Mrs Dodds were :—Frank Yates, Amy Jolley, Herbert Jolley. Sarah Jolley, Beatrice Ryder, Lucy Carey, Elsie Hardman, William Hardman, Alice Hardmao, John Hardmaa, Lily Hardman, Bessie Barton, Mary A Barton, James Lomas, F Lowe, T Lowe, Amy B Lowe, S Littlewood, F Littlewood, W Murray, H Murray, N Murray, S Murray, Rose Welford, Corwena, Jones, Harold Holfoni, James Scruton, Frank HoHord, Charles Sankey, E Ashworth, Charles Brookes, Arthur Davies, Harold Davies, H Spruce, F Spruce, Ernest Spruce, Albert Powell, Sam Littlewood, Ethel Clare, Harry Clare. N B M Davenport, Wm C Davenport, Mary H Barnes, Suzette Barnus, Sarah Barnes, Emily Scruton. Harold Scruton, Phyllis Lowe, Ethel Howard, Stanley Slater, A Wilkes, Ernest Buckley, Wm Lawton, May Woodland. Sister Ethel Clara was awarded a book prize, the gift of Mr Naylor, for introducing the largest number of members into the Tent during the year 1912. Mrs DoDD9, after presenting the prizes said that the temperance victory was not yet won. The boys and girls traine:l in the Rechabite juvenile tents, could assist in helping to secure that victory by remaining true to their pledge cad temperance principles until they reached ruanhoad. They would then he able to strengthen the forces of the temperance army and to strike a strong blow against the evils of the adrink traffic, and its direful results of crime, poverty and degradation, which would result in a complete victory for a sober tad happier condition of things in this country. (Artplaose). She complimented the workers on the splendid results of the teaching given in the tents and the success of the Rechabites order evidenced by snob a large number of the members present that night. She was glad to be present to see sa many to wish the John Dimond Juvenile Tent many happy returns of their 21st birthday. Councillor Bro James Shaw was called upon to make a presentation to Bro T Massey and William Wright of a barometer ach. The inscription was " Independent Order of Rechabites, John Dimond Juvenile Tent, instituted November 10th, 1891, in appreciation of 21 years service, as superintendent of the tent." The speaker said that he had a long connection with both of the iecipients and could speak with some authority and</p>
