      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LONDON CITY AND MIDLAND BANK.</head>
        <p>LONDON CITY AND MIDLAND BANK.</p>
        <p>" DEAR " GOLD AND THE PRICE OF COMMODITIES.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the London City and Midland Bank Ltd., was held last kriday at the Cannon- street Hotel, London, E.C. Sir Edward Holden, Bart , chairman, presided, and in moving the adoption of the report he reviewed the salient conditious which bad prevai!ed and were now current in the leading money markets of the world. As regarded our own country, there was evidence on all sides that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>TRADE DAD BEEN _GOOD,</head>
        <p>TRADE DAD BEEN _GOOD,</p>
        <p>Having given an exhaustive review of the monetary conditions prevailing in the leading money markets of the world the Chairman pointed out that dear money increased the prices of commodities to the consumers, and drove down the prices of securities. The liabili- ties of the countries he had reviewed had increased in a greater ratio than their gold. In this country the rate nad been higher for the purpose of protecting our gold, and although we had had resources to a rate of 5 per cent., yet the proportion of our resource of gold to liabilities this year was about 3 per cent, lower than it was at the same time last year. Why had we and the above mentioned countries not been able to obtain and retain more gold? The answer was, he said, because it had been taken by India and if those other countries continued in future to increase their trade without being able to increase their gold, then a crisis similar to or even greater than, that in America in 1907 would assuredly take place. Which would cause loans to be called in, loans to be contracted, and great losses to ensue. Being face to face with these conditions, the Indi an question was one which ought to be thoroughly discussed, for during the last two and threequarter years, India had taken no less than 67 millions sterling of gold in bars and sovereigns and little of it had returned. He hoped the Secretary for State for India might be iuduced seriously to consider the question of appoint- ing without delay a fresh commission. not .only in the interests of this country, but also in the interests of all those countries which were now unable to obtain gold to nits:it their increase in trade,increasing liabilities and ia?reasing credit. There were serious dangers ahead and it had become a pressing necessity to provide protection against them. Finally the Chairman made a running commentary on the leading figures of the balance sheet. Their deposits at the end of 1911 were about £4,300,000 higher than at the end of 1910, and at the end of 1912 they were nearly six millions higher than at the end of 1911. Iu two years therefore, they had increased about 10 millions and now stood at £83,664,000. The progress of a Bank depended to a large extent on the increase of its deposits- Their profits for the year 1912, after making additions to their internal reserves amounted to £1,010,000. (Applause.) The interim dividend which they paid and the dividend they now proposed gave them 18 per cent. on the shares. They further paid the income tax amounting to £41,887, which was slightly over 1 per cent of their capital. They there- fore paid in respeci of dividend and income tax an amount equal to 19 per cent on their capital. They provided £160,000 for their investments, £50,000 for Bank Premises Redemption Fund, £20,000 for 011icers' Pension Fund, £10,01,0 for Stall Widows' Fund, carry- ing forward £133,000. He thought these figures spoke for themselves and he ventured to think their results for 1912 would meet with approval. (Applause.) 11e moved the adoption of the report. The resolution was seconded by the Deputy-Chair - man (Mr W G Bradshaw), and passed. The next resolution, declaring a dividend of £1 2s 6,1 per share, making with the interim dividend paiu for the half-year ended ;;Oth Juno last, 18 per cent. for the year free of income tax, was also passed. The retiring directors, Mr William Graham Bradshaw and Mr William Fitz Thomas Wyley, were re-elected. as elso were the auditors. Messrs Whinney Smith and Whinney. The usual complimentary resolutions to the Chair- man, Board of Directors, managers and stall were unanimously agreed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>MELBOURNE SAILS.</head>
        <p>THE MELBOURNE SAILS.</p>
        <p>The Melbourne, the first protected cruiser built at the expense of. the Commonwealth Government of Australia, left Plymouth Sound on Thursday on her voyage to the Antipodes. She takes out with her a large number of men of the Imperial Navy, who have transferred to the Colonial service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>OLD BOYS.</head>
        <p>OLD BOYS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL DINNER AT THE GRAMMAR</p>
        <p>SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND THE COUNCIL</p>
        <p>"An Ugly Mirror."</p>
        <p>Happy memories of school days were conjured up on Saturday evening by "old boss" of the Boteler Grammar School who gathered to show their affection for the honoured school in which their boyhood was spent and to cement the frierdshipi formed in those early years. The re-union was the eleventh of its kind and the excepticnally large attendance was a proof of the increasing popularity of the function. It was also a complete justification of the departura made by the committee in arranging the dinner for a Saturday evening, for the ( hoice f date SE emed to meet the general convenience much mole crmpletely than on any previous occasion. It is also an exceedingly happy idea to meat within the walls of the old s(hrol its-elf, for the sentimental associations which play so large a part in the pro- motion of such a gathering are thereby consider-0)1y emphasised. The President for this year is Mr E A White and among other prominent guests were : Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Bev E C E Carleton, Rev. Horace Gray (headmaster), Alderman A Bennett, Mr Roger C Parr, Mr J Moore Nlurray (director of educa- tier)); Mes-rs. Cl:as S Burgess, Vele B Davies and C D Parkinson (past presidents), J H Smethurst (chairman of committee), Leonaid Pierp.. jut (hon. treasurer), and Stanley Walton (hon. secretary). Dinner was served by the Winrnarleigh Cafe Co., the catering giving every satisfaction. The toast cl "The King " as honoured on the proposition of the PRESIDENT. THE WILLCOCKS I\IEIIIOMM, FUND.</p>
        <p>Mr 3 II S'ivlETHl.7iisT submitted the toast of the frunder—" The pious memory of Sir Thomas Boteltr," and took the opportunity of referring to the WilicEcks Memorial Fond. He puinted out that they bad the sum of .4.,“"25 invested in the Warrinpton Corporation at 3?, per cent., and they had also £l6 10s in Parr's lien, making a total of £241 los ltd. For the first time in the history of the fund, they had bad an application this year to make a grant to abey who was leaving the sChool for one of the Universities. On the recom- mendation of the Headmaster they decided to grant a boy named Woodcock the sum Of £25, pa, able in three instalments. He believed the Rev E J Wincocks would have desired no rnors fitting perpetuation of his memory than that way of helping deserving boys--and he (Mr. Smethurst) believed Woodcock was a most dee erving boy who would do credit to the old school. (Applause ) Re'terring te the provision of an annual prize by the "old boys," Mr. Smethurst said the committee felt that, the prize should be guaranteed for at leaq, two or thief) yews, and accordiugly those " Old Buys" who could not attend the dinner were-invited to contribute 2s each The sum of £3 15s had been raised •in that nay. The committee had also been considering the possibility of altering the constitution of their organisation. At the present time they had an Old Boys' Union without a membership end the suegestion was that they should have a definite Union with a definite subscription of a small amount and a definite membership. The com- mittee agreed that the sooner they could come to an arrangement of that kind, the better. The Headmaster would, no doubt, use his influence with boys leaving school to induce them to join the Old Boys' Union and they would then have no difficulty in blinding up a real Union which would he of value to all who chose to enrol themselves. (Applause). The toast WbS honoured in solemn silence. "PROSPERITY TO THE SCHOOL."</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATIONAL VALUE OF NOT WORKING. The PRESIDENT, in proposing " Prosperity to the school " gave a witty speech full of interesting reminiscences of his own school days which went back to 1876. On the general question of education, he said they used to hear a great deal of argument as to the value of various subjects taught in the schools. There had been repeated discussions as to whether the educa- tional value of the classics or of the higher mathematics was sufficient to justify their continuance. There was, however, one phase of the abject which he never heard discussed, and that was : What is the educational value of not working at all ? (Laughter.) It might sound a hit strange, hut if they followed out the idea care- fully, they would find that in schools there was a con- siderable educational value in not working. When a boy, he was told that education did not mean what they put into a boy, but what they brought out of him, and that the classics and various branches of the higher mathematics were useful in the same way the gymnastic exercises were useful—the one. to educate and train the brain and the other to develop the muscles. Mr White then in a humourous way alluded to the " excellent mental training " which the unwilling boy received in evolving ingenious schemes for neglecting hiw home lessons and creating the impression that be was as well-informed as the more diligent pupil. In school life generally the school master and the boy were natural reemies—(laughter)— and in the waging of that very properly enmity, the boy got a good training for the battle of life. He became accustomed to facing failure and taking "lickings"—a training which he would find exceedingly useful when he undertook the serious business of life. He would also learn how to extricate him- self from tight corners. (Laughter.) On the whole, although the scholastic attainments of the boy who would not work might not be very high, his experiences in some cases accounted for h " by does a brilliant school toy often turn le: n oriudtdafailure in after-life, while the boy who is not brilliant at school is found to achieve success?" In school life, unfortunately, there was a strong tendency to remember the things one ought to forget, and to forget the things one ought to remember. %%hen the late Lord Winrnarleigh hart attained the age of 70, be (the speaker), had the privilege of conversing with him on several Occasions and his lordehip favoured him with a few anecdotes of his early days. The only story he related of his school life, however, was bow he played truant in order to watch the Princess Charlotte's funeral procession and how be got flogged for doing it. He (Mr White) gathered that in after-life his lordship thought it was well worth it. (Laughter.) "We used to wonder in our school days" continued Mr White "why those wretched people wrote those classics. (Laughter.) We had a special grudge against Cwsar. We thought he was such an awfully conceited chap,always telling us what he did. (Laughter.) It was quite enough to have done those things without writing about them. (Laughter.) We were also told that a certain number of the books of Euclid had been lost, and we thought it a very unreasonable thing that a similar fate bad not befallen the whole of them. (Laughter.) As T understand they have abolished Euclid from most of the schools, we were only indulging in intelligent anticipation. (Laugh ter.)The most humorous thing that over happened in school in my day was during a hot summer afternoon, when the whole school was in this room, busily engaged in an examination. The door opened straight on to the playground and in the middle of the afternoon the headmaster's pony gravely walked in, looked in en intelligent manner round the schooleossed up his head in disgust end walked out by the other door. I never heard such a hearty burst of laughter in school during the whole of the time f was there, and I think the boys agreed with the pony that we were very foolish in spending a lovely summer afternoon in that way, although we thought he did not understand that we were only doing it because we were compelled. " (Laughter.) Having referred to the glamour which surrounded school life in after years, Mr White said it was natural for " old boys " to cherish the illusion that things were better in their day than they are now. Viewing the matter seriously,however, they must admit that progress had been made and they all sincerely hoped that the school—removed at no very distant date to a better situation—would continue to prosper. (Applause.) The REV HORACE GRAY acknowledged the toast. He said that as part of a general knowledge paper recently set at the school he asked the question : " What would you say in response to the toast, 'Prosperity to the school '" ? He thought such a question might excite the minds of young Botelerians but, to his astonishment, he got only two answers out of the whole school. Air Gray read one of the answers which harked back to the days when the school had only three masters—including a writing master who received .£lO a year—and 30 boys. To-day there were eight masters—all University men—and 130 boys. (Applause). Having commended the effort to encourage the spirit of comradeship between old Botelerians, Mr Gray touched upon the many differ- ing theories advanced on the question of education, and said that only the other day he read an article in which it was laid down as a fact that no great chess player ever made a successful business man There were two great ideals of education—and they were as far apart as the poles. One was repression and the other was guidance. They had probably rill had experience of the master who taught with a long switch on the top of his desk and when he asked a question' the boys had to respond as quickly as it took to say Jack Robinson, or the switch descended. (Laughter.) With the rank and file repression had been largely ttied, but the teacher who adopted the methods of guidance was the one who in time to come would succeed. It was not generally realized that no man could make another learn anything, if he did not wish to learn it. The lot of the idler might be made so difficult and disagreeable that he would appear to learn, hut the knowledge he acquired was very superficial and when the repressive power NVIS removed, that which he had stored by also disappeared. There were many pupils in some schools who were in such a mood that they would learn nothing, except under compulsion. He did not think there were many boys of that type in the Boteler Grammar School. It was not easy for boys to escape, but where that type of pupil did exist, it was largely due to bad training. In the majority of cases, that bad</p>
        <p>training was to be attributed to the parents, and it was sometimes followed up by the school master. The school had to put up with those scholars, but it could never make a success of them unless some happy tnuch came to the pupil's will and he wanted to learn. What they wanted to do was to use the boys' natural instincts. In this connection Air Gray commended the direct method of teaching languages and history—the text book being used as a suggestion, and the boy becoming an active unit in the lessons. Finally be quoted the following line., written by a BGtelerian., which be thought fittingly represented the feelings of "old boys" tows:cis the school --</p>
        <p>Sail on, 0 splendid in your vast resolve ! The goal draws near—lint say, cen ye forget With ne'er a sigh the lov'd things left?—Those eycs, Betray they not some stirring of regt et ? (Applause.) A c,dlection was then made in aid of the "old boys'" prize fund nod realized £-1 7s. making, with the amount sent by those who were unable to attoud the dinner, a total of 1:8 2.</p>
        <p>"-PROSPERITY TO THE COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON."</p>
        <p>Mr A.E BowEN proposed "Prosperity to the County Borough of Warrington." His speech consisted chiefly of a recapitulation of the salient features of the history of the town, special reference being made to the work of the Roteler family and to the erection of successive Warringtcn bridges. Mr Bowen also alluded to the tar-off days when Warrington not only supplied neight oaring towns with fruit and vegetables, tut exported potatoes to the Meditcrreuesn. Wairington ranked as one of the earliest manufacturing towns in Lancashire, the produc- tion of linen and sail cloth being two of its earliest industries. Air. Bowen remarked on the s,all wages received by the workers in three early times. Women were paid 2d a day for spinning, and men got an average of lba a week. Women received 23 6d a week for wind- ing and men 7s a week for warping. Men were paid 9s a week for weaving, and women got 55 and boys 3s 6d. At shoc-inaking an average wage of 9s per week could I e earned. With retard to the cost of living, cheese was bought at :;b.d. a pound, beef at 3d., bacon at milk at 21. a quart, while ',louse rent ranged lion, 205., to 303. a year. Referring to the Warrington of to-day the speaker expressed satisfaction at the efforts of the Education Committee to promote the health of the school children and especially commended the appoint- ment of a school dental officer. Trade at the present time was booming and the reason why such prosperity was to be valued was that it brought happiness to the people. (Applause.) ALDERMAN BEI\TNETT'S CHARACTERISTIC SpEEe El. THE COUNCIL AS AN "UGLY MIRROR."</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT in responding, said he was very much struck with two phases in the history of the lioteler family—the devotion with which they dis- charged !heir duty to the State arid to their native town. There was hardly a battle during the three or four hundred years in which the Botelers were at their prime, in which they did not take part. Warrirgton owed a great debt to the family for their local activities. They were identified with the Church, the Friary and the earlier Warrington bd They established our weekly markets which went back ack four or five hundred years and which were still held on the original dsy (Wednesday). They also founcled.our fairs which had practically fallen into disuse, after a course of some centuries. In the year 1300 they won for Nlarringter. its Magua Charta which secured to the inhaLitents privileges which, in their „y, were elmcst as important as those secured by the Magna Cherta wrung by the Barons front King John. Last, but by no means least, they founded tuat ancient institution. the Grammar School. Impressing upon his hearers the cksirability of following out the old Boteler traditions, the Alderman said there was a magni- ficent, field for public activity in the locality in which they lived. He sometimes thought many of their modern citizens—even old Butelerians—were somewhat blind to that phase of public duty. National duty perhaps appealed to them more prominently and, of course, there was more limelight about it. But it was not one whit more important than local duty. If they really desired to promote the prosperity of the town, and it all their perorations were not to he mere empty words, they ought to try to identify themselves with local lite and to do something for it. He said it with great regret, but be was sure there was a great lack of public spirit in Warrington—except at football matches and that kind of thing. (Laughter.) They were ready enough to show their pride in Warrington when paying a certain number of men to fight their battles in the field of sport, and they thought Warring- ton was winning great glory by achievements of that character. Put he did not think there was much glory in it ; the whole business was too mercenary. flow many were really prepared to wake sacrifices for the town ? Take the subject of public generosity—it was absolutely lamentable. They had had instances to the contrary from friends who had been with them at that festive board but, speaking generally, how little the RICH MEN OF WARRINGTON did for us ! Those great benefactions that we read of in similar towns—how few and far between they were in \Narrington. Len again, the Lumber of townspeople who took part in municipal service was pitifully limited The disgraceful scenes occurring in the Town Council were common talk on every trait:near and in every haunt of local life. Whose fault was it ? It was the fault of every old Boteleriaa and of every citizen of Warrington. "The Town Council " continued the alderman" is what you make it. If you don't like the picture, it is your own reflection. You see the image of yourselves in that ugly mirror. I ask you to improve the imago and then yon will see a better reflection. The people elect the Council and if the Council is not worthy of the town—and I sometimes feel it is not—it is the fault of the citizens themselves, and particularly the fault of those who have the ability, knowledge' energy and enterprise and who are too selfish to devote their time to the public service." Continuing he implored all who felt any vocation for public service to enter it. He could not help harking back to the old days very longingly and sometimes with a feeling of great regret, because while there were many things in Warrington to-clay of which they were legitimately proud there were other featnres of which they hid no occasion to be proud. We had "black rain from black skies," the roofs of the houses seemed to run with black sweat which, in turn, ran into a black river. In some quarters there were black and dirty streets, with grimy people. While we boasted about our wealth and menutacturing prosperity, we ought to be ashamed that such things sere possible—that side by side with wealth, there were the slums which existed in Warrington, that we had whole wards in which there was not a single open space, that we had lost the sense of civic beauty and artistic taste, that we had no statues no monuments and few fine public buildings. Under such circumstances wEAf.TH WAS A MOCKERY, a disgrace and a discredit to us. The more wealthy and prosperous we were, the more degraded we were, unless we used that wealth to the best advantarge. Until we had wiped slums away and made the town a decent, livable place, in which the citizens might find oppor- tunities for a healthy, natural life, we had no reason to plume ourselves. We had yet to learn a new method of valuing things. The mere piling up of tremendous fortunes, the mere spread of a town, the growth of its population and the multiplication of industries, were not sufficient, unless those things were accompanied by the higher things for which the school and educational institutions generally stood. Many of our modern towns, in spite of all the false pride which they had in their prosperity and progressosould disgrace an uncivilized community; they were absolute blots on the face of England. It was not necessary that manufactures should carry with them the slum, squalor, and filth that we saw around us in Warrington. Those present could do something to remedy the conditions by sending the right people to the Council, by personal service on those authorities, and by personal sacrifice. He would infinitely rather see evidences of that real activity and public spirit than hear any amount of beautiful words about the town and its past. (Applause.) They must strive to make the future of the town worthy of its splendid past. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>"THE PRESIDENT."</head>
        <p>"THE PRESIDENT."</p>
        <p>The health of "The President "was honoured on the proposition of 1.«.1r. SEGAR OIVEN, who described Mr. White as a true sportsman and scholar, and instanced the fact that when he pawed the examination for his profession he was third in all England. (Applause). A choice musical programme was submitted, the opening item being a pianoforte duet—Schubert's 'Marche Militaire," which was admirably played by Mr F M Darby, Mos. Bac., and Mr W H Payton, Mils. Bac. Songs were given in pleasing style by the Rev Horace Gray, Mr J W Bibby and Mr P 0 Bailey. Mr A E Bowen read an interesting paper aritten by a friend, describing a visit to Grappenhall and a talk with a local "character." Dr Law was, as usual, higly popular in his dramatic recitations. Mr W H Payton played the accompaniments very tastefully.</p>
      </div>
