      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>COUNCIL OR CHURCH SCHOOLS?</head>
        <p>COUNCIL OR CHURCH SCHOOLS?</p>
        <p>NONCONFORMIST OBJECTION TO PROPOSED TRANSFER. The Question of Religious Teaching.</p>
        <p>It is very seldom indeed that the religious difficulty is raised in the Warrington Echication Committee, but it cropped up on Monday evening on the proposal of the School Management Committee to close Cairo- street (Infants) Council School and to transfer the teachers and scholars to the Holy Trinity (Church) Schools. The chief points of debate were as to whether the teachers to be transferred would be expected to give Church teaching and whether the wishes of the parents had bi en consulted. Councillor H Roberts presided and there were also present :—Mr A J jolley (deputy chairman), Aldermen Bennett. James Evans and Smethurst, Councillors Archer, Birtles, Dickenson, Jenkins Lindley, Osborne, Pemberton and Wilkinson, Revs U 'C Briggs and N Wilson, Messrs R T Fairclough, J Jarman, H Kingdon, t.; E Parker, F Roberts, J Roberts, J M Murray (director) and F It Roberts (assistant director). At a meeting of the School Management Committee communications were read from the managers of the Trinity School making a proposal, in view Of the con- siderable reduction in the number of scholars now attending the school, as to the possibility of opening a portion as an infants' division. The committee recommended that, subject to the concurrence of the manaeers and the appioval of the Board of Education (a) the Trinity School be continued as a division for senior mixed scholars as at present, with the existing staff ; (b) the Cairo-street temperary (infants') Council School be closed ; and (c) an infants' division be formed at the Trinie y School, the existiug staff of the Cairo-street temporary (infants') Council School being offered transference thereto on theft existing terms of service. Mr Uesors, in submitting the minutes, said that to disarm any criticism of the fact that they were closing a Council school and transferring the staff, and pro- bably most of the children, to a non-provided school, he would point out that the Cairo-street school hart been condemned for four years in succession by the Medical Officer of Health, because of its insanitary condition. The Medical Officer had reported again and again that the children's health was endangered by their being taught in the school and the structural arrangements were such as to make it impossible for the sanitary conditions to he improved. That having been pointed out on four different occasions, the com- mittee were not prepared to take the risk of keeping 120 to 130 infants in a building so strongly condemned. Owing to the opening of the Evelyn-street Council Schools, the number of scholars attending the Trinity School had been very seriously reduced. Ot course it was impossible to build a new Couticil school which would cover only the immediate requirements at the moment for any given district. Provision was made at Evelyn- street for 1,260 and probably 500 or COO required accommodation in the locality, which adversely affected the attendance in some other neighbouring schools. The numbers in the Trinity School were reduced to SO on the books. Therefore, there was a large waste of space. If they were to find Cannon school accommodation for the infants et Cairo-street they must build. It was impossible to find land upon which to build in that district and the committee thought it was expedient tc try to make some arrange- ment with the Trinity managers, so that they could take over the Cairo-street staff and form a new infants' department there, particularly when it was remembered that the Trinity managers had surrendered an infants' department in Liverpool-road, as a consequence of the Evelyn-street schools beiug opened. He moved that the proposal be accepted. The Rev Father Winsoer seconded. Mr 1, It ROBERTS moved that the minute be referred back. The suggestion was more serious than appeared on the surface. It was a distinctly retrograde step to transfer children from a Council school to a voluntary non-provided school. Had the transfer been in the reverse direction he would have heartily supported it 'lt had to be borne in mind that the infants NS ho would be sent to the Trinity School had been originally sent to Cairo-street as a Council school. The parents doubt- less had exercised some choice in the matter, and had selected Cairo-street because it wai a Council school,free from religious tests, and it would have a disturbing influence on the educational system of the town that such a transfer should now be made. The feelings of the parents of the children and the feelings and opinions of the teachers whom it was proposed to transfer had to be considered. Had the committee taken into con- sideration the objections that parents and children might possibly haw) to transfer ? It was all very well to say that -Trinity School was the only place where accommodation could be found. He should like to be satisfied on that point. Surely there were buildings in the neighbourhood of Cairo-street which might be utilized and he wondered whether any inquiries on that point had been made. It was a much too serious busi- ness to be dismissed in a light, off-hand way. It was desirable that the fullest consideration should be given to the points he had raised and he therefore moved that the whole question be referred back to the committee.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENN'ETT, in seconding the amendment,said be thought the point was deserving of further con- sideration. Whether the proposal was a retrograde step or not he was not prepared to say. To many, possibly, it might have that appearance but he did not think tbe committee ought to adopt the minute with- out the fullest information and every consideration. Mr JARMAN inquired whether in case of the transfer being carried out the infants' department would be absolutely under Church control? The CHAIRMAN : 1 take it the teachers and scholars would be in precisely the same relation to the managers as their present teachers and scholars.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETH URST : Exactly. - Mr JARMAN : That is of course, that they will be under the managers of the Church school and they will be compelled to teach Church subjects as required,such as the Catechism.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Unless special arrangements are made to the contrary, undoubtedly Mr R T FAIRCLOUGH disagreed with the attitude and views of Mr Roberts, who he considered was sub- mitting his amendment too much on religious grounds. A Church school was to be transferred to a Council school at Latchford, and the managers of tie Christ Church School were not contemplating raising any such points as that; they were approaching the position simply from the point of view of the benefit of the community and of the children. (Mr JOLLEY : Hear, hear.) It seemed to him that the retrograde step would be to act upon the views of Mr F Ft Roberts who ought to show a more open mind on the vitally impor- tant point of the children's health. Churchpeople might have something to say in regard to Christ Church School if that step were taken in regard to the school in question. Alderman BOLTON said there were vacancies in Evelyn-street school and those parents who desired could send their children there. If Holy Trinity School was the only place available for a transfer, it would be much more desirable to make use of it than to leave things in their present unsatisfactory state. It was practically a case of Hobson's choice, and he thought thanks were duo to the committee for trying to make satisfactory arrangements in difficult circum- stances. _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER said the children's health should be the primary consideration. Father WILSON asked wh3ther in the event of the transferred teachers objecting they would be required to give religiou3 instruction ? 'Vie DIRECTOR: The managers said that the matter would be arranged to suit the convenience of the teachers. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Father Wir,sox : The wish of the teachers? The DIRECTOR : Yes. Councillor JENKINS : And the parents ? Mr liINGDON said if that were so—if there was an authoritative statement which could be embodied in the arrangements, it would dispose of the objection. He should support the proposal on health grounds. Mr C E PARKER asaed if the Director could tell them whether there was any objection on the part of the parents. If there was any objection on the part of the teachers or the parents it raised a very important issue. The committee recognised that it was pretty well a case of Hobson's choice. It was very undesir- able to keep the children in the condemned school if there was accommodation available near at hand. At the same time the points he had mentioned ought to be cleared up. The DIRECTOR replied that at present there were no objections from either, but lie did not know how fair it was to say that for the teachers, because they had not had any opportunity of objecting. With regard to the parents, the committee could not compulsorily transfer children from one school to another ; it rested with the parents to say which school the children should attend. Councillor PEMBERTON understood from Mr Murray that the teachers would not be called upon to give religious instruction. The CHAIRMAN: No. The value of Mr Murray': statement only amounts to this :--A manager has o some managers have assured him that that point would</p>
        <p>be taken into consideration in regard to the service. Councillor PEMBERTON : Well, make it a condition The CHAIRMAN: Exactly, I agree. Councillor PEMBERTON : Then if that condition 15 made everything is safeguarded ? The CHAIRMAN said the matter was not quite !SO simple as it appeared on the face of the minute, and in order to safeguard the interests concerned he suggested that if the proposal was for the present with- drawn and the conditions were carefully considered a satisfactory agreermnt mi At be arrivea at. Cooncillor PEMBERTON : There is no immediate hurry, is there ? '1 he CHAIRMAN: No. Councilior DICKENSON argued that the parents had not the freedom of choice that had been repre- sented. A child might be forced into a school because there was not accemmodation in the locality in which it lived, or where the parent selected it should attend. Toe DIRECTOR : Children are not forced into any particular school. Tne choice of school rests with the parents, E o long as there is accommodation. Couneillor DieKENsoN : But whea there is no accommodation, you are going to force the child into a school. The DIRECTOR: Yes, into some other school, but we do not choose the scnool. The CHAIRMAN: The choice of the parent is limited by the accommodation. Mr F R Ronsirrs said he was informed that the reason children attended the Cairo-street school from outside areas was because it had always been regarded as a feeder to the Arpley-street school. Father WILSON said it, seemed to him that if the Cairo-street school were closed the parents would have the same liberty of selection as any other parents. In the present state of affairs many chddren had to travel considerable distances to school, but that would not be so regrettable as to keep them in an insanitary building He was glad several speakers had emphasised a principle very dear to him, that the parents should be consulted as to the school which the children should attend. He hoped that would be always borne in mind. It seemed very desirable that it should be ascertained with certaintywhat would be required of the teachers. He could see serious objection to forcing teachers to leave a Council school and to go to a non-provided school where they would be asked to give religious teaching to which they might have reasonable objection. From that point of view alone, he supported the amendment. Mr UtesoN said the Cairo-street School must be closed It then became the duty of the committee to suggest an alternative. The School Management Committee refused to have the responsibility of carrying on the school any longer in face of the serious reports as to its insanitary condition. However, after hearing the views of several speakers, he felt there was a certain amount of difficulty and objection in carrying out the transfer without first ascertaining whether the teachers were prepared to give religious instruction which they woula no doubt be asked to give. He had the fullest confidence in the managers of the Trinity School that they would not require as a condition that those teachers should give definite denominational instruction He did not know the teachers, except the headmistress, and whether they might or might not be prepared to give such teaching. It would be an easy matter for the managers to arrange for the instruction to be given by teachers holding their particular views. In view of the discussion,he believed he would be fulfilling the wishes of the School Management Committee by withdrawing the minute in order that provisions might be made for safeguarding the interests of all concerned. The CirmILMAN thanked Mr Upson for having with- drawn the minute as he (Councillor Roberts) had found himself in considerable difficulty on the point. The teachers' Perms of service were with the Corporation and there was no restriction or obligation upon them, to give religious instruction of the kind referred to, so that re ally they would have been asked to enter a service, the conditions of which were entirely different from those under which they were onga,,ed. Whether the teachers objected or not it WWI the committee's duty at any rate to see that the conditions were not materially altered without their consent or approval. Treece was also the question of the children who were attending a school where n a sectarian religion was taught. It might be the wish of the parents that they attended such a school. All parents did not like to be put to the unpleasantness of claiming exemption from religious instruction under the conscience clause. It was an invidious position for any person to 0,, pvb if it could be avoided, and he quite understood that many parents would send their children to nueenomina- tional schools to escape the necessity of having to declare themselves in that way. The minute was accordingly withdrawn. TEACHERS' SALARIES INCREASED. The minutes of the School Nianaeement C ammittee recommended increases of salary 'to 191 elementary school teachers in accordance with scale, as from April Ist. Mr UesoN explained that the increases in the aggre- gate amounted to £990. That did not mean for a moment that they were-going to pay .000 more than last year. The increase was more apparent than real, because the total amount which the committee would be paying would be practically the same as twelve months ago. assuming that they had the same number of teachers in their employ. They had rescued that point in the aGlininistration of the scale where the increases which would take effect on April Ist were balanced by the fact that a considerable and growing number of teachers were reaching the maximum, a considerable number of those at the top were leaving to marry, or to take up new appointments, and their places were taken by young teacuers who began at the minimum. As a matter of fact in 1913 they would be paying .£BBO more in salaries than was paid in 1912, but that would be because they had eight additional certified teachers (including a headmaster) in tLeir service through the opening of the Evelyn-street School. They were not paying any more per head of the staff employed than in 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
