      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHILDREN AND ANIUSENIENTS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN AND ANIUSENIENTS.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the School Attendance Committee that the Watch Committee had made arrangements with the proprietors of all places of amusement, in the borough to exclude all children under 14 from seconl evening performances. SCEIOOLS OVERCROWDED. At the meeting of the School Attendance Committee, communications were submitted from the Board of Education, calling attention to the excess of the average attendance during the past school year over the recognised accommodation in the Christ Conrail, St.. Barnabas' (mixed department) and St. Mary's (boys' and girls' department) Schools. Tne replies which had been sent were approved. The Board of Education also wrote that the grant for the Silver-street Wesleyan School for the past school year would be paid in full, but that a deduction would to made from the grant conditionally due on the 30th September, 1913, if the average attendance then still exceeded the accom- modation unless substantial progress had oy that time been made with the provision of accommodatibm to relieve the school by the erection of the proposed. Oliver-street School.</p>
        <p>Mr PfatisTos: drew attention to the repeited threats. which the Board of Education had made to reduce grants because of overcrowding in several schools. He said that the threat with regard to St. Mary's had been carried out, and they had been penalized to the extent of $.:30. There were further complaints with reference to Silver-street and St. Barnabas' Sch iota, while at Hamilton-street School they suffered a reduction of £4O last year, and they were fearing a still greater reduction during the present year. He therefore urged upon the Sites and Buildings Committee to proceed with the utmost possible expedition with the erection of the contemplated new Council schools At the same time he appealed to members to refrain from silly and foolish blocking motions which impeded pro- gress and hampered the work of the Sites and Build- ings Committee. Resolutions were sometimes repeat- edly brought before the committee which at the finish resulted in nothing but a waste of time. Ha asked in future that no obstructive tactics should he employed, but that all would join in assisting forward the work of the Sites and BuAdings Committee. Councillor BIRTLKS seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON assured Mr Plinston that the Sites and Buildin2s Cimmittee were idling all they possibly could to push forward the erection of the new schools to relieve the overcrowding, but. they ,13. d architects, the land question, and "a few other obstreperous things of that sort to deal with." (Laughter). Councillor PEMBER.TON did not chink it fair to put the blame on the Sites and Buildings Committee and other people, because the Board of Education was largely rosponsibie for the slow progress. The Board continually imposed new restrictions, and kept altering the conditions, and when questions were addressed to the Board the officials at London took weeks to reply. With regard to the hint at alleged obstructive tactics, he should continue to oppose anything that was wrong and improper, and the only way to obviate that opposi- tion was to carry on the business in a proper way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>DENTAL CLINIC.</head>
        <p>THE DENTAL CLINIC.</p>
        <p>The committee agreed to the granting of a honorarium of twenty guineas to Mr Hadley 1-I Taylor for additional services rendered in connection with the dental clinic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>GIRL HEROINE DROWNED.</head>
        <p>GIRL HEROINE DROWNED.</p>
        <p>May Estate, aged 10, a girl boarded out by the Dundalk Union, was drowned on Thursday afternoon when attempting to rescue Patrick Mullin, aged 7, from a pond at Kilcurry. When Mullin fell into the water the girl jumped in to -rescue him. Both were drowned.</p>
      </div>
