      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>It is rarely that a team gets beaten by 27 points to 7, and yet gives such satisfaction to its supporters as did the Wirepullers last Saturday at Wigan. They played a rare good game, and it was only when Dickinson damaged his knee just before the interval that the home team could claim any substantial advantage. lip to this the Warrington forwards had dominated the play by their all round brilliance, and Wigan spent most of their time defending. But Dickinson's injury necessitated Skelhorne leaving the pack, which just gave the Wigan men the opportunities to push home their attack they had so far lacked.</p>
        <p>At half-time Wigan only held the mode3t lead of 11 points to 7, and had had by far the worst of the game' But the ultimate end was sure. The five Warrington forwards could not hope to hold the opposition to the end,and in the closing stages points came rapidly from the speedy Wigan backa. But it was not till three- quarter time that Warrington really cracked-up under the unequal pressure. They might easily have taken the lead early in the second half when Tranter was robbed over the line after a brilliant solo effort. Once they got going, the Wigan men played orilliantly, but I should like to have se.rul Warrington finish the game with all their men fit. It would be a novelty, if nothing else, at Central Park-,which has always been an unlucky ground for Warrington players.</p>
        <p>Once again chief praie must go to the six doughty forwards of Warrington. Their pace on the heavy ground was surprising, and the opinion was candidly expressed by the Wiganers that Warrington's was the finest pack seen there for some years. It is remarkable how they have trained on and much is due ti the fortunate circumstance that they have all been lucky to escape injury and thus maintain their excellent combination. The way they opened out and followed the ball was admirable and it must be a long time since the burly Sharrocks was so often captured in possession.</p>
        <p>What Warrington lacked was pace behind to finish off the fine work of the forwards. The halves did very well but the threequarter line was at the mercy of the Wigan fliers when it came to a question of getting points. On the defence the Warrington backs were " A 1" and until they had a numerical superiority the Wiganers found the barrier to the goal-line practically impassable, not-withstand- :mg their speed and cleverness. Renwick seemed quite out of place on the wing and did nothing at all, but on the opp ,site flank Williams looked well after Bradley, and the fair-haired successor to Ley- tham had the novel experience of failing to get a single point in the 27. He has been averaging three tries a week lately. But few teams have pushed Wigan harder than/Che Wirepullers this Reason.</p>
        <p>To-day, Warrington pay the return visit to Le&gt;eds, who got such an eye-opener as to the virility of the Warrington forwat ds on the last Saturday of the old year. The odds, this time, are cert-tinly on the Yorkshiretnen, who have accounted for the Wirepullers regularly at Headingley for some time. We do better there in cup-ties. But the watch is by no means a certainty for Leeds, and on current form the Warring- ton lads should have a fair chance of earning their 'first points in Yorkshire. They will be at full strength with the exception of Dickinson and Brooks, and I am hopeful that they may create a surprise.</p>
        <p>The selected team is : Jolley, full back ; Davies, Trainer, Renwick, and Williams, three-quarters ; Nicholas and Daintith, half backs; Thomas, tlhesters, -Skelhorne, Fearnley, Cox and R Thomas, forwarc's.</p>
        <p>The League table up-tq-date is as follows : For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'nt'gp. Hud'ileld `ll 19 2 0... 63114 468...23 25 121... 9,147 Wigan 22... 19 3 0... 77113 493...30 31 162... 8636 Broughton 19 12 3 4... 29 40 178...12 18 78... 7368 Hull K.R 22 15 6 1... 61 69 329...37 44 206... 7045 Salford q 3 14 6 0... 25 39 167...14 27 109... 7000 Dewsbury 22 14 7 1... 45 83 339...32 37 175... 65.90 Hunslet l4 7 1... 50 69 307...34 31 161... 6590 Wakefield T 23 13 8' 2... 36 63 261...36 58 216... 6086 Oldham 19 11 7 1... 39 52 231...33 55 165... 6052 Batley 90 11 9 0... 37 50 221...25 32 147... 5500 Swinton '. .00 10 9 1... 30 40 180...18 37 147... 52.50 Lem,. 02 11 10 1 41 42 208. 31 48 212... 5227 Whdale H. 20 9 9 2... 29 55 223...22 31 137... 50.00 1/..aarritigton 21 10 10 1... 27 30 144...27 39 171... 5000 r dford 22 10 11 1... 28 31 149...26 59 202... 47.72 Hull 22 9 It. 2... 25 36 158...33 61 219... 45'45 Widnes 91 9 11 1... 35 57 241...41 54 244... 45.23 Halifax 2l 811 2._ 23 43 175...38 52 232... 4285 St Helena 23 9 13 1... 35 53 229...37 50 221... 4130 Leigh ....... l9 7 12 0... 13 27 107...27 3i 165... 3681 York l9 5 14 0... 21 36 150...41 67 283... 26.31 Runcorn _2l 5 16 0... 24 29 135...43 71 299... 23.80 Barrow 00 4 15 15 32 126...40 60 260... 22.0 13ramley 2O 415 1... 21 27 123...45 91 363... 22.50 Keighley l7 313 1... 16 21 95...38 54 238... 2058 Coventry lB 017 1... 17 24 100...76131 545... 2.77</p>
        <p>The "A " team will —be at home in a Combination fixture with Huddersfield " A," who will make their first appearance at Wilderspo.l. They were lucky to beat the Wirepullers away, and we are looking-to a reversal of the result to-d,y. After last Saturday's shocking display, Mclntyre has been deposed, and through the injury of Brown, the full back position is placed in Jordan's care, while Rushton will make a re-appearance at threequarter.</p>
        <p>Warrington have this week re-signed Berry, the Young Warrington back who went to Evertor' after a short experience of the N.O game. He will turn out to-day at threequarter.)</p>
        <p>The "A" team is : Jordan ; Clare,Rußbton, Hudson and Berry ; A Brown and Douglas ; Naylor, Harmer, Critchley, Gibbs, Arnold and Blundell (a private in the King's Liverpool Regiment, who has been training for a trial at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Neg9tiations were completed yesterday for the transfer of James Tilley to Runcorn, whose regular full back is on the injured list.</p>
