      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Warrington at Wigan. Wirepullers Play Well but Lose.</head>
        <p>Warrington at Wigan. Wirepullers Play Well but Lose.</p>
        <p>The wirepullers took their strongest thirteen to Wigan on Saeurday, and for the greater part of the game they put up a fine fight. In the end, injuries to Dickenson weakened the Warrington defence, and Wigan won by a substantial margin. The Collier also had a strong team in the field and as the weathers was fine the popular fixture drew an attendance of 12,000. The team and officials were : vvarsiugton—Jolley, back; Renwick, Tranter, Dicken- son and Williams, tureequarter backs ; Nicholas and Daintetb, half-backs ; G I homes, Cheaters, SkelliJrne, Fearnley, Cox and Thomas, forwards. Wigan—Sharrock, back ; Bradley, Jenkins, Walford, and Todd, tereequarter-backs ; Weave and Thomas, half-backs ; Seeling, liamsdale, Francis, CeAdrick Silcock and Williams, forwards. Referee, Mr W McCutcheon Oldham ; touch-judges, Messrs H Lowe (Brougb ton) and T Kiley (Leigh), Warrington opened the game with their customary energy, and the forwards were quickly in evidence with a concerted dribble which beat Walford and Todd, aad resulted in the capture of Sharrock near the line. Wigan tried hard to get the ball away, but the Warring- ton front rankers were remarkably spry on the heavy ground, and the borne line was hard pressed. Jenkins eventually burst through to centre, only for Jolley to return with effect, and a passing run by the Warrington backs looked promising till Dickenson dropped the ball when well placed. Wigan responded with clever work, but Jenkins was upset by Tranter and Thomas , the latter injuring his wrist in the tackle. The \Varrington third line was slow, ayd again Jenkins and Bradley came away speedily, only for Jolley to " plant" the winger in tine style. Again the Warrington forwards raced away, Fearnley, Cox and Chesters forcing Sharrock to get rid of the ball promptly.Skelhorne had a chance,but co ald not reach the ball. Back came the Wiganers with filie passing, Bradley making another dash which was ended in the firm grip of Williams. The homesters .were now within short range of the Warrington line, and long passes between Seeling, Jenkins, and Thomas enabled the latter to find a way through the defence, and the Warrington line was crassad. The scorer added the goal. Warrington again attacked, the speedy forwards grassing Sharrock in fine style, while a tine &amp;lon, of Jolley'a maintained the attack. G Thomas marked from a weak kick by Gleave and opened Warrington's score with a neat goal. Jenkins eventually burst through again for Walford to score, and soon alter Francis threw across to Seeling, who scored a third try by sheer dash. Warrington were still playing aggre, sively and forced play to the other end. Jenkins was smartly robbed by Tranter, the tall Warrington centre scoring a capital try, Renwick adding a goal. Half- time : Wigan, 1 goal 3 tries, (11 points) ; Warrington, 2 goals 1 try (7 points). On resuming, Warrington were again on the attack for some time, though an injury to Dickenson resulted in Skelhorne haying to play as extra threequarter. He was instrumental in stopping Jenkins when the latter looked like goina through. Teen Trainer created a sensation by breaking clear in his own half and punt- ing over Sharrock's head. An exciting race followed, and Tranter was in the act of grounding whoa he was pushed off the ball by Jenkins, who kicked dead. This was hard luck for the Wirepullers who might have taken the lead. Wigan now forced mattes and Coldrick was the means of Wigan attacking. Bradley was held up by Williams when he appeared almost certain to go through. War- rington relieved and Whams made a good run along the right, but he passed to an opponent and the lost grounu was speedily rtcovered. Wigan continuiug to force matters, Gleave handed on to Sealing, who made no mistake at the eorner. There was a quarter of an hour yet left for play and the visiting forwards con- tianed to show very earnest fight, but they were let down behind. Jenkins made a dashing run, and, bluffing Williams, the Welshman had only Jolley to beat, and this being accomplished the \Warrington defence was pierced, Thomas adding the goal. Warrington appeared to fall away after this, and after Jenkins h.d recorded his second try the day's sport concluded when Seeling in the last minute succeeded in forcing his way over, Trioru,s adding a goal. Result :—W;gan, three goals seven tries (27 peiuts) ; Warrington, two goals one try (seven points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>THE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " BADLY BEATEN.—Warrington "A " were decisively beaten by Wigan "A " at Wllderspool on Saturday, after a game in which con- siderable feeling was introduced. There was a large gate. Tbe visitors brought with them Curran, their latest Colonial capture, who soon took a prominent part in the play and registered three tries. Harmer opened the game, and for a brief spell play settled in the Wigan half. The visitors were the first to take up a dangerous position. and Curran dashPd over for Hilton to kick a goal. The borne forwards rushed to the Wigan end, where Seddon proved safe, but Critchley opened Warrington's score a moment later with a goal from a mark. Wigan came again, and Curran broke claan through and scored under the posts, Hilton again adding. the extra points. Warrington experienced a piece of hard luck, Clare stepping into touch near the line. Hilton frequently opened out the game and the visiting backs showed some smart COM- bination. A gliring car of obstruction by Mclntyre resulted in the award of a try to Wigan, Hilton adding his third goal. At the interval Wigan led by fifteen points to two. 111-feeling was engendered in the second half, and the football was of a nigh order. The only score came from a good individual effortby Cnrran, who registered his third try, the goal kick ;vat.t. failing.‘rnßgteosnn, ;lt Wigan, 3 goals ; 4 tries (l 8 points) goal, 2 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday February 1, 1913,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP. FIRST ROUND-REPLAY.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP. FIRST ROUND-REPLAY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFoRD v. HARRoNVEY. These teams again tried conclusions at New Brighton on Saturday, when Wftrowby had a strong team in the field. The game was only in progress a few minutes before Myers opened the account for Harrowby. Latchford retaliated, and Probyn effected a fine save from a close-quarter shot. A few minutes afterwards a penalty was given against Latchford and was taken by Davies, who scored the second goal for Harrowby. A little latgr the Latchford centre forward had hard lines, putting the ball just over the bar. Davies put in a good shot after a solo effort, which the Litchford goal-keeper just managed to clear. From a melee in the home goal, Smith put in a shot, and Prohyn saved what seemed like a certain goal. Half-time: Harrowby 2 goals, Latchford nil. In the second half the leaders had again the advantage and registering two more goals, won by four goals to nil.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Ag'st Pts Lostock Gralam Lyrnm l2 7. 1 4.,. 28... 21... 18 Mobberley 13... 6... 5... 2... 21... 21... 14 Barnton Villa Ougtitrington Park 12... 5... 5... 2... 17... 18... 12 Bowdon Vale Latchford Broadheath Ougbtrington Amat 11... 5... 6... 2... 21... 30... 8 St Vincent's 14... 3... 2... 17... 30... 8 Altrincham Lads 10... 0... 6... 4... 11... 23... 4 BOWDON VALE V LYMM.</p>
        <p>At vicarage-lane, Bowdon, on Saturday, before a fair attendance. Lymm soon forged ahead and after a good run Barber opened their account. Midfield play followed for a time : both sets of backs were kieking well. After the game had been in progress about 30 minutes J Clare, with a nice pass, gave Wilson (the Lymm centre) a grand p,ss, for the hitter to dash through the home backs. He worked over to the left and when practically on the goal line passed across the goal to , Haddon (out- side right) for the latter to score a capital goal. The Vale dashed to the other end and Hadden did well to clear from MKinual and Bailey. Hinde relieved the pressure with a big kick. Lymm were showing the better form forward, and before the interval Eladden further increased their lead. Half- time: Lymm, 3; Bowdon Vale, o.—ln the second half the Vale had more of the game. After nice work by</p>
        <p>_F WORK, EFTS, ORE RECEIVERS FINED.</p>
        <p>An echo of the recent works thefts cases was herd the Borough Police Court on Saturday before Mr J ‘'‘ Hayward (in the chair), Messrs it T Fairclough, T Pemberton and A J JolleY.</p>
        <p>- Arthur %\ iiliam Hazeldine, 37, Selby-street, and Ernest Hamblett, Leigh Villas, Grappenhall, were charged with having during th,e past six months received shaving soap and scent, the property of Messrs Crosfield Ltd, from Thomas Lawton, well knowing the goods to have been stolen. Hazeldine with receiving two sticks of shaving soap, value 24, and Ilamblett with receiving six sticks of shaving soap and three bottles of scuit, value 13s 6d.</p>
        <p>Mr Henry Greenall, who appeared for "Messrs Crosfield, sAid that on the 18Lh inst the man Lawton was convicted at that C.)urt on a charge of stealing soap and perfumes. In conse uence of a statement he mane then, Inspector Williamson saw tha prisonPr Hazeldine at his house and told him that Lawton Lad stated that he had given him some shaving sticks. Prisoner said he had paid sixpence for the stiAs. The man must have known perfectly well that that was not their value. He also made a statement to the effect that he knew Lawton had stolen them and that he had been stealing for some months. The Inspector then saw the other prisoner woo admitted that he had had the shaving soap and scent. For three sticks of soad he paid threepence and he gave a drink for the bottles of scent. For the remaining sticks of soap he paid threepence each. Although the actual amount involved in the present prosecution was somewhat small, pilfer- ing at the works was assaruing serious proportions. The Company therefore felt it was their duty to deal seriously with cases they discovered. There were a large number of employes engaged in the works and it was as much for their protection as in the interests of the firm, proceedings were being taken. Certain privi- leges were given to employes and if men abused those privileges it was probable that innocent people might be suspected. It was quite probable that some of the privileges might be taken away on account of the way they had been abused. The men could buy the articles in a proper manner.—ln fining each defendant as with thf,alternative of a mouths' imprisonment the Chairman said they had only just missed being sent to prison. They ought to have been sent to prison, but the Bench hoped they would take the case as a warning.</p>
