      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LIQUOR CONTROL. MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., AND</head>
        <p>LIQUOR CONTROL. MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., AND</p>
        <p>CLUBS.</p>
        <p>[TO THE. EDITOR. " Ex I)IINEE."j</p>
        <p>Sir,—Enclosed please find copy of letter sent to H Smith, Esq, M P, in answer to correspondence pub- lished in the " Examiner" on the I,Sth inst.—Yours truly BENJAMIN T. PARKES (Hon. Sec. Citizens' Temperance League). 8, Willis-street, January 23rd, 191:3. Dear Sir,—ln reply to your letter of the 14th iust, we are surprised at your amazement, and think it is based upon a false idea. You say :—" I am amazed that so many who are devoted to the cause of Temper- ance should remain satisfied with demands for further restricting the sale of liquor in premises licensed and controlled by the magistrates and at the same time ignore the fact that the trade is increasing enormously in clubs under conditions which ve:y often constitute a grave scandal." We are pleased you have had the courage to pen these words, and we ask all who are members of clubs and read this correspoadence to note them, and those who are responsiLle for the carrying on of this " Uncoutr3lled source of supp:y " to ponder well over them. Yet, we do not agree with you that s) many temperance workers do ignore it, and would respect- frilly remind you that in the Temperance Bill of 190 S (which was supported by all Temperance Organisations of our land, and especially by theabove League at a memorable meeting held in -Parr Hall on April 11th of that year) contained clauses for the annual registration and control (including inspection) of clubs. You know, sir, as well as we do, how that Bill was slaughtered at a private meeting held at Lansdowne House.</p>
        <p>The Bill, a copy of which you forwarded, is not so satisfactory to us as Pal t 4 of the 190 S Bill was, with this exception, that, in your Bill clause 7 defininitely states the hours for the closing of the bars in clubs. It certainly could be strengthened by including clauses dealing with the following points : 1 Objections by persons other than the police to the registration or renewal of registration. as covered by Clause 37 Of the 190 S Bill. 2 The cancelling of registration on conviction of offence as covered by clause 39 of the 19S Bill. 3 The refusal of registration, if iu respect of the premises for which application is made, during the preceding five years a licence has been f,rfeited or a renewal of licence reluzed as covered by clause 40 of the 190 S Bill.</p>
        <p>4 The authority for inspection to be very explicit as covered by clause 41 of the 1908 Bill. if you and your friends will accept the suggestions nhmed we promise you what assistauce we can give by arousing public interest in the measure, and as no Bill will, we hope, in the future, having passed the House of Commons, be killed outright, you may be successful in having placed upon the btatute Book ofthe realm an Act, for which you will deserve the honour which comes to all, irrespective of party, who have honestly tried to do something for the welfare of their fellow citizens.</p>
        <p>I hate followed your example and ,sent a copy of this letter to our local Press. Yours'truly. BENJAMIN T. PARKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>H. Smith, Esq„ M.P. LETTER FROM REV. H. OGDEN.</head>
        <p>H. Smith, Esq„ M.P. LETTER FROM REV. H. OGDEN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As a vice-president of the Warringt Citizens' Temperance League, I was much interested in the answer which the Member for this borough gave to the resolutions that were forwarded to him by the above League. I was more interested in what the Member omitted than in what be said. May I add ,sir, that my inability to be present at any of the League's meetings has made it impossible for me to reply through that agency. But sir, I am interested in what the Member said but I am equally interested in what he failed to say. There are no two opinions about clubs in the circle of temperance workers. There is this difference between ourselves and the Member, we should aenihilate the drinking practices of clubs, he would—well, what ? One would venture to suggest that he should commence a temperance campaign in the clubs of this town of which he is either the president or the idol. The constituency which he represents has prubahly fewer drinking clubs in it than any town its size in Lancashire. Those that are in it are the illegitimate children of the eolitical parties and probably one half of them are under the patronage of the member for this borough. As a temperance worker I would suggest that the member carry his missionary outfit witn him to the clubs of which be is the patron. if any opening presents itself at the Carlton a word in season would be welcome. Sir, the member for this borough has written about an agreed subject. We are glad that on the question of temperance be it not an entire heathen Be is better than the company he dwells in, but why labour the question of the clubs when all temperance bodies are clamouring for public or magisterial control? There is one phase of club life, however, that does not pertain in public-house life, the club officials can pur- chase beer from whom they will. I suppose nine tenths of Warrington publicans sigh for that charter. But now, sir, that we are agreed about clubs, what has the member f.r this borough to say t,'.&gt; us about breweries and public-houses ? The member says he is interested in the temperance question; let him give us evidence of it. It has been whispered to me, but then it cannot be true, that the member will preside at the annual Licensed Victuallers' dinner at Birmingham shortly. Rumour has it —but Rumour is ever a lying jade—that the Member was a very valiant, opponent of the last Licensing Bill. But then, all may change their opinions. one the less, 05 per cent. of temper- ance workers were anxious that the Bill should become law. Does the Member for this Borough believe in prohibition, and if he does would he send his tract to Wilderspool Brewery or Walton Hall? because I might incidentally mention to the Member, lest he be not aware, that Wilderspool influences this town very badly on Saturday nights. Does the Member support Local Option or Local Veto? Would he give the right to any locality to say when, where, at what hour and to whom drink should be sold in that locality? Would he give to any locality the right that is theirs, namely of renewing licences when that locality should desire ? Would he refuse the selling of drink to women before noon ? Would he close the public house on Sunday ? Would he support a law that refuses drink to a working man at El in the morning? These and questions of this type the Member for the Borough must answer ere temperance workers can credit that his interest in Temperance is serious. Evidently there is no immediate prospect of a Licensing Bill, but if the Member is agreed with the Temperance worker on the questions I have submitted to him, he will be a valuable asset whenever a Temperance Bill is introduced into the House of Commons.—Thanking you in anticipation, I remain, yours sincerely,</p>
        <p>H. OGDEN.</p>
        <p>134, St. Mary Street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHF ORD. The First Eleven defeated South Manchester by 3.0 at Withington last week, a distinctly good performance. The South backs were rather shaky to start with and Pearson and Minerals scored two smart goals. On changing ends South Manchester severely bombarded the Latchford goal but Bruhl excelled himself, two of his saves from hot shots by Pitt and Roberts being remarkably fine. Near the end Latchford again attacked and after Pike in the home goal had emulated Bruhl's example several times Itlinards put on a third goal with a fast shot close in. Pike, Howard. Pitt, and Roberts were the best of the home side. For Latchford, Fletcher and Knight were in grand form and were the most polished backs on the field. Isherwood and Dale centred well from the wings and along with the halves worked some nice positions for the inside forwards. South Manchester " A " turned up at Latchford short but the sides were equalized, each team playing 10. At one time the home team were leadinz, 4-1 but, the score was 5-4 in their favour at the finish. Eccles, Dixon and Gibts in tbe backs and H Fletcher and Hobin in the forwards played well for Latchford. The scorers were Hobin (2), H Fletcher (2), Banks (1). The First Eleven at St Helens this afternoon is :—J Bruhl, goal ; T Dixon, C 13illington, backs ; J B Wilkin- son, A E Bradshaw, A Knight, halves ; R A Dale, J E Pearson, W H Minards' A Starkey, F W Isherwood forwards. Bus from Academy 1.15 p.m. The Second Eleven v St Helens" A" at home is : Three fullbacks, H Wright. W T Eccles, W Mull ; C H Morgan, H T Gibbs, H Banks, halves; 0 Laver, F Robin, E Banks, H Fletcher, H H Cotten, forwards. Bully off at 2.45 p.m.</p>
      </div>
