      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>TUR,DAY—llefore Couucillor H Roberts (in the chair),</p>
        <p>Aldermen Bennett and Smethurst, Col. Ede!sten, Messrs (.; Jackson Holmes, T Locker, W Rictiardson</p>
        <p>and J Hepherd. DID HE INTEND TO STEAL ?—Daniel Taylor, of no fixed address, was charged with stealing on January 11th, a gent's vest, value Is Gd from outside the shop of Messrs Park and Bowden, 59. Buttermarker-street and further with stealing on the 10th January a barrel of bone grease value from tbe works of the Warrington Products Co.—The Chief Constable stated that prisoner pledged the vest for 6d and gave a false name and address. He stated to Detective Sergeant Holland that he had bought two vests from a man in a public- house and also that a woman had given it to him. The barrel was stolen during the night, taken some distance from the works and abandoned in the street. About two hours later prisoner tried to remove the barrel but it was too heavy for him, being about a quarter of a ton in weight.—Prisoner said he had no intention of stealing the barrel.—He was sent to prison for three months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr J W Hayward (in the chair), Messrs C Jackson Holmes and W T Williams.</p>
        <p>RUNNING AWAY.—Peter McGuitmess, labourer, of no fixed address, was sent to prison forone month for having run away from the town, leaving his wife and three children chargeable to the Union,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>DOMESTIC TROUBLES IN LEOI-1 STREET.</head>
        <p>DOMESTIC TROUBLES IN LEOI-1 STREET.</p>
        <p>HOW A QUARREL WAS HAPPILY</p>
        <p>AVERTED,</p>
        <p>" What! Another suit, John. You're downright extravagant. That's the second suit of clothes in two months."</p>
        <p>"Quite right, girlie dear, but you see this is an extra suit."</p>
        <p>"And a pretty penny it's cost judging by the look of it. How will our Easter holidays fare if you con- tinue to spend twice the amount on your clothes that other men do? "</p>
        <p>"It's all right, dear--you will be better off when the holidays come round than other wives are."</p>
        <p>" Well ! I can't see it. That suit must have cost more than Dick's—it fits perfectly." "I see, I can't keep it a secret any longer: the truth is I got this suit for 12/11 from that firm in Florsemarket-street, Warrington, 'Suits Limited.'" "To measure-12/11—impossible." "It's right, and what's more they've got the biggest range of patterns to choose from in the shop I've ever seen."</p>
        <p>"But 1211! I can't believe it. The 'fit' is perfect." " Oh ! well, perhaps, I should have explained that this is an exceptional sale of theirs; although the value they give is always phenomenal. Everything is being sold below cost to clear the stock befcue their • new spring goods arrive. That's why I got this suit at 12/11. Now! Art satisfied? "</p>
        <p>"I wish Suits Ltd. sold costumes, that's all." 'They do, my dear, but, don't you agree I've got a real genuine bargain?" " Yes ! And you had better mention it to Dick in case he hasn't read of it in the ' Examiner.' "</p>
        <p>Suits Ltd.'s sale of suits, &amp;c., is the greatest sale ever held in Warrington. All goods have been reduced below cost in order to clear them before 1913 materials arrive. HAVE YOU ORDERED YOUR EXTRA. SUIT YET? If not, call for patterns and judge for yourself. The address is : Jl3 BILEE BUILDINGS, HORSEMARKET ST., WARRINGTON, "SUITS LIMITED."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Lancashire Catholic Players' Society.</head>
        <p>Lancashire Catholic Players' Society.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening the members of the Lanca- shire Catholic Players' Society gave the first of three performances of "King Arthur" (Comps Carr) in the Holy Name Hall, Manchester. The title role was admirably portrayed by Mr W Kilduff, of Warrington (late manager of the St. Mary's Drama,tic Society) who acted with great power aud was rewarded with round after round of applause. Mrs W Hickey (136 e Miss Nora Fleming), who is also well-known in Warrington amateur dramatic circles, achieved a triumph as Queen Guinevere. It is interesting to note that in the original production of " King Arthur," the two roles above mentioned were played by Sir Henry Irving and Miss Ellen Terry respectively.</p>
      </div>
