      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SIX MONTHS FOR A COWARDLY KICK. . _ .</head>
        <p>SIX MONTHS FOR A COWARDLY KICK. . _ .</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday Peter CT)sirove (24), a collier, was charged with felonious wounding, at Earlestown, on November 25. Mr Singelton pro- secuted.</p>
        <p>Evidence was given that prisoner and a labourer named Birtwistle were playing " tip it," in the Rail- way Vaults, where a quarrel ensued between them. As they left the house prisoner tripped Birtwistle and he fell. Later on Birtwistle said prisoner would hear more of the affair, whereupon Cosgrove dealt him a violent blow in the eye. While he was unconscious on the pound prisoner kicked him, freaking both bones of one of his legs. Prisoner said ho had acted only in self-defence. The jury returned a verdict, of "Guilty "and prisoner was sentenced to six months' hard labour; his Lord- ship observing that nothing could justify the cowardly act of kicking a man when he was on the ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>EVILS OF PhOTECTION.</head>
        <p>EVILS OF PhOTECTION.</p>
        <p>MR. J. A. SEDDON'S INDICTMENT OF AMERICA Mr J A Seddon, Labour candidate for Newton Division, who spoke on Monday night at the nnruial social gathering of the Haydock Women's Labour League, said he had returned from representing the trades unions of this country in America. He had asked his friends to take him among the workers, and there he saw pictures of poverty that were enough to make one's blood run cold. The leaders ‘.l' the trades union move- ment had to admit that under Prot e,tion it was possible for the rich to grow richer, hut the pier became poorer. hie went into the mining districts, and he found that where trades unionism was weak there the men were living practically in a state of slavery. He said, refci ring to Saturday's meeting at St Helens, Lord New.on ought to be ashamed of himself. Then he noticed that Lord Wolmer had said that he would fight the next election. If that was so, be (Mr Seddon), would challenge him to resign, and in a by-election test the feelings of the Newton Division.</p>
        <p>NUTS 8/ SLACK, WASHED</p>
        <p>NUTS, BEANS 81 PEAS,</p>
        <p>CROMPTON &amp; SHAWCROSS LTD.,</p>
        <p>Colliery Proprietors, DAUM LANE, WARRINGTON, MI inquiries to F. W. PRAEGER, District Agent.</p>
      </div>
