        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 1, 1913.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY FEBRUARY l, 1913.</p>
        <p>The Warrington COUNCIL OR Education Committee CHURCH at its meeting on SCHOOL? Monday evening had to consider a proposal which, innocent enough on the surface, raised important questions of principle. The Cairo- street (Infants) Council school has repeatedly been condemned ,y the Medical Officer of Health as hopelessly insanitary and the Committee decided that, in the interests of the children's health, it was imperative to or find new premises. Owing to the erection of the Evelyn-street Coun- cil School the attendance at Holy Trinity (Church) School has been very seriously depleted, and the recommendation which the School Management Committee brought forward on Monday evening was to close the Cairo-street School and transfer the staff—and presumably the scholars—to the Holy Trinity School where a new infants' division would be formed. Mr. F. R. Roberts very properly took exception to the arrange- ment on the ground that the views of the parents and teachers concerned had neither been invited nor considered. Many of the parents would no doubt exercise their right of choicein favour of Cairo-st. School because it was an unsectarian school and would resent the idea of their children being trans- ferred to a school in which they would receive Church teaching—unless they chose to make thernselves conspicuous by pleading the conscience clause. The teachers, as servants direct of the Education Authority, have not been called upon to give definite religious instruction, and it is not inconceiv- able that they might find it distasteful to begin at this stage, to instil {Church doctrines into the minds of the scholars. There appears to be a tacit understanding with the managers that the wishes of the teachers shall be consulted on this important point, but in the absence of an authoritative statement to that effect the committee would not have been justified in approving the 'proposed transfer. After several members had pressed for safeguards on behalf of teachers and parents, the minute was withdrawn and we have little doubt that the committee will insist that, at all events, the teachers' conditions of service shall not be materially varied. The only satisfaction vouchsafed to the parents, up to the present, is that there is no obligation upon them to send their children to any particular school, but that assurance is worth very little when schools in certain localities are so over- crowded that the so-called selection of a school narrows itself to " Hobson 's choice."</p>
        <p>The letter from Dr. A ,ElOO ARGUMENT. Markel to the Score-</p>
        <p>tary of the Warrington Infirmary, enclosing a donation of £lOO, is a fine reply to the plea of those wealthy firms which have reduced their contributions to the institution because of the cost of National Insurance. Dr. Markel takes the view that the great scheme of state Insurance "far from drying up the. founts of charity, should hearten all those whose desire it is to make systematic war with disease and distress," and his cheque will, we hope, have a solutary effect on those townsmen who are so ready to ease their backs of charity's burden at the expense of the Infirmary and for the specious reason so callously advanced.,</p>
        <p>A poster on the local DERRY'S DECIDER. hoardings appropriately says that " London- derry election spikes Carson's guns." The fact that they were dummy guns, and com- paratively harmless before, does not alter the significance of the Nationalist triumph in Derry. Before the election Ulster returned 17 Unionists and 16 Nationalists,a preponder- ance of political opinion which sufficed for Carson and Co. to claim that Ulster would not have Home Rule. Now that the position is exactly reversed it follows ipso facto that Ulster is a Home Rule province. The Government's mandate is thus unassailable, and no-one need hesitate to laugh unrestrain- edly at the comedy in the House of Lords. Ireland, and England too, are united in their demand for Home Rule. Even Sir Edward Carson must bow down before the fiat o f Londonderry. Ulster has spoken!</p>
      </div>
