      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Barber—Toft.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Barber—Toft.</p>
        <p>A wedding in which many local people were keenly interested took place early on Monday morning at St Paul's Church. The contracting parties were Mr T Fairclough Barber, of Greenfield House, Latchford, and Miss Arline Bertha Toft, eldest daughter of Mrs H Toft, Horsematket-street. Mr Barber is an old captain of the Lancashite and Warrington Football Clubs, whose fame as a three-quarter back, was of more than local character. 'An years or so have elapsed since he regularly appeared for War- rington, but he has since then been almost continu- ously identified with the club as a member of the management committee. and a large circle of friends will wish the popular "Fair" Barber a happy and prosperous married life. The wedding was quietly solemnised iii the presence of only immediate relatives, by the Rev E E Peters. The bride wore mole coloured velour cloth trimmed with amethyst and mole velvet, and was attended by her youngest sister Miss Nora Toft, as bridesmaid ; she was gowned in turquoise blue, with black velvet beefeater hat and pale pink ostrich feather mount. Mr Richard Denham, Padgate, uncle of the bride, gave her away, and the best man was Mr William Taylor. Following the ceremony there was a recep- tion at the residence of Mrs Toft, after which Mr and Mrs Barber departed for London for their honeymoon</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Mann—Wrigley.</head>
        <p>Mann—Wrigley.</p>
        <p>The wedding took place on Thursday at the Irlam Parish Church, of Mr W P Mann and Miss Mary Jane Wrigley, the ceremony being performed by the Rev R Martin, D.D. (vicar). The bride, who was given away by her father, wore a navy blue costume and tegal hat with grey lancer feathers. The bridesmaids were the Misses Amelia and Edith Wrigley, sisters of the bride, and both wore navy blue costumes and large picture hats trimmed with forget-me-nots and moss roses. Mr W A Mann, brother of the bridegroom and Mr J A Wrigley acted as groomsmen. A recep- tion was afterwards held at Woodville, Cadishead, the home of the bride's parents.</p>
        <p>Plans for the new Memorial Hall at the Liberal Club, which is to be the generous gift of Mrs. John Crosfield, have been approved. * *</p>
        <p>Who has been knob-sticking? The Society of Painters and Decorators has lodged a complaint with reference to the painting of standards by motor-men and conductors. By the by, the man who paints his own gate-post will be looked upon as a job-robber! *</p>
        <p>The Council is to be asked to consider "the desirability of extending the boundaries of the County Borough." Is it true that the proposition comes from Padgate, where there is considerable nervousness anent the cost of the new sewage scheme?</p>
        <p>The Fire Engine Committee has resolved to advertise for a 450 gallon motor fire engine. Before spending a "cool thousand" in this direction we hope the Council will fully discuss the project. For our own part we should prefer to see the money spent on Parr Hall organ. At present it sounds more like a fire-engine than a musical instrument. * *</p>
        <p>The motor-buses are coming, and tenders have been received, but before deciding, Alderman Smethurst, Alderman John Evans, Councillor Wilkinson, and Councillor Peacock are going on a tour of trial and inspection. Joy-rides of a free and easy character will no doubt be included in the programme.</p>
        <p>There has already been a great demand for tickets for the great meeting which the Right Hon. Augustine Birrell, K.C., (Chief Secretary for Ireland) will address in the Parr Hall on Saturday, February 15th. Ward workers are therefore advised to make early application at the Liberal Club, or they will run the risk of disappointment. Women will not be admitted. to the meeting.</p>
        <p>There has been an apparent slackening of activities on the Warrington Bridge works and many townspeople have been inquiring the why and the wherefore. We are authoritatively informed that the concreting of thP ribs of the first portion of the bridge was completed on January 23rd. The ribs now have to stand six or seven weeks before the " centreing " can be removed and, in the meantime, no further work can be done on the west side. Work has, however, been commenced this week on the training. walls for the east side.</p>
        <p>A country correspondent sends us a hedge cutting which is itself ocular demonstration of the mildness of this exceedingly moist winter. The twig has hawthorn leaves fully half size, and old inhabitants of Moore say they never remember the hedgerows being so green in January. Elsewhere in Cheshire the white rock, primrose, and polyanthus are in bloom. These precocious plants may escape being nipped in the bud, but they are pretty sure to meet with a blighting frost or two before spring time comes round in reality.</p>
        <p>* * * At the annual gathering of the National Service League on Thursday evening Captain Crosfield had a good deal to say about our military unpreparedness, and finally prophesied that national service "was coming." We might add: So are the Campbells ! Mrs. B. S. Townroe said, with confidence, that "they had made great gaps in the walls of their opponents. The whole fabric was now tottering under their attack." This cheerful complacency reminds us of Mr. Bonar Law's statement at Edinburgh that the Tory party was never so united as it is to-day. Indications may entirely to the contrary, but indications can be conveniently ignored. We hope to publish an extended report of the proceedings in our next week's issue.</p>
        <p>Like threatened me-n, the Parr Hall organ lives long. The Sub-Committee had got so far as to recommend the provision of a new instrument at a cost of from £1,300 to £1,500, but the recommendation has been quashed by the General Purposes Committee on the ground of economy. We can foresee the charge being made that this Committee of the whole Council is deficient in the artistic sense, and the charge will probably not per- turb them. What strikes us, however, is that a Council which joyfully provides bowl- ing greens for working men, is wanting in clarity of vision when it persistently jibs at the provision of reasonable aids to the recreation of the more educated section of the community. Music is surely as commend- able as bowls!</p>
        <p>The " World " this week, paying a hand- some tribute to the work of rehousing the poor accomplished in Liverpool, sets forth the following table, whose figures may be described by that gorgeous Oriental phrase— " Apples of Gold in Pictures of Silver." EFFECTS OF REHOUSING IN LIVERPOOL. To DEcE3II3EIt 31, 1912.</p>
        <p>Total number of sanitary dwellings erected 2,663 Erected prior to 1897 Erected since 1901 (These are for labouring classes, and are reserved for the dishous-ed).</p>
        <p>SOME TIEST:LTS. Death Rate: Declined from 50 to 27 per 1,000. Tuberculosis: Declined from '1 to 119 per 1,000. Typhus Fever: Once never absent; in 1910. not a case. Typhoid: In 1896, 1,300 cases ; in 1911 200 cases. Police Prosecutions: Have fallen 50 ner cant. Cash Saving to City: At least £65,000 per annum or double the cost of the rehousing work * * *</p>
        <p>„ New blood in the Town Council obviously implies reform and we note with interest a proposition from Councillor Osborne, the new member for St. Austin's Ward, for the meet- ing on Tuesday. Councillor Osborne's pro- posal is for the creation of a new municipal office: that of permanent shorthand writer to the Corporation, whose duties shall be to take a verbatim report of the pro- ceedings of all committees. Considerable objection is, naturally, raised against the proposal in many quarters, but it has a lot to recommend it. So little is really known of what transpires at committee meetings, disputes as to questions of fact are so frequent in the Council, and there is so little frank intercourse between the committees, that a reliable report of the business done and the comments made would be of value to every member of the Council. There is no suggestion that the reports should be made public and in other respects the advantages seem to outweigh the objections. An inter- esting discussion on the question is assured at the Council meeting.</p>
        <p>Territorial Efficiency Medals. The quarterly list of nou-comtuissioned officers and men of the Territorial Force who have been awarded "the Territorial Force Efficiency Medal," isciled on Tuesday, includes the following :-4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment—Private H W Eckersley, Cor- poral G Knight, Bugler W Miller, Colour-Sergeant R Oxley, Colour-Sergeant F Pitnlott. \Vest Lanca- shire Divisional Transport and Supply Column, A S C —Staff Sergeant-Major J W Bullet], Corporal E G Evans.</p>
        <p>Pelona'.</p>
        <p>On the ree-mmendation of the Warrington Medical Association, Dr Bowden has been elected s member of the local Insurance Committee.</p>
        <p>Museum Committee Lectures.</p>
        <p>The Warringtrn Museum Committee beg to anncunce that a series of three lectures on "Florence and its art" will he delivered under the euspiceA of the Ogden Trustees by Mr .J E Phythian, commencing on Saturday next,in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street. The Children's Ball. At a meeting of the ladies' committee on Wednesday it was teported that the net profit of tho children's hall recently held in the Parr Hall, ',mounted to 13,3 2d. This sum has been handed over to the Children of Warrington Cot Fund. Junior Liberal Association.</p>
        <p>"Economies in Politics" is the title of the monthly lecture which will be given by Mr W Abbott, M.A , Altrincham, under the auspices of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association in the Horsewarket-street Chambers on Monday evening _. _ •</p>
        <p>Royal Theatre of Varieties. " For money—not love" is the title of the powerful drama which the Royal Stock Co. will present at the Royal Theatre of Varieties next week. Sara Rosebery, The lively little Lancashire lass, will also appear in song and story. There will be the usual matinee on Thursday at 2.30. ANTHRACITE STOVES, clean, cheerful, smokeleLs, DO fnmes.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND. ,51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Picturedrome. The star picture for Monday, Tuesday and Wednes- day is "The Black Mask." it contains without doubt the most remarkable serie3 of sensational incidents yet displayed in one picture. On Thursday, Friday and Saturday "The Surgeon" will be shown. It is a story of a New York doctor degraded by drink, who goes out West and makes good. Alliance Box Co., Ltd.</p>
        <p>The annual tea and dance in connection with the above took place on Monday in the Co-operative Hall, Sansey-street. Over a hundred employees sat down to an excellent tea provided by Atkinson's Oaf Ls Ltd., after which dancing was indulged in until 2 a m. Dur- the interval songs were ably rendered by Miss C Rigby, and 'Messrs C McDonald and W Mchugh. The duties of MC. were carried out by Mr B Appleton. Masonic Installation. Bro Henry Isnerwood was installed as Worshipful Master of the " Lodge of Lights" (:48) at the Masonic Rooms, Warrington, on Monday afternoon. There was a large assembly of• the brethren, the Installing Master being W Bro the Rev T T Lee Jones. The newly-installed W,M invested his officers as follows : Bro John Evans, S.W ; Bro C J Smith, J.'W ; W Rro the Rev H V Pigot, I'M, P.Pr.G.C, chaplain ; W Bro J Hallwood, PM, treasurer; W Bro J Critchley, P.M, secretary ; Bro T Irlam Fletcher, S.D ; Bro R T Harding, ID •' W Bro F Howard, P.M, D.C, ; W -tiro It Shacklady,P.M, P.Pr.G.D.D.C.. ADO; W Bro T D Amog, P.M, P.Pr.G.O., organist ; Bros P Peacock,l.G ; J Muskett, S.S ; .1 Banks, J.S ; H Haviour, A.S ; John H Singleton, A.S ; and G S Rice, tyler. The usual banquet took place in the evening at the Lion Hotel. LAMPS, Oil Stoves, Coek Stoves.—J.S. GARLAND Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. The Late Mrs Benjamin Bennett.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon the remains of the late Mrs Benjamin Bennett (whose death.at the age of 7) years, was recorded in last week's •` Examiner ) were laid to rest in the churcnyard at Stockton Heath, the Vicar, Rev J Collins, officiating. After the hearse followed Mr Benjamin Bennett (husband), Miss Bennett, Mr Baxter Bennett, Mrs Plinston, Mr Ernest Bennett, Mrs Jones, Mr Tom Bennett, Mrs J Lowe, Mr Ben Bennett, Mrs G F Innocent, Mrs J D Smith (sons and daughters of deceased), Mr H C Jones, Mr Edwin Plinston, Mr J Lowe (Golborne), and Mr G F Innocent (sons-in-law), Mr S C Jones and Master H T Jones (grandsons), Mr ,1 Henry Bennett (Stockport, nephew), and Mr George Davey Smith. The bearers were Mr Walter Bennett (Cuerdlev), Mr Hugh Booth Bennett, Mr George Burgess "(Appleton), and Mr Will Evans (Northwich), nephews. Among (there present at the church were Mr William Sileock, Mr James Critchley, Miss Ethel 'Jones. Many floral tributes were received. Wbist Drive at the Liberal Club One of the most successful social gatherings ever held at the Liberal Club ,s as the whist drive which took place on Tuesday evening. The guests, number- ing over 100, were received by Councillors J Wilkin- son and F Lindley, Messrs. T: Pemberton, Jos. Wilkinson, II Johnson, F Lindley and E A Sanders. The large billiard room, in which the tables were arrang?d, had been very tastefully decorated with plants by the manager (Mr A T Clay) and presented a most inviting appearance After manv keen games, the prize winners were :--Ladies: 1, Mrs H Birch; 2, Miss E Bailey; consolation, Mrs. J T Locker; gentlemen: 1, Mr J W Sanson ;2, Mr Cliffe ; con- solation: Councillor J T Locker. Mr P Peacock pre- sented the prizes to the successful players, The catering was in the hands of Atkinson's Cafe's Ltd., and gave .every satisfaction. CUTLERY.—Joseph Rodgers &amp; Sons' celebrated Knives and Forks, &amp;c.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr Peter Bate. On Saturday morning at the Castle Rubber Co.'s works, a very pleasing presentation took place, when the staff and workpeopte as-ietnbled to give Mr Peter Bate. manager, a clock and ornaments to commemorate his 2.5 years service with the firm. Mr T O'Brien in making the presentation said that considering the large number of employees who had been with the firm for so many years, it was quite unnecessary for him to dwell on the numerous good qualities Mr *Bate possessed. He was certain, however, that he was expressing the sincere xvisli r f every member of the firm when he liopnd that Mr Bate would be spared to manage the affairs of the company for at least another period as long as the one they were commemorating. Mr Bate in responding said that he felt a difficulty in adequately expressing his thanks, and said the gift would always be a pleasing memento of the fact, of which he was exceptionally proud, that he had during the years of service with the company, earned the goodwill of all those with whom he came into contact. Local Off ical's New Appointment.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Tynemouth Town Council on Wednesday, Mr Walter Sumner, of the Town Clerk's Department, Warrington, was uranimouslv appointed chief clerk in the town clerk's department,Tynemouth. Mr Sumner commenced his business career in the office of Messrs Robert Davies and Co. in 1.89 i, where he remained until he obtained a position in the Town Clerk's office in July 1903. He has had considerable experience in municipal work and recently has been chiefly responsible for the administration of the Revenue Acts with reference to local taxation licences, this work having been transferred by the Govern- ment to County and County Borough Councils in 1908. In this connection he has been brought into direct daily contact with many townspeople, and whilst carrying out his duties n a highly satisfactory and conscientious manner, he has deservedly won the esteem of all those with whom he has had duties to perform. He is highly respected by all the officials of the Corporation, and is to be c-mgratulated on obtain- ing his new appointment, for which be is eminently fitted. The Council summons for the meeting on Tuesday next contains a resolution that Mr Sumner's resignation be accepted with regret. Mr Stanley Wilson (formerly deputy town clerk of Warrington) is the Town Clerk of Tynemouth. Warrington Swimming Club. - -</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Swimming club was held in the Masonic Hall Bold-street on Wednesday evening. Mr F Garside presided over a large attendance. Mr S Ince (the secretary) in sub- mitting his report stated that they had had a fairly successful season and,considering the slight experience the polo team had had, they had done exceptionally well. Out of 12 matches they won five, lost three and drew four, those including two friendly matches. They had scored 32 goals as against their opponents 29. The Works Squadron Championship race was not a success financially.—The financial statement sub- mitted by Mr J Wilkinson showed that the total income had been S-26 14s. 10d. and after paying expenses they had a balance in hand of :£ll Ss. 10d. Both reports • were adopted. The officers were elected as follows : - President, Alderman Bolton ; a numb r of vice-presidents including Mr A H Crosfield, Councillrr J Wilkinson, the Chief Conetable (Mr Martin Nicholls), T Locker and. Joseph Dolan ; Secretary, Mr S Ince ; Assistant Secretary, Mr R Pepper ; Treasurer, Mr J Wilkinson ; Auditors, Messrs A Jolley and A D Smith ; Corn- mitteee, Messrs A Ashton, J Newton, Beddard, J Peers, W Furnival, II Barlow, J Critchley and T Pemberton. Mr R Pepper was appointed captain of the polo team and Mr J Rees, sub-captain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Whist Drive and Dance.</head>
        <p>Whist Drive and Dance.</p>
        <p>St, Paul's Young Men's Bible class held their annual whist drive and dance in Wincnarleigh Cafe, Sankey-street, on Thursday. Mr W Binks presented whist prizes to 1, Miss Wilkinson, 2, Miss Shutt; 1, Mr J Kenwright, 2, Mr Or Baird. Dancing was kept up until 2 p.tr. M.C.'s were Messrs H S Lightfoot, hon secretary, R Pepper, hon treasurer. Pianist Mr C. Baxter.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. The annual Congregational Business Meeting was held at St. John's on Thursday evening, the cbair being cceupied by the minister, the Rev W Reid. The treasurer submitted the balance sheet for the year 1912, which was considered highly satisfactory, showing, as it did, an increased income under each of the various heads. The reports of the Sabbath school and all the congregational organizations showed that a useful work was being vigorously carried on under the able guidance of the minister. The church membership, which has steadily and continuously grown since Mr Reid took charge, showed a net increase of 25 during the year. FOR MODERN FIMPLACES and Cooking Ranges Corn bining economy with simplicity try J. S. GARLAND Ironmonger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Church Society Lectures. The Church S-ciety has arranged another interest- ing series of lectures to men and women for the coming Leet. The preachers will include: The Lord Bishop of Hull, the Revs Bernard Heywood (Vicar of Swinton), Canon Howson (Rector of Woolton), F Lind m Parkyn. (Vicar of Holy Trinity, Southpprt), J G- McCormick (vicar of St Michael's, Elbury square, London), Canon Dickson (Vicar of St Mary's, Waterloo), H p Brown (Rector of Middleton, Cheney, Northants), Dr Cogs- well (Rector of Wallasey), and Dr Warman (Principal of St Aidan's, Birkenhead) The series will open on Friday next, when the Rev Bernard Heywood will address the women in the afternoon on " Prayer " and the men in the evening on " Marriage and Divorce."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>DEATH OF MR THOMAS IRELAND.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR THOMAS IRELAND.</p>
        <p>Through the death of Thomas Ireland, Grappenhall loses one of its most respected residents. Mr Ireland died on Saturday last after an illness from heart affection lasting five weeks. By his quiet, unassuming manner end his broad-mindedness, he had gathered round him a good number of people whom he could truly call his friends, and by these his loss will be greatly felt. He was educated at Grappenhall Schools, and from there he went into the service of the L.N.-W. Railway Company. He was an engine driver for the company for many years; in conversa- tion he could recall some very interesting events as the result of his railway experience, amongst which he related the fact of being the first driver to take a goods train over the Runcorn Bridge. He also formed one of a party to board the first # engine over the iron bridge at Latchford Locks after the completion of the Manchester Ship</p>
        <p>Canal. Mr Ireland left the railway 30 years ago. Twenty years were subsequently spent as landlord uf the Cheshire Cheese Inn at Latchford, and from active business life he had been retired for the past ten years. He took a very keen interest in the Warrington Borough Band, ar.d was one of its most ardent and practical supporters. He was at one time a member of the Grappenhall Parish Council. Mr Ireland was a Freemason of the Gilbert Greensll Lodge 1250. and throughout his life was a staurch- Conservative. He leaves a widow and two daughters.</p>
        <p>•• - , 140' • • • ‘'.3.,.,,,,,gt.4.41,4;&gt;'‘ • k, • 5.,„ „ • • TT:IE I.AIE Ant. 'I'I.IOMAS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TEIE FUNERAL</head>
        <p>TEIE FUNERAL</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at Grapoenhall Parish Church on Tuesday last. The mourners were Mrs Ireland (widow), Misses Doruthy and Beatrice Ireland (daughters), Mr William Ireland, Altrincham (nephew), Mr H Didsbury, Birkenhead (cousin), Mr and Mrs W Gillibrand (brother and sister-in-law), Mr S Hildage (Newark) (brother-in-law), and MI s Morley (Altrincham), sister- in-law). Many old friends and residents paid their last tribute of resp,ct at the graveside. There were no floral tributes, by request.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>••"' ,1; 4.`• „ • 0 k) • ti• , • ;;VXVY'. • • colk . , A 6 .'; , , , • ct, A • 14,4; f '</head>
        <p>••"' ,1; 4.`• „ • 0 k) • ti• , • ;;VXVY'. • • colk . , A 6 .'; , , , • ct, A • 14,4; f '</p>
        <p>QUERIES AND ANSWERS.</p>
        <p>OUR READERS are invited to send It gal queries to be answered week by week. Inquiries must be short, concise, and plainly written, and the name and address of the writer given to show that the inquiry is genuine. The replies are by a member of the profession, and every effort is made to secure accuracy, but we cannot assume any resnonsibility for the opinion or advice given. Mark all envelopes containing queries " Legal," and address them to the EDITOR,' EXAMINER,' WARRINGTON. If 3ou do not wish initials to be used in the reply please t-uggest a nom-de-p/ame.</p>
        <p>A 0 J.—lf you were employed from the beginning "for the purposes of your employer's business" you cught to have been insured. If you were emp;oyed for a purpose not connected xvith your employer's business it is nevertheless likely that you ought to have been insured e4ince you were empfoyed regularly cn Thursdays This being so, it is useless for you to stamp the card for the weeks before you entered an Approved Society since you ale not entitled to sick benefits until 26 weeks after ycur entry into the society. The form of application for insurance cannot be antedated.</p>
        <p>W.W.—(l) No, there is no other waiting period in the case you mention. There is no second waiting period unless 12 months have elapsed and fifty weekly payments have been made since the last sickness benefit was received. (2) The Approved Society does not alwa)s insist on a low wage declaration. In this case no arreats would be debited against the woman.</p>
        <p>SUFFRAGETTE.—If a married woman who was a member of an Approved Society before marriage becomes employed on the death of her husband, the- period of one month after his death will not he taken into account for the purpose of reckoning arrears.</p>
        <p>CURIOUS.—If a married woman who was a member of an Approved Society before marriage continues to be employed, she may either remain insured on the same terms or remain suspended and become a voluntary contributor at reduced rates.</p>
        <p>DOMESTIC.—An insured domestic servant who marries may either continue paying her 3d a week or pay no more. In the former CRSO she will get medical treatment and medicines; sick pay of ss. a week for 13 weeks and disablement pay of 3s. a week afterwards. Sick pay is not given for the two weeks before and the four weeks after confinement. In the latter case her Society will put aside two- thirds of her transfer value for her benefit and will pay her on confinement 5s a week for not more than four weeks and, if It thinks fit, grant certain pay- ments during any period of sickness or distress. INSURANCE.—The Agent who told you that if you continued to pay your premiums for a little time longer you would get a Free Policy deceived you and ;on can recover the premiums you have paid. "HOLLY COTTAGE."—Your neighbour who has called his house "Holly Cottage" has committed no offence merely because your house is also called "Holly Cottage," and is not actionable.</p>
