      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Sole Agent also for the Invincible " TALBOT " which recently attained a speed of 113 miles per hour, beating all records for Cars of similar horse power by 26 miles per hour.</head>
        <p>Sole Agent also for the Invincible " TALBOT " which recently attained a speed of 113 miles per hour, beating all records for Cars of similar horse power by 26 miles per hour.</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS' CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>at a meeting of the Guardians. lie further observes that tenders received from tradesmen in respect of quarterly supplies are presented to the Guardians and a resolution is passed authorising the Chairman to open,number and initial the tenders in the Board room; they are then handed by him to the Clerk who has them extended, that is, the prices quoted per unit are multiplied by the estimated requirements for the period of the proposed contract and the results totalled. The tenders are then presented to the Guardians at an adjourned meeting and are considered on their totals only, each accepted tender being accepted as a whole. The goods estimated for are ordered from the con- tractors with the exception of food stuffs and other perishable goods which are ordered as required. The system of accepting tenders en bloc is open to objec- tion, in that it prevents the consideration of tenders from any tradesman who is unable to supply the whole of the articles set out. One tender only was received for each of the following heads : meat, mackintosh sheeting, uniforms, wines and spirits crockeryware, soda-water, coffins, thirds ; two only for each provisions, oatmeal and flour, groceries, paints, sweeping chimneys, tobacco and snuff, drugs and drapery. lam to request that the Board may-be fur- nished with the observations of the Guardians on the above.</p>
        <p>Mrs SIMPSON suggested that the letter be referred to the House Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr MATHER : If only a certain number of trades- men tender, what are the Guardians to do? Shall we send the auditor round to them and ask them to send tenders in ?</p>
        <p>Mr A BROOKS: No, divide the contracts. But I would like to know how you are going to divide chimney sweeping and that sort of thing. If he had tackled groceries, it would have been a different thing, but those are the-very goods for which we have the most competition. Mr JOLLEY said the matter was one for the Guardians and not for the auditor to settle, although the auditor was, of course, within his legal rights in calling attention to it. He (Mr Jolley) believed the system aqopted by the Board had resulted in their securing the best quality of goods at a very low price. The reason why in some instances there was only one or, as occasionally happened, no tender, was that competition was so keen that many tradesmen would not trouble to supply goods, because the margin of profit was so small. He moved that the Clerk be instructed to reply to the Local Government Board to the effect that Guardians, after full, frequent. and careful consideration, were satisfied that their method was the best, in view of all the local circum- stances and conditions, in the interests of the rate- payers as a whole. Mr A BROOKS, in seconding, said it should also be pointed out that the very items in which they got much competition were those which could not be divided.</p>
        <p>The CLERK-said that, with regard to drapery, there was something in tbe auditor's complaint. The system of taking tenders en bloc did, in that case, tend to restrict the number of tradesmen competing On one occasion the Board received four tenders for drapery from local firms, but because two of them did not tender for all the items they were thrown out and ndt considered. The com- petition was therefore tied down to one or two who were prepared to tender for every item. With regard to the opening of tenders, the order of the Local Government Board was strictly complied with. Mr A BROOKS said the drapery tenders included nothing but what a draper oueht to stock. If the orders were split up, the Board would have to pay fancy prices for oddments. Mr. Jolley's resolution was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>AUDITOR CRITICISES THE SYSTEM.</head>
        <p>AUDITOR CRITICISES THE SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>Doctors and Insured Patients.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr B L Bent presirlng. There were also present : Mrs Simpson, Mrs Collingwood, Rev A M Mitchell, Messrs A J Jolley (deputy-chairman), G Mather, W Houghton, J Billinge,W Gill, J Backbouse,W Houghton, W Flanigan, T Ritchie, J A Hughes, W Furness. A Brooks, M W Sewn, A Bottomley (clerk), It I Partridge (assistant clerk), J Brown (master), and C E Lowrance (superintendent of Cottage Homes).</p>
        <p>REV. A. M. MITCHELL'S QUERIES. On the minutes or the Finance Committee, the Rev A M MITCHELL asked whether the committee went carefully into the question of pay- ments. With regard to the Workhouse master's children, it was understood that there were to be regular paymei ts for their rations. Did the Finance Committee give attention to the point? There was also the question of visitors to the institution.At a recent meeting of the House Committee, it was reported that one of the officers had had some friend or triends staying with him for a few days. The committee ought to be able to say what amount had been paid in respect of those visitors—in fact a scale of charges ought to be drawn up. Then again a coal bill of over £4—working out at 223 a ton—bad recently been passed. He thought that was an exorbitant .ch urge, and the Finance Com- mittee ought to be able to detect it. Mr A J JOLLEY (chairman of the Finance Committee) .said the members of that committee were quite as anxious as Mr Mitchell to look after all the details. They did not show their anxiety in the same form but it was just as real and earnest, and in the interests of the ratepayers. NEW BUILDING SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The Building Committee again considered plans for the erection of a building for the accommoda- tion of male and female imbecile and epileptic patients. The architect (Mr Segar Owen) sub- mitted two schemes and, after careful consideration, it was resolved—by four votes to one—that the Board be asked to proceed with the erection of a building in accordance with plans prepared by Messrs W and S Owen, on lines somewhat similar to a building erected at the Reading Parish Workhouse ard in accordance with the suggestion of the officers of the Local Government Boird, and that the Local Govern- ment Board be asked to give their sanction thereto. The Committee, were aware that if the scheme was proceeded with forthwith in its entirety, the question of the erection of new Board room and offices must be dealt with at once, and, so as to defer the office ques- tion for a time, they recommended that temperary pro- vision be made in the main building for the reception of patients, also padded room accommodation ite. Mr Owen was instructed to modify his plans accordingly, and at a further meeting of the committee he submitted a set-,nni- on those lines, involving a total expenditure of £12,580. Cousiaeiable discussion took place on the above minute, the Rev A M MITCHELL moving an amendment that it be referred back. From the first he had opposed that very expensive scheme, because it seemed to him that the Board were taking upon themselves a work which ought to be done by another authority. There was little doubt that when the total outlay came to be reckoned up, it would be practically double the -amount stated in the minutes. Referring to the fact that the Board would shortly go out of office, the rev. gentleman said the Guardians reminded him of people who, the nearer they were ta bankruptcy, the greater dash they made. The Board was approaching the close of its existence and it seemed to have a great desire to spend lavishly and make a big show. Mr W C BROOKS. in seconding the amendment, con- tended that, as part of the scheme was of a temporary -character, the ultimate cost would be £20,000. They would have to undertake the erection of new offices and Board room in the immediate future. He favoured the scheme which was originally p4ssed by the com- mittee but rejected by the Board. Mr BILLINGE characterised Mr Brooks's estimate of £20,000 as very far-fetched. The scheme was the better adspted to the requirements of the Board, whereas the original one, which Mr Brooks favoured, did not commend itself to the officers of the Local Government Board. After further discussion the Building Committee's recommendation was approved by 13 votes to 3. Mr W C BROOKS: k very unlucky number! PRAISE FOR THE INFIRMARY. . .</p>
        <p>The House Committee recorded having learned with pleasure that Dr Corker, medical officer of the Rochdale Workhouse, on a recent visit to the Workhouse Infirmary, had spoken very warmly as to the high state of efficiency of the nursing staff and general arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>VISITORS TO THE WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>VISITORS TO THE WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the House Committee, Mr W C BROOKS introduced the question of persons visiting relatives or friends in the Workhouse and stated that he was asked by a person to give a note requesting the master or matron to allow her to visit an inmate of the Workhouse. At the time the person called he was Lusy and he told her to pay the visit and mention his name. The person afterwards spoke to him and stated that when his name was m ntioned the matron stated that she would please herself whether she took any notice of Mr Brook's request or not. The committee adjourned consideration of the question until the next meeting and the clerk was instructed to ask the matron to attend. DOCTORS AND INSURANCE PATIENTS. It was reported to the House Committee that Dr Liston, of Warrington, had requested permission to interview and examine a patient in the Infirmary for the purpose of giving a certificate under the Nat ional Insurance Act. The committke gave very careful con- sideration to the question of the desirability or other- wise of outside doctors visiting and examining patients in the Workhouse Infirmary and recommended that permission be refused in all such cases. This was agreed to. L.G.B. AUDITOR'S CRITICISMS. THE CONTRACTS SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>The CLERK read the fQllowirg letter from Mr J D Davy, assistants secretary to the Local Government, Board am directed by the Local Government Board to inform the Guardians ot the Warrington Union, that Mr P T Turner, the assistant district auditor, alter his audit of the accounts of the Warrington Union for the half year ended March 31st, 1912, reported that the Guardians had not complied with the provision in Article 47 of the Genera! Consolidated ()icier, 1847, to the effect that no tender shall be opened otherwise tnan</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LORD NEWTON. WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE AND THE HOUSE OF CCMMONS.</p>
        <p>Lord Newton was in the chair on Saturday at a quarterly meeting in St. Helens of Newton Division Conservative and Unionist Association. Lord Wolmer. M.P., also attended. Lord Newton said that it was more difficult now than formerly to say when a Parliament would come to an end, when its members were each interested to the extent of £4OO a year. At the presant moment the ll,tuse of Commons was nominally discussing a Fran- chise Bill, and discussing it under such conditions as Mr Asquith thought fit to impose upon it. What the House of Commons was trying at the present moment to do was to " dish" the women and to sneak out of difficulties which they had created, either be their own dishonesty or bs their ignorance. "The House of Commons,'" continued Lord Newton," has been alritady sufficiently degraded by Mr Asquith. It has beerr"degraded as much as it is possible to degrade and humiliate any constitutional assembly, but I ques- tion whether it has ever presented so absolutely grotesque, ridiculous, and humiliating a spectacle as it does at the present moment." His impressiom was that nothing would cast such absolute dismay into the Liberel ranks as for the House of Lords to allow the Home Rule measure to pass. He believed any prospect of that kind would fill the Liberal party with the utmost consternation. (Laughter.) If it were passed be believed they would find the difficulties so insurmountable that they would seize the earliest opportunity for leaving office. He did not think the question of allowing this Bill to pass the House of Loras bad ever been really considered, although the suggestion had been made. But the objection to adopting any such course was that it would be a distinctly dishonest proceeding, and he hoped that, whether mistaken or right, the action of the Lords would be dictat,d by honest motives. It would be a dishonest thing to accept the Home Rule Bill for many reasons, but one was that it had never been properly before the country. (Applause). Load Wolmer defended the position he had taken us in regard to the use of the funds of trade unions for the purposes of members of one political party in Parliament It was only fair and just, he said, that trade union, should be allowed to send their representatives to the House of Commons without impediment, but what the Labour party wanted was to use trade union as supporters of one political party. At the next general election he was prepared to maintain the position that every trade unionist, whether Conservative, Socialists Liberal or what not, had a right to choose his own political views for himself. To that mast he nailed his colours, and he was prepared to fight on that question and win against all-comers.—(Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>HAIRDRESSER'S "ACCIDENT."</head>
