      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WAR CZ !ROTOR 119. t • TT • ( Is' ".-• . • . . ewe, RELIGIOUS</head>
        <p>WAR CZ !ROTOR 119. t • TT • ( Is' ".-• . • . . ewe, RELIGIOUS</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sunday the annual sermons on behalf of the Church of England Waifs and Strays Society, were preached morning and evening by the Rev Herbert A Wafter), the organising socretary for the Liverpool district. A "pound service" was held in the aftel noon, when pounds of tea, coffee, anger, rice, flour, Sze., were brought by members of the con- gregation adults and children and placed near the altar. The gifts were afterwards sent to the society's local home in Salisbury-street.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Men's Bible Class.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon the speaker at the above class was Mr J B Baxter, who gave an interesting lecture on "The Bible." The origin of our Bible was traced from the ancient manuscripts; and the various heroes of Bible production were introduced. The painstaking energy which was ever characteristic of the translaters, their scholastic abilities and the thoroughness of their research, all make our Bible, a book in which we can implicitly believe. The need for a revision of the completeness of the methods adopted to produce the revised version, were pointed out by the lecturer. The president of the cla,s (Mr J C Holland) end several members, expressed their hearty appreciation of Mr Baxter's able address.</p>
        <p>, ()</p>
        <p>. -r". 11if,,, elit, - 4IPAV,F., Ai, -, '''' ":47r.. 184,1;::•:::::....;. 7....., ••::::;°: - • i ' .V. • ' . V''g' '-',... \ : • i 'a ,r. . A f ( , ... . - ' =; . 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>English Church Union.</head>
        <p>English Church Union.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington branch of the English Church Union was held at the Masonic Hall, Bold- street, on Tuesday evening, when the Rev L L Orton (Society of the Faith) gave a lecture on " The Orthodox Greek Church." He contended that the schism between the Eastern and Western branches of the Catholic Church was largely due to racial antagonism and that made re-union all the more difficult.. Referring to the vca.: in the Balkan States, he said all Christians looked forward to the tin e when the Turks would be driven out, and the Greek relfgionists would return to the possession of their Own city. The Rev W J Stanton, who presided, said that one hoped that one of the results of the war And the alliance of the Balkan States would be that the schism, although one entirely of nationality, might be speedily healed, and that there would be peace within the whole of the Orthodox Church. On the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr E H Coates, a vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday, presided over by the President (Mr B Farrar), who gave a short report of the Lancashire P.S.A.Federation meetings at Ashton-under-Lyne. Opportunity was taken to con- gratulate Aldermen Parker, president of the Federation, on attaining his 76th birthday. Mr Thomas Dale gave the address on "Choose ye this day whom ye will serve" and "As for me and my house,we will serve the Lord." He emphasised the desirability of a good choice, and said that to serve God meant to make sacrifices for God and man, in trying to bring peace and brightness to the homes of the people. Mr Cotton read the lesson, and Mr Himsworth led the prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Guild.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Guild.</p>
        <p>The above society met on Monday evening, when ft paper was given by Mrs Henshall, of Latchford, on "The cry of the oppressed." With many apt illus- trations, the speaker pointed out bow history repeated itself and as God raised up Moses to free the children of Lrael from their cruel oppressions, so He had from time to time, in our own land and in other lands, raised up good men and women to fulfil some work and in doing this, their source of nower had been felt and acknowledged throughout the entire earth, a mighty influence for good. This was the only source ...from which oppression in all its firms could be mitiiited and released; a truer standard of Christian duty faith- fully followed, would strengthen the individual and the nation and overthrow the causes of oppression. There was a good discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>Preaching at St John's or; Sunday evening last; the Rev W Reid spoke of the various sources of sacrifice in connection with Judaistic worship and its orderly sequence running there through. Taking the sin offer- ing, with its roi.t idea of atonement, be proclaimed the fundamental of Christianity as Christ, in his life, death, resurrection and intercession. The burnt offering,witH its root idea of consecration, marked the next point of development, and spoke of the willing and complete self surrender to the call of God and humanity. The peace offering, having, at its root the idea of thanks- giving, was the point on which the preacher dwelt the most, putting before his hearers suggestions of the out%ard forms in which the thanksgiving spirit might clothe itself, and which were to be found in inereasing interest in and faithfulness. to the various ministries oi the Church, in various rtprns of its worship, and in the upkeep and extension of its ordinances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Sunday School.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Sunday School.</p>
        <p>A jumble sale promoted by the members of the yonng ladies Bible class was held on Saturday. Mr W H Sherburn presided, and in the coarse of a brief address expressed his pleasure at the opportunity of renewing old acquaintances with the peop;e at Ellesmere-street. He had every sympathy with the object which brought them together that afternoon, namely, the renovating of their class rooms, and hoped the day was not far distant when ft substantial brick building Would replace_ the old one which had stood them in such good steactin the days gone by. Mrs Frank Hickman, in opening the sale, said it was the first time she had occupied such a position. She was pleased to be of any possible assistance to them and wished them every success. Brief speeches were also made by Messrs F Hickman, T H Gregson and J einch. On Monday evening a successful concert was held, Mr John Upson presiding over a large gathering. A highly enjoyable programme included items by Miss E Hewitt (soprano), Mr 0 Dickenson (tenor), Mr E Walker (baritone), Mr J Newnes (humorist) and Miss L Scarisbrick (child vocalist). A novel feature was provided by Mr 'V/ W Dunbobbin and his performing dog "Comic."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>80ld-street Wesleyan Church. "Self-Righteousness."</head>
        <p>80ld-street Wesleyan Church. "Self-Righteousness."</p>
        <p>Choosing as a text Luke xviii, 9, the Rev ET Williamson, 8.A., spoke nu " Self-righteousness." The parable of the Pharisee and the Publican contains, not only a message to some individual Pha-isee of far-if days in Palestine,but a modern lesson to a number of people,who trust in themselves that they are righteous, and despise others. This lesson is perfectly ph,in ; its teaching being that such persons are, judged by ultimate standards, entirely ong It may be allowed that, as a statement of fact, the Pharisee's self-laudation is quite true. The falsehood does not lie in the state- ment. It is to the self-righteousness and pri le that we must look, for the weak spot that vitiates the life. The self-righteous man is always a weak man. The- Pharisee was sufficiently strong-willed to refrain from extortion, adultery, and gluttony ; yet, his strength of will did not imply strength of clrracter. He was crippled by the weakness of vanity, which leads man to commit treacheries of which he would not dream himself capable The strong-willed men, with bitter racial, doctrinal, or political prejudices are puny individuals withal, who worship the smallest of deities, viz. themselves The haughty Englishman is too vain to recognise the fine points in the German ; the orthodox individual is too orthodox to appreciate the vastness of knowledge; the heretic is too busy worshipping heresy to examine the other side of the question. This type of man is too small for us to worship. The great souls are those who have felt the vastness of the world ; feeling with Kelvin and Darwin, that their knowledge is slight, their ignorance profound. The greatest s .ints, like St. Paul and St. John. have confessed themselves the burr blest of sinners. This is not sheer morbidness, but a genuine conviction that, with all their striving, these saintly souls are still an infinite distance from the ideal. The universe is a spacious affair, and it is the sense of its spaciousness we need to preserve. During the Fprwice the anthem," Happy and blest are they," was ably rendered by the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Wycliffe 13ongregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe 13ongregational Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev H H Stull. 8.A., of Darwen was the preacher at this church on Sunday. The hard was the subject of his address to the children, based on the text "My soul is continually in Thy Hand." In an interest- ing way he showed the characteristics of the hand and impressed upon his young hearers the importance of keeping their bands free from dishonourable actions. 'their souls, he added, were in an especial degree in their own hands. The sermon dealt with the story of the vineyard (Matthew 20). He said it was the story of human life in its relation to the eternal, and our little figurines and calculations were out of place in regard to such a matter. God wanted our service, loyalty, faih and love early in the morning of life rind all through life but He did not coerce us into these things. '1 here was no bargaining as to the reward, .s God had a way of reckoning of His own. A small quantity of work done in a right spirit was of greater value than a large quantity of work done in a wrong spirit, however, zealously it might have been performed. In the evening, the discourse was specially addressed to young people, the text being "Rejoice, 0 young man, in thy youth ; and let thy heart cheer thee in the days of thy youth, end walk in the ways of thine heart rind in the sight of thine eyes ; but know thou, that for all these things God will bring thee into jude- ment." (Ecclesiastes xi. 9). Youth, he said, was a ' critical time, for momentous issues were being settled in the early years, It was the determinant of what the future life was to be. He proceeded to speak of what the parents can do for their sons and daughters, and secondly of what the sons and daughters can do for themselves, He showed that modern industrialism had brought about the early economic independence of young people and weakened the home ties. It was the character of the parents rather than their money that had the greater effect on their children's lives and happiness. He advised sons and daughters to rejoice in their youth to be pleasant and make themselves agree- able to older people. Owing to the gross miscarriage of education, conversation and reading were becoming lost arts. He exhorted them to cultivate these arts and also their sense of imagination, and above all these things to have a reverent spirit, honouring God, their King and country. Collections were taken on behalf of the Lancashire Congregational Union which works for the extension of Congregationalism in the country. To-morrow (Sunday) the Rev. T T James, M.A., commences his ministry at Wycliffe Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Cairo-street Chapel. Dr. Mellor and his Work In Warrington</head>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel. Dr. Mellor and his Work In Warrington</p>
        <p>have never found an atmosphere in which freedom was so intensely real as it is in this church," were the words of the Rev Dr S A Mellor in an interesting address at the annual congregational tea party in con- nection with the Cairo-street Chapel, held in the school- room on Monday evening. About 160 persons partook of tea, the tables being presided over by Mesdames Mellor, Pemberton, Shaw, C Madeley, Hill and the Misses D Monks and Houghton At the subsequent meeting, the choir, under the direction of Mr J M Muir, submitted an excellent anti varied musical programme. Mr W H Woodcock presided and was supported by the pastor, Dr Mellor, the Revs N Cross '.Southport) and E S Russell (Liverpool), and Messrs F W Monks, J W Davies (secretary), H Noble and E Plinston (superin- tendent of the Sunday scho('l). In his opeuiug remarks the Chairman cheracterised such a large meeting as reminiscent uf Cairo-street in days gone by, when the chapel might be said to be at its best. Those days might be repeated to-day and the, chapel might he seen at its best once more. Since Dr Mellor's urrival there had been a considerable increase in the congregations, but still there were a few vacant places which they would like to see occupied. It was really important, if Cairo-street was to attain a high pesition arid bear out its n ible tra tat they should all take a regular part in the services. It was plainly evident that the Sunday School must play a large part in the future developments of the work at Cairo-street, and to see such a large progressive Sunday School as they now had was a good augury. He thought they would agree with him that Dr 'Mellor had made a great impression on Warritigton. (Hear, hear.) Whether they agreed with him or not, in everything he said. they must be conscious that he was teaching them how to think—(bear, hear)—and that was a very important point. He was, as they all knew, deeply interested in all kinds of social work and he realised as he (the speaker ),and all of them realised, how much there ought to be done and how itch there could be done if one really tried to do it. Tee Doctor was by his sermons teaching them to think for t her 'selves, if nothing more. They were extremely Odd to have the visitors with them from other towns to encourage them to work. (Applause), Mr E PLINSTON gave a resume of the Sunday School work and said the Sunday school and all its institutions were in a progressive aria flourishing condition. At the beginning of lase year they had 184 scholars on the books and at the end of the year they had 235. (Applause.) That was a very good advance. The increase had been mostly in the adult classes and they now had almost. 140 scholars over the age of sixteen years. (Applause ) bought they ought to be able , to look to the fir ure of the school and chapel with levery confidence with such a goed number of young peeple under the leadership of eliss Houghton and Dr Mellor. Not only were their numbers increasing hut the older scholars were beceming more animated with that spirit of loyalty tied self-sacrifice which was so much desired. Although they were, doing so well there were two directions in which he would like to appeal to them for support. They needed more teachers for the boys' class and they would also like to increase the number of scholars in the infant and junior classes. They had a sick club, band of hope, teachers' prepara- tion class rind reading circle, librars, girl's club,a penny bank and a youn; pa pl •'s so iety. They also had a cricket club which did good in its ewn way. (Applause). In an interesting ederess Dr Aleesoß said that when four more Sundays had passed by he would have been with ahem exactly a saar, as their minister. (Applause) He thought they could look back on that twelve months with a certain satisfaction, not undue or conceited satisfaction, but humble satisfaction. During that period they had done a little good work—(applause)— the size and character of that meeting bore witness to that fact. Mr Woedcock told them that meeting reminded him of Caito-street in its best days. It always seemed to be rather ma pity that best days should be considered te belong to the past For him the best days of an institution belonged to the future. Best days could uever be past days. They were either present or to come and that was true of institutions, individuals and the wield at large. It was that sort of a spirit that in a way formed the basis of all his philosophy arid activities. It was because be believed profoundly and absolutely in th, future that be did what he did and said what be saia. He thanked them for the extraordinary kindness they had shown to him during his ministry. He had never found an atmosphere in which freed ne was so int.nsely real as it was in that church. (Applause.). The breath of their real life was freedom. The life of the tuture absolutely and utterly depended upon freedom. It could not be lived without freedom of mind, spirit, intellect and will. Without freedom, life was impossible. Sometimes in these days when a ministry was so essentially a personal thing, one was apt to forget the denomination and wider circles of church lite, tut they were there, and in their denomination the tradi i not' religion Was the tradition of freedom. That was what they had stood for through the centuries, and in those old chapels like Cairo-street, with such a magnificent hist re, tat gl 'Hone con-. dition of freedom had been realized. At any rate it had been realized in his case during the last twelve months. Never by word or sign, or in any way to bis knowledge, had any member or friend of the congreg Lion interfered with his freedom. (Applause, That wes not a tribute to his sincerity ; it was a tribute to the'r loyalty to principle. V% hen they became mem be s of that Church and asssoci- ated themselve- with it in any way and gave their hearts and lives to it, they became associated with a certain principle and to that they expressed their loyalty. Mr Woodcock bad said perhi pa it, was not always that members of the congregation end chapel had agreed entirely and altogether with the things he said. That was never likely to he the case and never could be amongst human beings The thing which was so destructive to their real spiritual energy and life, was the attemist to impose an-.'s views upon others to the exclusion of thPir OWD It was the assertion of an external principle of authority that made religion a dead and meaningletss thing. He had, never asked or</p>
        <p>expected that every individual should subscribe absolutely and utterly to the views he expressed. All he had expected and asked from them had been that they should endeavour to think for themselves and if the result of their independent and individual thought had taken them in a direction different from that which be bad taken, they had been able to differ, knowing that in spirit they were one. That was the character and life of that chapel and that was why it was possible for him to say things,freely, sincerely and openly. Continuing the Doctcr said he had! found Cairo-street happy in its officials and he took that opportunity of thanking all of them. He was proud of the increase of membership in the Sunday school and lie was also proud of the distinct increase in the size of the congregation. He was happy to think that that increase was likely to be a permanent and not an accidental or temporary one, in conclusion the Doctor said he believed—and he said it entirely without conceit for he was not egostic in the matter—that he had created an atmosphere in which people felt better for the coming into it. They entered the chapel and realised things which they could not realise in their ordinary life. To make the chapel what it c tight to be they wanted freedom, loyalty and, above all, sincerity of purpose. He felt sure that he had found those things during the 12 months he had been with them. How- ever, he could not have made any impression in War- rington if it had not been for the congregation who had given him the freedom he wanted. Ile stood for a strong congregation, spiritually and numerically and he thanked them. (Applause). After interesting addresses had been given by the Revs E S Russell and N Cross a vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman, the visiting ministers and I helpers on the proposition of Mr F 1V Motitis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
