      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Late Mr. Sam Hassell. Memorial Service at the Workingmen's</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Sam Hassell. Memorial Service at the Workingmen's</p>
        <p>Mission</p>
        <p>A deeply impressive memorial service to the late Mr Sam Hassid!, conductor of the Warrington Male Chora* Union, was field on Sunday evening in the Working- men's Mission with which he had been identified from early boyhood. There was a crowded congregation. The widow, parents and many relatives and friends of the late Mr Hassan attended. Mr Harrison, of Liverpool, was the preacher. The Warrington Male Choral Union was in attendance and rendered "The long day c,oses " very feelingly. Mrs B A Sanders gave an expressive interpretation of "0 rest in the Lord" and Mr W Shaw, of Runcorn, sang "Thou art passing hence" in a deeply sympathetic manner. Mr F Atkinson was at the organ rind played the i Dead March n " Saul." Mr Geo Dale, in the course of an appreciation of Mr Hassall, said that, while interested in many things, he was most widely known, respected and loved through the musical talent which had been bestowed upon him so lavishly and which he cultivated so thoroughly and assiduously. Towards the end of the year 1900 four or five young men who were closely connected. with the Bank 'Quay Mission decided to spend their winter evenings in rehearsing glees. So satisfactory were those, meetings, that they invited i others to join, and n ebruary 1901 the Warrington Male Voice Choir was inaugurated with a membership of 12, Mr- Hassall being leader. At the pre- sent time the Choral Union, of which Mr Hassall and Mr Nesbitt were latterly joint leaders, had a membership of over GO. He need not remind them of the splendid services of that organisation. They had all been keenly interested in the reports which had come to them from time to time relating to the honours that had been wi ill n important competitions, and they at Bank Quay Mission experienced a glow of justifiable pride as, in their thoughts, they had associ- ated with those triumphs the name of one whom they still claimed as one of them. His connection with the Warrington Musical Society extended over fifteen years and he was also orFanizer and leader of the Highfield Tannery choir. huncor. He was conductor of the mission choir for many years and was willing to help whenever his musical ability could be employed for the furtherance of some high and noble object. Mr Dale referred to several notable characteristics of the late Mr liassall, including his reverence or his parents, his genial disposition and good htunour.Finally he expressed deep sNmpathy with those who, by the ties of nature, were so near and dear to him, and who had so acutery felt the pangs of bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Qum Congregational Tea Meeting.</head>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Qum Congregational Tea Meeting.</p>
        <p>- - The congregation cud workers associated with the Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay, held their annual tea meeting on Monday evening and, as usual, the function was a very happy and enjoyable one. About 135 sat down to tea, and a nicely varied and very inter- esting entertainment followed. Mrs Crosfleld, of Colwyn Bay, presided and the audience expressed its appreciation of her great interest in and 'practical support of the work of the mission by giving her a very hearty welcome. The major portion of the programme was supplied by the Sylvan , Quartette Party (Mesdames Sara Silcock and 14 Eckersley. Messrs W Dutton and Sephton). The voices of the party blended moat effectively in " When the budding blown," " Good night, beloved," "In this hour of softened splendour " arid " Good evening." Madame Silcock was enthusi- astically encored for en artistic interpretation of " 0 flower of all the world." Madame Eckersley was heard to distinct advantage in " My dear Soul." Mr Dutton acquitted himself well in "*Thy sentinel am I," end Mr Sephton sang "The sailor's grave" with expression. ' Madam is Silcock anu Eckersley were a:sociated in the duet " I heard a voice," and Messrs Dutton and Sephton gave " The battle eve." Masters Dunbavaud and Dowling gave humorous recitatione with great success. Much-appreciated features were the dramatic recitals " he Friend " and "'1 he Lifeboat "by Mr Dean. Mr F Atkinson played the accompaniments in sympathetic style. The annual report on the work of the Mission was presented by the Secretary (Mr A Tilling) who stated that the year had been one of steady progress. He emphasised the fact that there was an indestructible link between the Sunday school and the chapeLso much so that the condition of the one havariabiy reflected the condition of the other. It was quite impossible to treat them as two distinct institutions, it had long been recognised by the past and present workers of the Mission, but in many churches the truth was only just beginning to he realised that the S nday school was an extremely important branch (if not the most important branch) of the church. The membership had been increased by 16 during the year, making the number on the roll 136, the highest it had ever been in the history of the Mission. The number of Communicants during the year was 820, an average of 68 per Communion service. The attend ,nces at all the services of the Mission had been fully maintained, and it was safe to say that the average attendance at the various meetings held en the Mission premises every Sunday (including Sunday school, , cha?el,n.nd lantern services) totalled almost 2,000. The ' new" Excelsior " 11.11 had proved, and was proving, a very great boon. While the thanks of the workers were given utterance to in the Sunday echool secretary's report a few weeks ago, they felt that in the Chapel ' report they must place on record their high appreciation of the splendid gift of Mrs Crosfield, and to again assure her that her kindness and generosity were a great stimulus to the workers, in the responsible and sometimes trying duties they had to perform. They again had no difficulty in filling their pulpit during the year. They had had rnen of many different denomina- ti"ns, and no doubt missions such as theirs did a good deal to break down the barriers which had divided so many Christians in the past. With regard to financial matters, the chapel accounts stood as follows :—Brought forward from the previous year £ll 18s 70, income during the Near £133 2s expenditure during the ! year £136 16s o.d,balance in treasurer's hands £8 4s 7j,d. The bazaar held in November was a great success, the total prefit, after paying all expenses, being £277 5s 2id. That, together with the amount they previously had in the Mission Extension Fund, made a total of £3ll Ss lid in baud. The Officers and Com- mittee for the year 1913 were as follows :—Presiden t Mr Nathan Hough ; vice-president, Mr George Dale ; treasurer, Mr T Taylor ; communion secretary, Mr It Jones ; general ecretary, Mr A Tilling ; Committee Messrs T Dale, D 11111, W Worrell, I? Atkinson A E Edwares, 3 Hassell, T Edmunds, T Woolley, P Cleave Arthur Gleave,S Millington, nos Ashley and Thomson. , The amount dispensed in relief by the League of Help during the pest year was £34' 19s 6d, in addition to numerous articles of clothing, S.c. The League worked very helpfully along with the Civic Guild of Help. The lantern services were crowded with children every Sunday night,and they thanked God for the opportunity of influencing for good so many children, a large number of whom did not attend any Sunday school or place of worship. In responding to a vote of thanks (proposed by Mr T Dale) Mrs Crosfleld expressed her pleasure at being present. She was glad to hear of the continued success of the work of the mission. The amount realised at the bazaar was more than she had anticipated and she thought all must have worked very bard to bring about such a result. Now that the new vestry had relieved the pressure in the Sunday school, she thought that the next matter whirls would require attention was the extension of tine chapel, which might be undertaken probably next year. She greatly appreciated the volun- tary work that was carried on by the misson and hoped it would meet with still greater success in the future. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Professor Hobhouse's Visit.</head>
        <p>Professor Hobhouse's Visit.</p>
        <p>Those who are interested in the principles under- lying social and political progress should embrace the opportunity of hearing Professor L T llobhouse at S o'clock. next Monday night at the .Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street Professor Hobhouse will deal with the relation of biology to politics, and his address should prove interesting and informative. Professor Hobhouse has been described as one of the most brilliant of the Oxford School of Philosophy, and his reputation as an exponent of social philosophy is already world wide. Only recently he was called and gave a series of lectures on Sociology to Columbia University at New York. He is the author of several important works on his subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>- •</p>
        <p>THE TRADE BOOM.</p>
        <p>NO CAUSE FOR INTERNATIONAL</p>
        <p>JEALOUSY.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS TO THE CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>The great boom in trade was the principal subject of the presidential address of Mr F A FROST at the annual meeting of tue Warrington Chamber of Commerce held at the Town Hall on Monday. He said he was proud to have occupied the presidency during a period of record activity, giving the highest figures of the Empire's commerce that have ever been reached, and this in spite of many disadvantages, such as the coal strike, which was general, most deplorable and disastrous in its effects on all concerned ; the transport strike in London, and the partial railway strike in the North. Had it not been tor those unfortunate setbacks the country would have been better off still. On looking around he noticed we were not the only nation that had prospered during the period under review. Some people are apt to look upon this with an unfavourable eye. Personally, he Was of the opposite opiriion, for JAe considered THE MORE OTHER NATIONS PROSPERED THE I:ETTER.</p>
        <p>FOR US; they would have greater needs which we could and did supply, and were in a better position to pay for them either in cash or kind. It was better to have an affluent customer than a poor one. Also the desire and ability for nations to trade together was a very strong deterrent to the abominations of detestable wars, which brought no good even to the victors. Wars, NNhether industrial or international, were to be avoided. The former could only be done on the basis of goodwill between employers and employed, possibly aided by the principle of arbitration, and the recognition that their interests were identical. The latter was some- what difierent for however much a nation objected to fighting it did dot altogether rest with that nation whether it was or not. Every nation was liable to attack from wiLhout, and therefore it was necessary to be so strong that any attack would be doomed to failure. For that strength they had to rely entirely on the manhood of the nation, and he hoped that before long every man would be able to be at his post, in an efficient condition to give a proper account of himself, should he be called upon to do so. As soon as this was acknowledged by all nations there would be little or no risk of outside trouble, and they could continue to attend to their own industrial progress without any fear. Given peace within and peace without, they hoped to see still further record figures, more pros- perity, less and less unemployment with its accompany, - jug misery. (Applause). The PRESIDENT moved the adoption of the report which, in the course of an exhaustive review of the events of the year, stated that the Council had from time to time considered the recent strikes and the industrial situation generally, and it had been decided to ask some prominent leader of industry to address q, pullic meeting at an early date on the possibilities of co-partnership as a solution of the question. The Council, after agitating for the improvement of Bank Quay station for several years, were glad at last to be able to report that the platforms had been raised, and that other improvements were in progress. But in their opinion much still remained to be done before the station was equal to present requirements, and was worthy of the importance of the town. The accounts of the Chamber showed a balance in the bank of Ifis _ _</p>
        <p>Mr HOLMAN KINGDON (Messrs J Crosfield and Sons, Ltd..) in seconding, said he was much interested by the President's allusion to national defence, which was really a form of insurance. Nowadays they heard a great deal about insurance from various points oe view, and the question of defence was one that should not be forgotten by any commercial man. The report was adopted. THE INSURANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDEN'T, as the Chambr's representative on the Insurance Committee, reported that the committee was very busy at work, with the object of bringing the banefits home for those whom they were supposed to benefit. At present it was all spade work, and the result would be shown later on.</p>
        <p>ILL-HEALTH AND COMMERCIAL INEFFICIENCY Mr KINGDoN, reporting on the year's work of the Education Committee, laid particular stress upon the importance of the physical welfare of the children in its effect on the commercial community. Warrington firms were mainly dependent on the product of the Warrington schools for recruiting the main portion of their staffs. An unhealthy clerk, even if intelligent, was in the long run of little use either to his master, his family or himself, and it was, therefore, only right that the Chamber, with no uncertain voice, should express its approval of the policy of the Education Committee that a healthy body must be a condition precedent to effective school education. It may fairly be said that the medical inspection side of educational work in Warrington was making progress. The medical clinic, which was the machinery tor seeing that the child's ailment,revealed by the school:medical inspection,obtpiined proper treatment was getting better known, and consequently tatter liked. The parents of the poorest children, who, except in serious illness, had hitherto had no doctor at all, were beginning to see the advantage of having their children's defects pro, ptly and properly attended to and on the other hand, where as was unfortunately the case, the ignorance or apathy of the parent prevented the child from receiving the necessary medical treat- ment power was now given under the Children Act to avoid the sins of the fathers being visited on the children. A special branch of medical treatment was the care of the teeth. Roughly speaking, 75 per cent. of the children attending elementary schools in War- rington had unsound teeth, and that fact alone, if further proof were necessary would be sufficient justi- fication for the dental clinic, which was established a little over twelve months ago, and which was getting into good working order. It was hoped that in time the town would see its way to confirm the appointment of at least one whole-time dentist for dealing with this particular work, and there was probably no direction in which public money could be better spent—on the principle of " a stitch in time," and that prevention was always cheaper than cure. England still had a lot of leeway to make up in social matters like those compared with what had been done by our neighbours across the North Son, where in 1912 no less than forty-five of the big towns had well-established dental clinics, which in many instances had been working for some years. Might the fact that Warrington had been the first of the large towns in this country to adopt practical means of raising the standard of national health amongst the children of the elementary schools be not only an incentive to further efforts in the same direc- tion, but also an example to others to follow in her footsteps.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE TRADE BOOM. The MAYOR moved the re-election of Mr Frost as president. He said that his abilities eminently fitted him for the office, and a more suitable gent lenian could not be found. He was rather disappointed—perhaps it was an oversight in the President's speech—that he did not make it clear whether the becoming prosperity applied to Warrington. He did not know whether the wave of good trade was actually reaching Warrington. They were told it had, but he had not been able to verify it from his own experience, and many other people were in doubt on the point. He was glad the President sounded the note of satisfaction over inter- national prosperity. They ought not to be jealous of the prosperity of other nations, because when other nations; prospered so did we pan i ?Assn Mr R T FAIRCLOUGH (Messrs 3 Fairclough and Sons Ltd.) seconded. Mr Frost had done exceedingly well for the Chamber, and he wished the members would respond in appreciation of his work. The Chamber would then be more useful than it had hitherto bum.</p>
        <p>The motion was warmly agreed to. Mr FROST, hi ac!inowledgment, said he did not think it necessary for him to say that the trade boom applied to Warrington. Without Warrington the Empire could not exist—(laughter)—and the Mayor ought to know better than anybody else that Warrington being the hub of the Empire, why of course trade was good in the town. (Laughter and applause.) The NTAYort (smilingly) : There are e,:ceptions to every rule,and I thought Warrington might possibly be excepted. Mr FROST: No, sir • if Warrington were down, the st would be down. _</p>
        <p>Captain Crosfield was elected to the vice-presidency. Mr FROST remarking that he had shown hy great ofrsonal sacrifice and his love of Empire. RS well as his devotion for his native town, that he was eniinently fitted for the position. Mr W Skinner was re-elected hoe treasurer, and Mr C D Parkinson was again appointed hon auditor. The retiring members of the Council were re-elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>FREE MEDICINE IN WARRINGTON. Veno's Specialities Working Wonders. FREE FOR SHORT TIME ONLY.</head>
        <p>FREE MEDICINE IN WARRINGTON. Veno's Specialities Working Wonders. FREE FOR SHORT TIME ONLY.</p>
        <p>A nurse is in attendance daily at Messrs. Boots, Cash Chemists, corner of Sankey street and Bridge street, Warrington, to give free doses of Veno's Lightning Cough Cure and Veno's Seaweed Tonic to all who call.</p>
        <p>Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is the far famed remedy for all Throat, Chest, and Lung Troubles, which is sold the world over and recommended by many doctors. Veno's Seaweed Tonic is guaranteed to cure the most aggravated forms of •••tornach, Liver, Kidney, and Blood Diseasds, and the cures which are daily announced are of such an extraordinary character. as to excite deep and widespread interest. All suffering persons are requested to call immedi- ately and test these remedies free. They can be purchased afterwards at Is l!d. and 2s. 9d. of any chemist. Don't hesitite to call. All are welcome Last day for free doses, Monday, February 10th.</p>
