        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>AVESLEYAN ENTERPRISE. Thursday's visit of the Rev. J E Rattenbuty to 80ld-street Chapel set me thinking of many things.How- !ever regarded, that well-known lecturer may be said ,Itt a very real sense to be in the apostolical succession 0 begin with, is he not a son of the manse? Then 8h55 entered into the labours of the late Rev. Hugh .ryrice Hughes in connection with the great West London Mission. Few but very young Methodists need !t? be told that ?rice Hughes organised and established !Ilat, extraordinary religious work at St. James' Hall, conjunction with the Rev. Mark Guy Pearse, and that in the meantime several other successful missions Ilaye been started on similar lines. Mr Rattenbury,as !3uperintsudent of the West Loudon Mission, upholds Its traditions right worthily. He has some wonder- Stories to tell. Loyal Methodists will take care that that excellent work of which he has charge shall ttint permanently suffer from lack of funds. 41RJSING ALLITERATI 'N. Bootless Baronet Bravely Battles with Bold Bad get!" In some such alliterative fashion did Mr ;raltael Peace head his contribution to these columns week 4 ago. It reminded me of the performer upon b. • wind instrument who wrote seven B's after fits "arne, and on being asked what the letters stood for rniPtly explained. "Best Bloomin' Blower in easeS o' th' Barn Brass Band !" GENTLE HINT. Last week's titbit concerning the superior young enrate brought to mind the rather severe definition :bleb postulates three sexes—masculine, feminine and orates. By-the-bye may one without impertinence ffer a few remarks upon a subject respecting the ;lerical profession which presses more and more? 14.,nrely it is not unreasonable that indignant I:,,cantrientatorB are beginning to insist that elocution .md form an integral part of the teaching it) every l„ "egical college. One has often heard people 5-(49elaln of the dry and lifeless reading of the tkenetures and of the monotonous delivery of sermons, (as Mr J Herbert Lewis, M.P., observed in a ' cvßenb in Wales a while ago) such people should rti!inlember that all men are not equally gifted. Powers ih„at do not belong to them by nature can be acquired !ter art—that is the point. :'''-'ERGYMAN'S SORE THROAT. if The same authority insists that it is not necessary I er anyone to suffer from this digressing and some- at prevalent complaint. He admits that he did so te'rmerly, but declares that a few shouting lessons ilted him of 't so that he can now "bawl for tof au hour without in any way straining the chords throat." I have noted that barristers are not I:ll°.iect to the malady as preachers are, and I find the 'tsnation in the fact that whereas the clergy address tu eirth Congregations from a pulpit and thus talk down e lthe . counsel while speaking in Court looks up to Judge, so that the strain upon the vocal chords is ,et7tParatively slight. t IPA-TES WANTED. 'here are two occupations, one for each sex, which Sr never overcrowded curates and domestic servants ii:ee always in demand. I know of a vicar who did not .4e,ei,ve more than one reply a week to an advertise- in a Church newspaper stating that he wanted a Cate, but when, after two months, the advertisement an error appeared in the wrong column, he had a yilludred replies from clergymen all over the country e`Twere also in need of a curate and thought he was tAe,,l'ic,44 a curacy. L3EF UL TREE. '„ 'ecotinuts, so long employed for the purposes of the 14/.Bi;ular " shy " at fair time, are nowadays largely all 8d in a way which I do not find indicated in the s,„, nut exhaustive little article printed in the I:l:t.thteirldren's Corner" last week. They enter very [nuneiderably into the manufacture of margarine. This substitute, which used to be made of foreign so that the ingredients were always problematical, tiltintew everywhere advertised as consisting. wholly of sand milk. No doubt when accompanied by the rtant y (1 a reliable firm, it is quite wholesome. time ago I was imposed upon with a sample, leo`eg led to suppose it was genuine butter until I bad lfairinned it. Since then I have eaten lots of it, and !th to detect any difference between the margarine and cAe article initiated. AND MOLER. h 'ne lines " Winter partners," likewise introduced ilia: Uncle Caleb," reminded me of another thing. I !tid out walking in the country, and called at a way- ij re Public house in connection with which was a farm aitl. ere.mbereJ that one field in particular was formerly ufested vvith moles, which were so troublesome that ',rood Price was set upon them and some of the abi urers thereabout occasionally made quite respect- ?. ° enma bY catching the creatures and claiming the t ae owl ta sr d a. n d therefore asked the landlord about the e told me a singular story by way of Iletheetltunting for their di-appearance for it was a fact had moles were no longer seen in the field where they to a been 50 "Innleu and such a nuisance. Pointing lt Young cat on the hearth, he stated that when, as a i‘te_t,en, it was introduced into the household, it almost ediately began to develop an interest in the moles ,bne'cin it took to killing them, invariably bringing the ite,.l,eB into the kitchen as if conscious that it was ,aeltil9ering appreciable service and was proud of its baelevernents. It would Sit for hours near their 'IN and P patiently await opportunity to destroy the ests s : anu tiLslls went on until the field was entirely ,eleared. 'CONCERNING A PROVERB. from time to time I have followed with great ' tate.rest the antiquarian researches of that most pains- itbk,,lng of " Examiner " contributors "A. M.", and current series on nursery rhymes appeal to me I lwteaalY. About a quarter of a century ago, when l 4 Leditor of the now defunct "Halifax Free Press, Athelburt Binns, who resided in the neigh- nod of Keighley, wrote for me on somewhat S o ;Or lines, and I have an old work dealing with it, know some little of the subject 'upon which ,it is now shedding fresh light. This by the way; not on that account that I began this paragraph. irenlasbire has an old proverb which is popularly ,obaeered " Where there's muck there's money.' I Iravry,e that in proposing a toast at the Commercial 'ther„eiders Dinner, Mr. P. O'Brien quoted " Where • is smoke there's money being made." The version v me, but it strikes me that the saying would :the'''nadY be in some such terms, and that " Where !ernorke:s„ muck " is a corruption " Where there's kit,h(:i.rleSeTeß recent public FOR LYME. public meeting under the auspices of kap° rnrn Free Church Council, the hon. secretary !bad ir3l'ed that " a registrar of marriages for Lymm :have be that appointed as a result of their efforts." I thto confess that, probably owing to my ignorance :Puzzle, local conditions. I have been not a little Any eu bY this. In all other districts of which I have ,t 0 th"Perience the functionary referred to has been tiafe? Nonconformist as a red rag to a bull, and they theirB4edlY rejoiced when, after years of agitation on Ithenallrt, the law made it possible for their ministers Pla'ves to register marriages at their respective of the '3,f worship and so dispense with the attendance 'Ns ti; enn,.xious official whose presence (even when he th ere te time) was at once an offensive reminder Of ti? invidious distinction made between the clergy sent. e Established Church and the ministers of Dis- bqt Leg communities. I daresay Lymm is all right, ps at present informed I cannot understand why Ito rethre (1 r.ee Church friends thete should so strive for and elightedl • welcome an appointment which their O :tsteci elsewhere have all along so strongly depre- Ilike i,ibeY will probably laugh at my foolishness,but, Dartle, I want to know you know; and when .that n_myself in that case I frankly avow it in order 1411;:rnii0rnay take pity and lighten my darkness. ‘-'‘-rDSTINE BIRRELL. sta, St week's issue had a note on this author and ,(eatn who n• shortly to speak in Warrington, and th-Y add somewhat thereunto. I recollect a writer r55.,,8 " Daily Chronicle" thus describing the then 1 t„ient of the Board of Education ;—" Mr Birrell Wi'Q iLwllat might be called an' Early 'Victorian ' face - fa:llroad broad brow, square head., ;haven lips, a keen 01%ms mouth, ,aavrintginwifthvoice. He wears an old- ed black r hardly visible collar, rsil°l adds to this effect. Looking at him to-day, one Ritheteha.ve thought him a figure from three-quarter, -,,,vit,ntury ago. And a commanding figure at that more to the like effect. The subject has said in the House of Commons: 41147_111 a Nonconformist, born and bred, a man 4ted in Nonconformist history and Nonconformist</p>
        <p>traditions, one who might al most be described as having been born in a Nonconformist library! " A glance at a reference book gives the date of the birth as January 19th, 1850, revealing that the right hon. gentleman was born near Liverpo A, the youngest son of the Rev Charles Birrell, pastor of Pembroke Chapel, and grandson of a prominent IN on- conformist; and that he was educated at Arnersham Hall School, near Reading. THE PARALLEL WITH " ELIA."</p>
        <p>To take up another point raised in last week's para- graph, it has been truly remarked that the student cannot read far into cue of his essays without coming to the conclusion that he has much in common with Charles Lamb; above all, what has been well described as " a sense of humorous gravity." A critic, reviewing his "Miscellanies," neatly summed up his individuality by saying : "Mt Birrell's charm lit sin his splendid sanity, his geniality, his sense of humour, his love of common humanity, and his fine scorn of cant" In ' Truth hunting " he writes of his beloved Charles Lamb: "He lived his life cracking his little jokes and reading his great folios, neither wrangling with nor accepting the opinions of the friends he loved ti-) see around him. To a contemporary stranger it might well have appeared as if his I,fe were a frivolous and useless one as com- pared with those of these philosophers and thinkers. They discussed their great schemes and affected to probe deep mystei ies, and were constantly asking: What is Truth ?' Ile sipped his glass, shuffled his cards, and was content with the humbler inquiry: What are trumps ? ' " BIRRELLISMS.</p>
        <p>As we were reminded in last week's reference, the author of "Obita Dicta" .whom we shall presently have the pleasure of bearing in Warrington, has added at least one new word to the dictionary. " Historians ! " he ejaculates in a most serious study on an historical subject. "Their name is Perfidy! Unless they have good styles they are so bard to read, and if they have good styles they are so apt to lie! " That comments a fellow-scribe,is a piquant `:Birrellism."No wit could be less artificial than such wit; though in his younger days Mr Birrell has confessed to having been con- sumed with a desire to be witty. This was in order that he might amuse his audiences, not bore them ; and the desire sprang from pity. "I no longer pity my audiences," he declares. "I punish them !" 83 Warrington now knows what to expect. ROUGH ON ACTORS.</p>
        <p>It is notorious that Mr Augustine Birrell is peculiar in his utter .lack of sympathy with actors. It has been pointed but that of actors indeed, he has written quite scathingly—for a man of his genial nature. He speaks of "the notorious bad taste of actors" and of " the inherent pettiness of the actor's art," and notes, in his whimsical way, as an objection to the actor's call- ing, that no record of his art survives to tell his tale : " When old gentlemen wax garrulous over actors dead and gone,young geptlemengro w somnolent Chippendale the cabinet-maker is more potent than Garrick the actor. The vivacity of the actor no longer charms (save in Boswell) ; the chairs of the former still render rest imp )ssible in a hundred homes." Warrington has of late years been visited by many distinguished authors, lecturers and politicians, and most of us will count it a privilege to be able to add Mr. Birrell to the list.</p>
