      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>p e•'•"••••"-r\dv‘r‘fteNe•'‘e,•%/ iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. Ne-,‘"•.........•,-.</head>
        <p>p e•'•"••••"-r\dv‘r‘fteNe•'‘e,•%/ iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. Ne-,‘"•.........•,-.</p>
        <p>SPIDERS' NESTS AS FLYTRAPS.</p>
        <p>In Mexico, in certain localities, the people suspend in their apartments nests of sociable spiders taken from oak trees, to which the flies come and get taught, thus ridding the houses of disagreeable guests. These nests, which the natives call mosqueros, vary in size. They sometimes cover a space of six feet. The exterior of the nest is composed of an envelope of two kinds of thread. One somewhat stronger than the other and extending from one branch to another, constitutes the cordages which maintain the edifice. The other threads, softer and glutinous, serve to capture the prey. The interior of this pocket is filled with a network of threads inextricably entangled. Among them numerous cells and galleries are skilfully disturbed, which give the mass a spongy appearance. Each year as the rainy season approaches, the Indians go into the wooded mountain region to gather more mosqueros and bring them back to their homes. They suspend them to the ceilings of their rooms where from that time the spiders continue to live as if they were at home. They place these nests not only in the rooms which they occupy but in their barns and stables as well. A curious thing is that the nests seem to exercise an irresistible attraction to winged insects, which is owing perhaps to their colour or some peculiar odour that our sense of smell is not sufficiently subtle to perceive. The mosquera grows larger concentrically, so to speak, after each capture; when an insect comes and gets caught he is immedi- ately seized and covered in a cell by the spider who makes him his prey and is finally devoured, leaving an empty place which becomes a new cell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>SOME UGLY LITTLE flips.</head>
        <p>SOME UGLY LITTLE flips.</p>
        <p>If you don't believe in fairies, and the elves are not And you your have nfrojefnd aitsh, ii brownies or in gnomes, Let me give you just a glimpse Of the ugly little Imps That invade to-day so many happy homes. Impoliteness is an Imp whom every child should try to shun, And older people, too, without a doubt, Impatience is another Who will cause you lots of bother 'Lest you send him quickly to the right-about. IMPertinence and IMPudence are naughty little twins And, oh, it is astonishing to see he mischief that they do! And, my dear, if I were you, Their comrade I would never, never be. One little IMP will sit astride a pencil or a pen When'er there is a problem hard in view, And draw his mouth 'way down, And whine out with a frown : IMPossible ! IMPossible to do !"</p>
        <p>IMPudence and 111 Penitence and IMPulae are three more</p>
        <p>(Though the latter is not always under ban) ; And there are more, no doubt, Who are hovering about To get us into mischief if they can. Of little foxes you have heard, who spoil the lovely vines. These ugly IMPS are dangerous, toa, you see. Let us raise a battle shout ! We may put them all to rout Oh, what glorious victory that would be ! THIS WEEK'S STORY. OVERHEARD IN THE FISH SHOP. " 'Ere, mister, this lobster's only got one claw."</p>
        <p>"I'm sorry, sir, but you know lobsters fight one another, and I expect this one lost in the fray." "Well, take it away, and bring me the winner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>GOLF LINKS ON THg L. (t;', N.-W. R.</head>
        <p>GOLF LINKS ON THg L. (t;', N.-W. R.</p>
        <p>In response to a large number of inquiries on the subject, an interesting booklet has been issued by tbo London and North-Western Railway Company giving particulars of the Golf Links on or near their system, with secretaries' names, fees, ttc. These include some of the most sporting links in the Country. Exceptional facilities are now offered to golfers, and tickets at a single fare and a third for the double journey (minimum is.) are issued to individual golfers traveLling to a golf link for the purpose of playing golf, on surrender at the booking office of a voncaer on approve.' form signed by the Secretary of the Golf Club. Full particulars may be obtained at any of the Company's Agencies, Town Offices, or the Enquiry Office, Evaiton Station, N•W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>The, Examiner, Saturday, February 1, 1913.</p>
        <p>iANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS.'</p>
        <p>If we quote the Welsh song we shall see that it has the same features as the others. By your leave we will sing Concerning our king Our king is well drest In silk of the best In his coach he does ride With a great deal of pride And with four footmen To wait upon him We have travelled many miles Over hedges and stiles To find you this king Which we now to you bring. Another wren-song which hails from Carmarthenshire is a kind of dialogue song and insists that the hunt shall be carried out in the ancient way. It is too interesting to be omitted:</p>
        <p>0, where are you going says Milder to Malder 0, I cannot tell, says Festal to Fose, We're going to the woods, says John the Red Nose 0 what will you do there says Milder to Malder We'll shout the cutty wren, says John the Red Nose 0, how will you shoot her With cannons and guns? 0, that will not do With arrows and bows 0, how will you cut her up With knives and forks ? 0, that will not do With hatchets and cleavers. 0, how will you boil her In kettles and pots ? 0, that will not do In cauldrons and pans</p>
        <p>0, who'll have the spare ribs ? 0, Icannot tell,</p>
        <p>We'll give them to the poor, says John the Red Nose. AN ENGLISH SURVIVAL. Let us now see what we have in England and Scotland that belongs to wren-hunting. The English nursery rhyme is very old but there is no printed version dating earlier than about 1740.</p>
        <p>We will go to the wood says Robbin to Bobbin, We will go to the wood says everyone We will shoot at a wren says Robbin to Bobbin, We will shoot at a wren says everyone, She's down, she's down says Robbin to Bobbin How shall we get her home says Bobbin to Robbin We will hire a cart says Robbm to Bobbin Then hoist, hoist, says Bobbin to Bobbin She's up, she's up, says everyone So they brought her away After each plucked a feather And when they got home Shared the booty together.</p>
        <p>The Scotch rhyme is so much like it that it need not be quoted. The wren is "slayed," carried home "in a cart" and goes into the house by " pulling down the door cheeks." It ends.</p>
        <p>I'll hae a wing, quo' Fozie Mozie, I'll hae anither, quo' Johnnie liednosie I'll hae a leg, quo' Foslin 'ene, And I'll hae another quo' brither and kin.</p>
        <p>Putting these ditties together and taking careful account of the wording and allusions we shall find much to engage our interest. In the first place they prove that the old ceremony of hunting the wren included the cutting up of the body and the eating of the portions. So tiny a bird as the wren would never be hunted for its food value and we cannot come to any other con- clusion than that the bird was cut up in a sacrificial manner every part being valued and even the feathers possessing curious powers. Other allusions show that the wren was hunted by organized bands of hunters. They kill him with bow and arrow or sticks and stones; then he is put upon a cart and carried home ; he is boiled in the brewery pan ; the king and queen are said to have the best parts, poor people have the giblets and the feathers are divided.</p>
        <p>The Carmarthenshire version is specially interesting as containing a protest that the programme should be carried out in the old way and that a cart should be used and bow and arrows as of old.</p>
        <p>All these arrangements are an absurdity as applied to a tiny bird of only an ounce or two's weight, but they are very interesting as handing down to us the old traditions of wren-hunting and as reflecting in a crude form habits and beliefs that were once realities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>"WHO KILLED COCK ROBIN ?"</head>
        <p>"WHO KILLED COCK ROBIN ?"</p>
        <p>A CHAPTER UPON A STRANGE SURVIVAL.</p>
        <p>Visitors to Egypt, who examine the stone carvings at Abydos come upon a familiar scene as they enter one of the side chapels of the temple of King Seti. Chiselled upon the wallis a scene that might easily serve as an illustration of the dirge of Cock Robin. A dead bird lies upon a bier; around it are sorrowing birds which look like hawks, whilst one !which is on the wing appears to be carrying down a seal amulet. These carv- ings at Abydos are about 3,300 years old. So early in the world's history did people tell stories about martyred birds over whom "the birds of the air fell to sighing and to sobbing." Can this, then, be our old lamented friend Cock Robin, and did they thus early sing his dirge?</p>
        <p>We hardly think so; but if we can get a clue to the bird at Abydos over whom his friends are weeping we may have a clue to the much-bemoaned Cock Robin of a later day.</p>
        <p>Our inquiry will have to proceed upon general lines and we may not get very con- vincing results; but ancient bird-lore is very interesting and won't disappoint us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>BIRD AND ANIMAL SACRIFICES.</head>
        <p>BIRD AND ANIMAL SACRIFICES.</p>
        <p>There can be no doubt that many nursery pieces refer to bird sacrifices and cont-iin echoes of the ritual with which they were sacrificed.</p>
        <p>In very early times certain animals and birds were held sacred, just as the cow is held sacred in India to-day. These were held in perfect veneration and it was considered an unlucky and even dangerous thing to do them harm.</p>
        <p>Yet periodically one of the sacred creatures was killed as a sacrifice with elaborate ritual and a solemn service. The subject has been discussed at great length by our antiquarians, particularly by Professor J. G. Frazer, and it has many attractive features. According to the learned Professor, our early ancestors believed the spirit of the sacrificed bird or animal to become incarnate in other creatures of its kind. They seem to have regarded the animal as a representative ot a certain clan and in killing it together they believed that the sacrifice constituted a bond between a number of kinsmen. _ _</p>
        <p>The ancient usage must be mentioned here because it gives us our starting point in a discussion of the curious attitude our ancestors took towards certain birds. We are specially concerned just here with the wren. This little bird was singled out as sacred not only in England but throughout Western Europe. Possibly the little golden crest worn by one kind of wren gave it dis- tinction. That it was held in respect can be discovered from its names. In Latin it was rex avium, (king of birds), in Greek Basilikos, in French the wren is Roitelet. In Spain, Italy and Germany too, the wren has a name which means king or little king.</p>
        <p>In— later ages some curiosity was felt that so small a bird as the wren should be called the king and so the story was invented which told that the birds challenged one another as to which could fly nearest the sun, that the wren concealed himself among the eagle's feathers and managed when the eagle was exhausted to fly a few inches higher. But the true explanation is that the wren was held sacred by early man and was called king in a very remote past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MODERN WREN SACRIFICE.</head>
        <p>MODERN WREN SACRIFICE.</p>
        <p>Let us now take note of the fact that the custom of sacrificing the wren is still observed. In early days, long before the dawn of history, it was sacrificed for the reasons we have briefly giver. In our own day it is sacrificed in circumstances of peculiar cruelty in Ireland, France and the Isle of Man. Happily, the custom appears to have completely vanished from England. In the Isle of Man it takes place on December 27th. This date marked, according to the old reckoning, the beginning of the New Year. The people used to observe a watch-night in the Church and then pass out to hunt the wren. When a bird was secured it was fastened to a long pole with its wings drawn out wide, and was carried through the lanes to the song,</p>
        <p>"We hunted the wren for Robbin the Bobbin."</p>
        <p>The chant described that the bird was hunted with sticks and stones, that a cart was hired, that he was boiled in a brewery pan, eaten by the king and queen, and the pluck given to the poor. Such was the song. The usage was to carry the bird round the parish and then lay it upon a bier in the churchyard. Next came a funeral of great solemnity with dirges in the Manx language called "The knell of the wren." The concluding scene was a dance in which the people joined hands and danced in a ring to music.</p>
        <p>Fifty years or so ago it ceased to be a ceremony for up-grown people, but fell into the hands of the boys. These killed a wren and carried it from door to door suspended by its legs in the centre of two hoops. The hoops were decorated with ribbons and the boys sang the old dirge. A feather plucked from the wren was left at each house in exchange for a piece of money. The old superstition of the sacredness of the wren still lingers in Manxland in the belief that the feathers of the sacrificed bird possess a charm and will preserve the owner from shipwreck for a whole year. The wren is no longer buried in the graveyard but upon the sea shore or waste land.</p>
        <p>The Manx people have lost all knowledge of the meaning of the wren hunt, but have invented a legend to explain it. II is to the effect that a fairy of great beauty acted the part of Syren to the men of the island,leading them by her sweet voice to follow her until she had drawn them into the sea. At last some soldier arose to destroy her but she escaped by taking the form of a wren. They go out on New Year's morning in the hope of settling accounts with the bad fairy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WREN SACRIFICE IN IRELAND.</head>
        <p>WREN SACRIFICE IN IRELAND.</p>
        <p>In Ireland wren hunting was common until the last century. It still survives in Leinster and Connaught. Here also the bird is carried round hung in hoops to the song. The wren, the wren the king of all birds, Was caught St. Stephen's day in the furze Altho' he's little his family's great, Then pray, gentlefolk, give him a treat.</p>
        <p>This verse is useful to us in our enquiry as retaining the old belief that the wren was a royal bird.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WELSH RITUAL.</head>
        <p>THE WELSH RITUAL.</p>
        <p>In Wales the custom, we think, is now abandoned, but up to quite recently in Pembrokeshire several wrens were carried round in a cage on January 6th (the old Twelfth day) from house to house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>BIRD SACRIFICE IN FRANCE.</head>
        <p>BIRD SACRIFICE IN FRANCE.</p>
        <p>Further light COMI3S to us as we cross the Channel and study the rhyme of France. Here, too, wren-hunting was practised through centuries, but we have not been able to ascertain whether it is still followed. They used to catch a bird on Christmas Eve and take it to the priest who set it free in the Church. The people vied with each other to catch the first wren, and if the fortunate one was a woman this gave the sex the right to jeer at the men and smear their faces with mud.</p>
        <p>In other places the lucky man or woman was hailed king and carried round shoulder high. On Twelfth Day he attended Church in mock state and called on the Mayor and Bishop.</p>
        <p>They have also in France a song that describes the plucking and dividing of the bird. It goes over the ceremony showing that they began at the left eye and travelled over the body by an orderly plan until the last feather was taken from the tail. Yet all the time the bird is said to keep on singing, a detail that reflects the old belief that the bird was not really dead, but that his spirit passed into the rest. A Breton poem gives another piece of infor- mation. It tells how the wren was kept in a cage and fattened until the day when the butcher came and sacrificed it to an accom- paniment of ritual and then revelry. In this realm of bird sacrifices we also meet our friend Cock Robin. But he figures in so many aspects that we must make him the subject of a special chapter. A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>(To be Continued.)</head>
        <p>(To be Continued.)</p>
        <p>PIMPLES SPREAD ON FACE.</p>
        <p>20, Frederick St., Bradford, Yorks, Eng.—" Some two years ago I broke out down the side of my face with some pimples. They got worse and they spread down the side of my face. I could not sleep at night for itching and scratching, and also got sl I would not go out as I was a very bad sight to look at. It spread to my head and the hair began to fall off my head. I was told it was eczema. I commenced to try some salves and ointment but found that they did not do me any good, but I got worse. "I happened to see an advertisement about Cuticura Soap and Ointment so I thought that I would try them. Before I had used one set I was nearly cured. The secoud set my eczema was gone and my skin began to heal and my hair began to' grow again. After using four sets of Cuticura Soap and Ointment lam perfectly cured and my skin is as smooth and clear as before I had any skin disease." (Signed) Fred Imeson, June 5, 1912. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p book post-free from nearest dept: Newbery, 27, Charterhouse Sq., London; It. Towns I Co., Sydney, N.S.W ; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; ' Muller, Maclean and Co, Calcutta and Bombay; "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
        <p>THE NEW ZEALAND OIL INDUSTRY. Good progress is being made at tic Taranaki Oil Well, Ltd., works. The refinery buildings are in course of erection, and the plant is coming to band. Tee derrick for the first rotary drill has been completed, and the plant mostly on the ground. The railway i siding into the works s well under way. Three yield- ing wells are all flowing freelyt and the bore at Bell Block is now down 110 feet. ?reparations are well advanced for starting a further bore in that locality, and bricklayers are badly wanted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
