      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>" BUkNS' NICHT."</head>
        <p>" BUkNS' NICHT."</p>
        <p>THE REV. W. REID'S STRIKING TRIBUTE TO THE POET.</p>
        <p>"It's guid to be merry and wise, It's guid to be honest and true, It's guid to support Caledonia's cause, And bide by the buff and the blue." _ _</p>
        <p>These lines from Burns' "Here's a health to them that's awa' " could be very appropriately applied to the gathering of local Caledonians and " freens " at the Lion Hotel on Saturday evening to celebrate, in common with canny Scots the world o'er, the anniversary of " Bobbie " Burns' birthday. The festivities, which took the form of a dinner and concert, were conducted in a congenial atmosphere, real Scottish wit and humour prevailing throughout the whole proceedings. A great deal was said about " the land o' cakes" and about its sons being more successful than Englishmen in various walks of life. Those successes, according to Councillor J T Locker, who proposed the toast of "The Warrington Association," were due to the splendid education which Scotsmen received. The numerous other toasts were ably submitted, that of" The Immortal memory of Burns" being entrusted to the Rev W Reid who in the course of an eloquent tribute said that Scotland produced Burns, Scotland claims Burns but ScAland cannot hold him ; the must give him to the world. As the guests assembled, Piper Stoddard, of Seacombe played various Scottish airs on the bag-pipes. The dinner, which was an excellent one, was well served. Ile traditional customs were observed in the serving of the haggis, characterised by Burns as the "great chieftain o' the puddin' race." This was brought in with all the ceremony due to it, Piper Stoddard heading a pro cession of waitresses and waiters, each carrying aloft a dish of the pudding. The president (Mr J Fulton Smith), presided and amongst, those present were : —The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph), Rev W Reid 2 Councillor Locker, the Chief Constable (Mr Martin Nicholls), Chief Inspector Williamson, Messrs J C Copplck, G Bowman, W Skinner and J Tennant (hon. secretary). Dinner over, the Chairman read the following rgreeting from the Manchester and Salford Caledonian Agacciation :</p>
        <p>hearts alowe, we're burnin' now, To join the mighty chorus ; To dower Rah's lays is boon a' praise— His spell is hangin' o'er us. Free out the past, borne on the blast, A sang we'll never tine sir, Says you an' me shall brithers be, In mem'ry o' lang syne sir. The loyal toaAts having been submitted by the President and duly honoured, the Rev \V Item pro- posea the principal toast of the evening, " The immortal memory of Burns." In the course of an eloquent tribute he slid there were many thoughts in his mind in regard to Burns, hut the one that arose readiest to the surface was the environ- ment of the poet and the influence it had on his life Carlyle had said that Burns was fortunate in his father, characterising him As one of those strong,indeJendent sturdy type of men, of thoughtful and intense char- acter and a man of real practical integrity ' • a man who valued knowledge and kept an open mind for all that came his way, and who wa3 friendly and tearless." One could not forget Betty Davidson' with her wealth of tale', ballads and songs. Burns had his books about him, not many, just a few choice ones—Shake- speare, Pope, Milton, Ferguson, Ramsay and the theological works of his days, such as might have hen feeund on the shelves of the average cotter, and the Bible ; that, must not be forgotten These boeks were few in number, but B erns's after-writing- showed that they hal been well conned, and the influence of the home of Burns went with him through life. That was seen in his response in later ye r's to the cotter's "Let us worship God," in which he desyribed the " cheerfu' supper," " the patriarchal sire," and "the big ha' Bible." They had the matter summed up in a nutshell in the following lines From scenes like these old Scotia's grandeur springs, That makes her lov'd at home, revered abroad. There was no doubt that Burns carried with him his father's God, and that he never lost his faith in him was seen in the words : An atheist's laugh's a poor exchange For Deity offended. Of course he bad other books—human nature, open fields, &amp;c.—and these he read not letts intelligently than the volumes he had about him, and as he went further into life one was struck with the fact that he realized what every man could if he chose, namely, that he had a mission in life."o that I for puir aid' Scotland's sake some useful plan or brick could make or sing a song at least." One could read the progressive stages of the poet's life in the words he dashed off with such con- summate skill from time to time, and there was no doubt that he was given to his country in a day when Scot- land's harp was neglected and somewhat broken. It was his privilege to take the harp of the country and re-string it, and as his sympathetic fingers played over its strings, and with his soul ablaze, with consummate skill he sang the hopes, the fears, the loves, the joys, the aspirations and the ideals of his people. Ile drew inspiration from his native mountains, valleys, fields, trees and rivers. He sang to his people in their own tongue. In regard to the complaint of many people that they could not understand the language which Burns used, the rev gentleman said that it was suggested by some that the English language should be enriched by some of these Scotticisms and choice inspirations. It was certainly dull work to those who had to get a glossary and keep it by their side and have to turn over the pages through the alphabet in order to find an explanation of words met with in reading Burns. The works would be emasculated if they attempted to do away with the original language. It was a language, not a dialect, and if -they attempted to rob it of that they would take away its vitality, its life and fervour. Burns's genius and his tenderness came into view as he took the little things, the every-day and commonplace things, and transformed them—the sheep, daisy and the mouse, even turning aside his ploughshear to avoid cutting the thistle down. Away at the back of it all, peering through it all, one could see evidence of a great breadth of mind that ran through, actuated, and dominated the soul and being and all the exercises of the man's life. Burns was not an ecclesiastical reformer, minding creeds only when he saw something more than their; uselessness; namely their danger. Scotland was being scorched by ultra-Calvinism. It might be urged that his work was destructive only and, pbrhaps, not without reason, had he written neither more nor less than "The Holy Fair," "The Kirk's Alarm," "The Twa Herds," e&amp;c., each of which made instructive reading though not drawing-room literature, but a decidedly constructive policy was shown, as he said : " God knows I'm no' the thing I would be, Nor am I even the thing I could be, But twenty Hines I rather would be An atheist clean, Than under gospel colours hid, Be jest for a screen. The heart ay's the part, ay, That mak's us right or wrong." All this because Burns saw not in the Christian faith what he saw around him—cant, hypocrisy, tyranny, wrangling and bitterness—but kindliness goodness, justice, righteousness and love. Dr Craik ha"cl said "The generous verse of Burns springs out of the iron-bound Calvinism of the land like liovving water from Horeb's rock." Yet the truth that ultra-Calvinism was mis- interpreted, and whist which misinterpretation Burns directed his energies of sarcasm and wrath, wd very plainly seen in Burns himself. Burns was noble even in death, and the very ruins of his life collosal. With what would be called the war spirit to-day Burns had no sympathy. True, he wrote a martial song, which, if he had penned naught else, would suffice to keep his memory for ever fresh. But his was the charter song of freedom. He sang in defence of the common heritage of humanity, and in giving to</p>
        <p>cotland " sc,,ts mina. nee'b. gave to tne peJines of 'Le world their rignt of liberty. With him it was liberty or death. (Applause.) Scotland produced Burns. icotland claimed Burns, But Scotland could not keep .irn. She must give him to the xiorld. In the day hen irreligion and atheism was doing its best to flood he world, and when Scotland, in her recoil against base things, was pinning her faith to a human creed that threatened to separate and alienate her from faith in mankind, Burns in his God-inspired way, converted ier into the vestibule of the realm of universal brother- nood.</p>
        <p>Then let us pray and come it may, As come it will for a' that ; That man to man the wand o'er, Shall brothers be for a' that. The "Lament" was played by Piper Stoddard and the toast was drunk in silence. Councillor J. T. LOCKER proposed the toast of "The Warrington Association." He said it was an o:ganisa- tion whose main objects were charitableness and sociability. Those were the characteristics of a race of which they had need to he proud. Referring to the excellent president they had in Mr Fulton Smith, he said that gentleman had rendered valuable service to the town in connection with municipal affairs, and he (the speaker) who had been proud to ha associated with him on the Council, felt that the town of Warrington lost a great help-mate when Mr. Smith decided to withdraw from that side of public life. (Hear, hear.) He, how- ever, still took a generous interest in most of the local associations. Continuing, the speaker said that in most instances one found Scotsmen 2.t the head of the com- mercial concerns with which they were connected. That imprssion had been confirmed by his long general experience in calling upon great commercial concerns. They found that Scotsmen had made greater progress than any other nation with such e small and limited population, as compared with other parts of the empire, and they had great reason to be proud of their advc.nce. They had been most prominent in the race of Anglo- Saxons in their emigration. They had colonised and were holding important positions in the United States, all of which in his opinion was due to the splendid educa- tion which was, and had been given to Scotch people. In the colonies they found men of culture, who had risen from ordinary homes in Scotland, and were now rendering service to the Empire and more particularly to the homeland, which was almost priceless. In his study be had come to the conclusion that it was the educational system of Scotland which had made them pre-eminent in those particular positions. He hoped it would be the fortune of our British legislators to give to this England of ours a system which would be equally useful to the younger generation. If we could only get the sentiments and inspiration in our youngsteis which appeared to exist in young Scottish people, then we should have a race which would emulate the best achievements of the past. (Applause.) The Me-vort (Councillor Dr Joseph), in responding said some of them might be puzzled to know under what pretext he could come forward as a champion of so great a country, which had produced so great a man as Robert Burns. He though that, to a certain extent, he was on his defence in that case. his mother was Scotch,born in'Scotland of Scottish parents.(Applause) Relating a story, his Worship said that in the latter part of the 18th century that state of education which Councillor Locker had spoken of had not arrived He also mentioned the fact that the late Professor Alexander Murray was his maternal great great uncle. The professor had worked himself up from a shepherd boy and was appointed at Edinburgh because of his being practically the only one who could transcribe a letter written in Abysinnian to King George 111., which was of great diplomatic importance. Continuing his Worship said he thought that Councillor Locker had spoken so well and, he believed, so truthfolly of Scotland,that he (the Mayar) was entitled to say some- thing to make Scotsmen worthy of the observatioLs he had maue. It was a well-known fact that the countries which were barren or unfertile and whose natural resources were small, produced the quickest-witted men and women. It seemeo that where the natural resources were very peat', it tended to ignorance as regarded the mind and body. That was the reason he thought, why dm Sc sen were so successful in contesting with their fellow men in other parts of the world. There was no doubt about it that another thing in favour of Scotland was that its people were not only fond of acquiring knowledge but had a great admiration for b-arning. l'hat was a powerful reason for their advance- ment. It was said that the Sc itch were fend of mohey (Laughter ) He did not think that characteristic could be applied especially to Scotsmen. (Laughter). It might be that Scotsmen took more care of their money than others, but he thought that was also a good quality, so long as they were not too niggardly and made ordinarily gcod use of their money. Applause). Mr W SKINNER in proposing the toast ot "Our Guests" said there were not a large number of Scots- men in Warrington but the society had greatly extended its membersh:p until they now had nearly NO. (Applause). Mr J C COPPOCK replied. In one respect the Scots were a much-maligned race, for they were the most humorous people in the whole world. A toast, which was submitted in humorous and felicitous terms was that of " The Lasses "proposed by Mr P H BROWN, who said he thought they would all agree with him that the lasses to a great extent dominated and directed Burns' life. (Hear, hear.) Ladies had come to make themselves a felt power in the world. Men regulated their everyday life accord- ing to the ladies. Their comings and goings were directed by them and, if it were not for the ladies, life would be an aching void, it would be dull and insipid— a miserable existence. (Applause). Mr W WEBSTER responded. Referring to the suffragettes, he aaid he crid not think the majority of women were anxious to enter Parliament. He thought, however, that women should have the Parliamentary vote on the same terms as they already exercised it at municipal elections.lf a woman was a housekeeper and paid rates she ought to have the franchise. (Hear, hear). The toast of "The Artistes" was honoured on the propoposition of Mr %V MufuLav, Mr MCARTHUR responding. In the absence of the Vice-President, Mr J Davenport, Mr MARTIN NICHOLLS (Chief Constable) proposed the toast of" The President," remarking at the outset that the only association he had had with Scotland was when he once served with a number of Scotsmen and they were spoken of locally as "foreigners." (Laughter.) He believed Mr Smith had been the President for something like seven or eight years and it was in a large degree due to him tbat the association held such an important position in Mining- ton. On one occasion a Scotsman in Warrington was desirous of going to his native soil, as he said, "to die." His fare was paid, at the instance of members of the Caledonian Association, but it was not long before be was back again and in the local " Palace of Justice." (Laughter). In his reply Mr SMITH said that they as an Associa- tion had set out to try and help those who were not very well off when they came to England, but he was glad to say the calls on the secretary were very small indeed. (Applause.) The artistes who contributed to an excellent, musical programme were : Mrs U Miller, Miss Lockerbie (violinist), Messrs J McArthur, R Mitchell, James Robertson, J Clayton, and G Marshall. Mr Torn Ford was an accomplised accorcpanist. In true Scottish fashion all the members arranged themselves round the room and,each clasping the hand of a " freen, "sang " Auld Lang S 3 ne."</p>
      </div>
