      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>LALL RIOdIS RESERVED. 3</p>
        <p>DELL.</p>
        <p>BY HELEN MATHERS,</p>
        <p>author of " Comin' Thro' the Rye," "Love ani Fortune," "Cherry Ripe," " The Story of Sin," "Blind Justice," "Found .Out," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>nd conduct than in any book on etiquette ever written; and there's a remark about a fair woman; without decorum being like a. jewel ol gold in a pig's snout that you will do well tc consider."</p>
        <p>"Oh ! you mean that rubbish about- our going with uncovered heads to church," she said, lightly; "as if the parson oughtn't to be do Lighted to see us there at all! Besides, that text in the Corinthians applied to Orientals—not us. Those poor women wore a heavy veil, con cealing even their features ! " "Bare-headed, bare-armed, bare-footed," said the man, "what is there to distinguish you gl3n- 'lewomen nowadays from a pack of factory girls Jut for a spree? Heavens! If when one meets a group of you on the sands the men's arms aren't round your waists, one feels that they uught to. .13c!',</p>
        <p>Ile girl laughed again. She was beautiful in a warm, slumbrous way, with a skin like ivory, and rich dark hair and eyes; but now she shot a quick glance at the man's face as he looked thou_g._htfully out to sea. _ . .</p>
        <p>"Whose 'fault is it," he said—"the women who give too much,_ or the men who ask so little? We only demand modesty, decency • we don't want to share the woman we love with the whole world. There should be charms, there should be graces, jealously hidden from the world, and kept only for home consumption. Iz. the free-and-easy, go-as-you-please manners of women towards men, of men towards women, is to be found the real reason of the failure of chivalry on the one hand, of true womanliness on the other."</p>
        <p>"Oh ! " cried Lenore, with lazy scorn: "wo don't faint at a mouse, and die of a mig)--;n1 as our grandmothers did, if you mean that ! " "But did they?" said the man. "There must have been good stuff in those girls, physically and n;entally, to make the splendid mothers they did. With our own parents went the last of the old cchool. Motherhood nowadays is practically a lost art. The man who wrote the line 'To suckle fools and chronicle small beer' was a fool himself, and a vicious fool at that! " "Now you are indecent ! " cried the girl; yet she was not angry. He kri,lw that she would not resent 'anything that he might say. His nostrils contracted disgustfully, as he moved a little further from her. • It had all gone so pleasantly from first to last —this untrammelled companionship of man and girl—winked at by a blind and complaisant world that had itself set a lax standard of morals and manners. Yet the man knew well enough that it was all wrong--that there had been no true courtship the courtship that should be the dim, cool, fragrant alley leading to the sacrament of marriage in the temple (for marriage is a sacrament to all true women), as it would have been to Dell, from whom Lenore had stolen him; and now he was to slip by a lade wicket, as it were, into marriage, as men tisid girls nowadays drifted, without respect, without illusion.</p>
        <p>Lenore drew nearer, and rubbed her e,.)ft cheek Against his coat-sleeve. Yes, that was how she :lad detached him from Dell, by giving so much 4.5 keep him away (and Dell had given so little), apd now he had come to see that modesty was the first and chiefest good in a woman, modesty t.nd a love of home and little children, and 'aeauty was of very little account when it made :tself cheap. Dell's winsome, homely face seemed 10 him now the sweetest thing in life, as repre- renting the sweetest things life had to give, in rye, good repute, and honour, at that moment.</p>
        <p>The girl thrust out a little naked foot, beauti ful in form and colour, spanned by a. tan-coloured strap, .and laughed. "The doctors are for ever preaching the open air cure," she said, " urging us all about marc. air and light, and less clothing, yet the momer: we women leave off our hats and stockings, you men grumble ! " "Do you ever read your Bible?" said the man." You'll find in it finer rules for manners a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>He sat quite rigid—his nerves crisping in re- 'pit under Lenore's unwelcome caress—knowing tat he had come to the turning of the ways, hat to marry this girl meant the wrecking ti his future life, and that if he had not the Jourage to break with her now, he would in- vitably break with her after marriage. And as he gat watching his stern, lean face, his silence, is stillness, 1 frightened her. This was more than mere huffiness. Suddenly he felt danger in the air, and sat up straight. " I know who it is," she said, angrily, "it's Dell." He shook his head. "I have not seen her for Dyer a year," he said. It was for exactly a year that his intimacy with Lengre Lepell had lasted. " Dove-eyed, demure glcved," (she would die a shame to be seen even putting on her gloves in the street!) "and a little mother to a host of squalling brats—apparently she is your ideal of what a woman should be!" "Listen, Lenore," he Said. "We have had a good time, you and I. and now we are going to part. No one will say anything. In the present lax state of society, our affairs are no one's busi- ness but our own, and plenty of men are ready to take my place—for beauty is so very, very rare."</p>
        <p>Suddenly it struck him how horrible it was that he should be sitting here, saying these things—incredible things; but the girl herself had put the cart before the horse. And yet he was to blame. . . . a man is always to blame if he /cm a woman make herself common and cheap to him. "It's Dell," she said, under her breath. "Dell, Dell, Dell!"</p>
        <p>"Lenore," he said, and almost groaned, "why don't you women make us men respect you— Heaven knows we are eager enough to do it! " Lenore laughed contemptuously. Her sleepy eyes were now widely open, for she knew that she had played her cards badly—carelessly—and lost the man. _</p>
        <p>Nor could she punish him. When a woman claims complete independence, and emancipates herself wholly from home authority, she forfeits the proLction that her mankind could and would extend to her did she conform to the canon's of conduct that rule girls who respect themselves. Of course, she knew that these accidents some- times happened to girls of her views and habits— that marriages, drifted towards without visible intention on the part of the man, sometimes did not come off; but it was very rarely that a man had the moral courage to "got out" at the last moment, and she ha.d not for a moment expected such courage in -Wilco Gibson. And the man was rich, desirable in every way, and—here was the sting--he would go back to Dell and marry her, and she, beautiful Lenore, would have to put up with one of the inferior men eager to take truce's place. "I feel a cad and a beast," he said; "yet know that what I am doing is for the happiness of both of us—if love were not forthcoming from me you would seek it elsewhere." "Oh! don't mind me," said Lenore. in a hard, dry voice. "It has taken you a considerable time to find out that we were thoroughly unsuited to each other—but better late than never." He whitened, understanding now why so many men married unhappily—because it. was easier to them to be unhappy than cads; yet he knew that his whole future life hung on the issues of the next few moments—that he must he a brute, and a. resolute one, if he were to cut himself clear of her—and suddenly he sprang. to his feet. "Yes, go," she said, furiously; "go back to Dell," and she turned on him a face so deformed by passion that he hardly knew it. Truly "hell bath no fury like a woman scorned," and he felt like a beaten hound as he left her.</p>
        <p>The children were all at school, and Dell was done in the little Highgate house. fragrant with flowers, wnen miss Liepeu was announced. There was only a. very slight acquaintance between the two, and Dell rose, surprised, as the tall, beauti- ful girl swept in, made a pretence of touching her hand, and plunged at once intc the subject that had brought her there. , "Miss Brunton," she said, "for a year Bruce --Gibson and I have gone about everywhere to- gether. ,He behaved to me its the girl he was going to marry, and now he has left me." Dell had been working; now she folded her hands on the work, and said, gently: "He asked you to marry him?" "Men do not ask girls nowadays," said Lenore hotly. "They just drift into an engagement; but the one who backs out is—dishonourable." For a moment Dell was silent, a lovely colour coming into her cheeks, that transformed her al- most to beauty. "It don't know." she said, quietly. "You see, that's all just playing round, and marriage is a working partnership, is it not? A man should be so very, very sure before he asks, and a girl should hesitate so very, very long before she consents." • "Oh call it sordid prudence, not love," cried Lenore—" the sort of 'Love me little, love me long' business." "Hasty love is quickly gone," concluded Dell. "Miss raepell"—she lifted her brown eyes— " Bruce loved me, and, when he asked me, I was prepared to confess that I loved him,- but you took him away from me—you made him appa- rently one of that fast brigade to which you be- long. But he is too strong. too fastidious a man to permanently adopt its manners and mode of life. Have you come here to ask me to persuade him to return to it?" "I have come to. you," said Lenore, "to put it to you, as 8. potnt of honour between one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, rebru.ary 1, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, rebru.ary 1, 1913.</p>
        <p>/comm. and another, not ts 7 take him back as Four 'over when he really belongs to me." "Who speaks of 'honour'? Who stole first?" :tied Dell, with a strange note of passion in her usually quiet voice. "We were very happy, and, though I am plain, I could have made him the sort of wife he wanted. Oh! you beautiful people are never such happy, beloved wives as we despised ones, who study a man, and keep his home sweet and restful for him!"</p>
        <p>"Restful, with a houseful of brats!" cried Lenore, furiously, the atmosphere of he room, the soft white needlework under the girl's womanly hand, showing in sudden, violent con- trast to her own bridge-playing, cigarette-,,mok- :ng entourage, as she knbw it would to Bruce when he came—for come he surely would. "I love children," said Dell, and touched *ith 3m exquisite gesture of tenderness the work in 'ler lap. "I could no more do without my little nrothers and sisters (especially now our dear mother is dead) than they could do without me. And Bruce "—her voice unconsciously softened— " loves them too."</p>
        <p>"And I hate them. They are a fearful expense lnd an awful worry. So you will take Bruce back?" "No!"</p>
        <p>The voice was very soft, but very final, and the man who, finding the front door open. had walked in with the freedom of an old friend, eard it as he paused on the threshold of the !rawing-room.</p>
        <p>"Then he will come back to me!" cried Lenore, springing up joyously. "Don't move, my carriage is waiting in the lane. May I go through this window?" dnd, with no further leave-taking, and the usual lack of manners of her type, she vanished. Dell, left alone, mutely interrogated the ceil- ing and furniture, instead of Heaven, and re- sumed her work; but a tear fell on it, and rusted her needle.</p>
        <p>"She is. very lovely, and he is jast—a man,!" ;he said. Then, hearing a slight sound behind her, turned to see Bruce standing just inside the door. _ _</p>
        <p>" 0-1-ti4 she cried, wP.rmly, "do you add eavesdropping to your other good qualities?" He made no reply, but in a methodical way brought a chair 11D. and sat down opposite her. He was pale and thin, but handsomer than ever. =he thought, in his lean, brown way; and he had the look in his eyes that had always mastered her before she found him out as the weak, self. indulgent beauty-lover that he had proved him- self for the past year. "Dell," he said, quietly, "I heard your tromise to Miss Lepell just now, and you have just got to break it, even as I have refused to pay my debt of honour to her. We will be moral runagates if you like—but---" "Speak for yourself." she said, coldly, and took up the work .on her lap, and drove hot rusted needle steadily through it. "While the man is abroad, .sowing tares, the woman sits at home, and thinks to some purpose. Go back to Miss Lepell. She has many claims on you " there was scorn in her voice--" that I have not. that I never should have, were we engaged a rifetime." Then she turned resolutely to other matters. inquired for his people, spoke of her father and the children, gave him tea, and made him feel that for a whole year he had shut himself out from the only real home that since his mother's death he had ever known.</p>
        <p>Whon, six months later, the engagement of Miss Lepell was announced to a rich mustard. maker, and it was followed, at no great distance of time, by the marriage. of "that handsome, charming, rich Mr. Gibson to a homely girl of the 'Little Dorrit ' type, named Dell Bnxnton " —or so said the world—neither Bruoe nec Dell minded. [THE END.]</p>
        <p>to accept the theory that the wind conveyed the ticket from the top of the car to the bottom of the car at the other end. Mr Steel: Many things are possible. The defence of the prisoner was reserved. The magistrates committed him to the Quarter Sessions, bail being allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>"FARES PLEASE !" WARRINGTON TRAM CONDUCTOR CHARGED WITH EMBEZZLEMENT.</head>
        <p>"FARES PLEASE !" WARRINGTON TRAM CONDUCTOR CHARGED WITH EMBEZZLEMENT.</p>
        <p>Wesley Horton,a tram conductor in the employ of the Warrington Corporation and residing at 182, Padgate- lane was, at the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday, committed to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions on a charge of embezzling the sum of 2d.. the monies of the Warrington Corporation. A large amount of interest was taken in the case and there was a crowded court. The magistrates were Coun- cillor H Roberts (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst and Bennett, Messrs. T Locker, J Hepherd, W Riobard- sou and Col. Edelston—Mr A T Hallaway (deputy town clerk) conducted the prosecution and Mr T S Steel (instructed by the Tramway employe's association) defended the prisoner, and intimated that if, after hear- ing the evidence, the magistrates were of the opinion that a prima facie case hoci been made, his client elected to go tor trial. Mr Halloway said the sum appeared trivial in itself but the charge was a serious one. The prisoner was a person who was in a position of trust, and the offence was one which was most difficult to detect unless certain steps were taken as in the present case. The evidence would show -that on Friday evening, January 17th, two clerks in the Borough Treasurer's department met in the evening and proceeded to Thewlis-street, where they boarded a tramway car, which left Ryden ds-street at 8.20. They at on the top of the car and purchased two tickets. On 384 and On 385. They marked those tickets by a pin prick through the "d " beside the figure one. They left the car on arriving at the terminus, leaving the tickets behind them on the seat. On the return journey from Smik.ey Bridges to Warring- ton at 8.30 the car was boarded by a checker near the Ship Inn. The checker did not know the numbers of the tickets that bad been takon by the two clerks, but te did net know that they proposed to mark them in the way he had indicated to the Court. Consequently when he was examining the tickets he would be able to tell by the feel of them whether the identification mark was on or not. He examined the tickets and found that they all corresponded with the waybill, The way- bill showed that tickets On 384 to Cu 306 had been issued on the journey from Sankey Bridges to Rylands- street, though the prosecution alleged they were issued on the journey before. The checker found the ticket On 385 in the possession of a passenger on the car. On 384 was not to be found on the car, but there was evidence to show that a pasuenger had left the car before the checker went on to it,. It was also interest- ing to note that that ticket, On 385, which they alleged was sold on the journey to Sankey Bridges, was punched for that journey, which appeared to his mind to be corroborative evidence of their statement to a great extent. The next ticket Cu 386 which was sold was punched in the right,secton. The duties of the bench that morning had been considerabl / simplified by the action taken by Horton. William Thomas Eccles and Percy Clarke, two clerks in the Borough Treasurer's office, gave evidence in support of Mr Hallaways statement. Mary Ellis, who travelled on the,8.30 car from Sankey Bridges to Rylands-street, staid she paid her fare to the prisoner. The checker came on the car, and examined the ticket, and she gave her ticket to him. A gentle- man had pre,viously left the car. Cross-examined.—She did not remember the prisoner punching her ticket. She was asked to make a state- ment to the checker immediately on leaving the car. When George Washington Pendleton, a checker, was called Mr Idallaway humorously remarked that there was hardly any necessity to have him sworn. (Laughter). Witness stated on oath that he boarded the 8.30 car from Sankey Bridges to Warrington near the Ship Inn. He inspected the tickets and the waybill finding that all the tickets corresponded erectly with the waybill with the exception that ticket Ou 384 was missing. He found Ou 385 with a pin prick in the "d" in the possession of the last witness, but the ticket was punched on the outward section instead of the inward section. Cross-examined by Mr Steel . He bad known Horton for about eleven years and had always found him to be truthful. It was customary to make up the waybill within 200 yards of the terminus. By Mr Steel: It was not a common practice with the conductors when they had made up their waybill and passengersibad got on that they credited the tickets on the return journey. Detective-Sergeant Holland said when charged Horton replied, " It is not true." Mr Steel said he failed to see any evidence in regard to the sale of the ticket On 384. If those two tickets had been sold on the outward journey and booked on the waybill for the inward journey it would simply be an iriegularity. With regard to the ticket On 385 there was the evidence of Miss Ellis which, he sub- mitted, was not evidence on which any court would act and find a prima facie case had been made ont. Miss Ellis did not remember how many people were in the car, and how many tickets were punched before she received her ticket and she had no recollection as to whether or not her ticket was punched. The two young men got on the car after Horton had made up his way- bill, mad in order to avoid altering it he booked them as on the inward journey, and the ticket which was produced and given to the checker by Miss Ellis was not the one his client issued to her. It appeared to him to be unfortunate that no question was put to Horton by the checker with regard to the ticket. Lying about in the car were innumerable tickets and Miss Ellis was a delicate and nervous woman and there was a possibility that she might have picked up a ticket thinking it was the one which was issued to her. The Chairman : You are going to ask us to assume a very extraordinary state of things. Mr Steel, continuing, said be was sure the Bench would appreciate that though he might have acted irregularly that would not justify him being charged with embezzlement. The Chairman said the tickets in the first instance were issued on the top of the car, while the ticket VVISR afterwards found inside the car at the bottom. Could he suggest any means by which the ticket could have been conveyed to the bottom of the oar? Mr Steel : It is not for me to suggest. The Chairman: I hope you are not going to ask us</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL MUSIC PUPILS.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL MUSIC PUPILS.</p>
        <p>The annual distribution of honours certificates and prizes in connection with the Trinity College of Music, London, took place in the Concert Room of St George's Hall, Liverpool, last Saturday afternoon. Mr Behrend presided and the Lady MayoreEs presented the prizes. Amongst the recipients was Miss Marie Hawley, 77, Knutsford-road, Warrington, who was awarded the silver medal of the College, on obtaining highest marks at the three examinations in the last year in pianoforte playing, junior division. The medal, which is of beautiful workmanship, bears the inscriptions: "Trinity 3ollege, London, Liverpool Centre, Gloria in Excelsis Deo " and" Marie Hawley, Pianoforte, Junior Honours, 94 marks, 1912." Miss Hawley's teacher is Mr William Cooke, FRC 0, organist of Lymm Parish Church, this being the fifth time Mr Cooke's pupils have received medals at these functions, a pupil of his last year receiving the Lord Mayor's Gold Medal for highest marks in the senior division.</p>
        <p>77, Knutsford-road, Warrington,</p>
        <p>Plabs Parkinson,.</p>
        <p>,ISS ULADYS IL Legh Villae, Grappenhall</p>
