      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>Miss Gladys R Hale, daughter of Mr Alfred Hale, Leigh Villas, Grappenhall, has had conferred upon her after examination, the Professional Diploma of Licentiate of Trinity College of Music, London (L.T.C.L.). In the theoretical part of the examina- tion (which took place in Lond( n last month) Miss Hale satisfied the examiners in a severe paper on the Art of Teaching, in addition to general rudiments and harmony. Miss Hale obtained exceptionally high marks—DO out of 100 the practical work in piano- forte playing and was highly complimented on her performance by the examiners. She reilly is to be congratulated on her success in this very difficult examination, as is her teacher Mr. William Cooke F.R.C.O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RIDDLE FOR WOMEN.</head>
        <p>A RIDDLE FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Why do thousands of women suffer when there is nothing to be gained by doing so? Every woman knows that over and over again she will experience really great pain in the back and hips, and a throbbing headache which makes her feel a hopeless victim to the penalties of her sex. All these sufferings may be avoided. They weaken and unnerve the victim if neglected, but women can be spared them if they will use, according to a woman's own directions, a woman's medicine for women. Any chemist will supply Mrs. Lydia E Pinkham's Vegetable Compound, which acts in harmony with Nature, restoring and preserving health. It is wonderfully soothing. It brings speedy relief. Read what it did for a Manchester lady "I have for two years been troubled with pains at the bottom of my back and hips. I had tried several remedies, arid none did me any good. Lydia E Pinkham's Vegetable Compound has made me feel quite a different young woman. It is a most sure and reliable cure." Lydia E Pinkham's Vegetable Com- pound only costs 2/9 for a bottle, or 4/6 a very large bottle. The Lady Manager, the Lydia E Pinkham Medicine Co., 425, Hampstead Road, London, N.W. ; will send it direct at these prices. She will also send free a book to 'women only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>LANCASHIRE P.S.A.'S.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE P.S.A.'S.</p>
        <p>Delegates from many parts of Lancashire and the adjacent counties attended the annual meeting at Ashton-under-Lyne on Saturday afternoon, of the Lancashire and Neighbouring Counties Federation of P.S.A. Kindred Societies. The meeting was presided over by the Rev J G Gascoigne, of Manchester, who was re-eleoted president of the Federation. Mr S Porter Walley, of Bolton,was chosen as president-elect Messrs. James Molntosh (Oldham), James Dunkerley (Ashton), J P Matthews (Oldham), and Thomas Howarth (Bacup), were elected vice presidents ; Mr Stanley Jarvis (Farnworth) was elected treasurer ; and Mr J R Jackson (Bacup) was appointed secretary in succession to Mr James Dunkley (Ashton).</p>
