      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>Votes for Women.—Advocates of women's suffrage are naturally disappointed at the turn of events, which have once more interposed to prevent the House of Commons from dealing in a free and unfettered way with the question of votes for women. They were promised that they should have this opportunity when the Franchise Bill came up, and they have some right o complain of the unreality of the situation arising t:rom the doubt as to whether this course conformed to .he rules of Parliamentary procedure. Clearly this 'angled situation should have been forseen but Suffragists will have no cause for regret, if as a result of Luis confusion facilities are afforded for a Woman's Suffrage Bill standing on its own merits, which would command the support of the members of all parties, in tavour of votes for women. The question divides all parties, all possible Governments, and also women as a whole, but the country would welcome as a guide, and index to popular opinion a plain, straightforward pro- nouncement on the issue from Parliament. It is a question upon which Members of Parliament are prone to act on their own personal judgment, but they should pay some regard to the trend of opinioa in their con- stituencies of which presumably they have some know- ledge. In this connection it may be mentioned that last year out of forty-nine candidates at by-elections in Great Britain, only eight declare" themselves anti- , Suffragists. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Women and the Legal Profession.—The part taken by women in professional, industrial and political life, is everywhere expanding, and in the Colonies, France and other countries there are women barristers practis- ing on equal terms with men, and with equal success. In this country the Bar refuses to admit women to the honour of dignity and membership, and the Law Society still withholds facilities to enable them to enter the profession of solicitors. A test action against the society has teen commenced by four University women, to determine whether on the ground of sex alone they could be debarred from earning their living as solicitors. Ihere is no question that this opposition on the part of use Bir and the Law :,ociety, is directly traceable to the fear of increased competition. Women have been debarred from becoming lawyers, divines, and until recent years from becoming doctors, but there is 00 doubt that they will sooner or later be admitted to the legal profession. This reform evoked at first some &amp;tie jealousy in France, but, according to a lady who , speaks from oxperience, the men arid women advocates at the Palms tie Justice, are always on the hest of r.re,s fraternising freely, and sometimes sharing chambers.</p>
        <p>Lao,.k,uas Weather Lore.—The 2nd of February, Cal.dletuas Day, is an imp.ir tant festival in the Churches, in both Protestant and Roman Catholic countries, and the state of the weather on that day is proverbially said to determine that of the Spring. Dozens ot ancient lands attest its importance, and insist that the vorse the weather on Candlemas Day the better it will oe afterwards. If the sun happens to shine upon this occasion it is an ominous sign indeed, for it means that nly part of the winter is over and that there is as much or worse to corne.,_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>The Mormot and Seal.—The barbarous cruelty permitted in pelogic sealing on the coasts of Newfound- land and Canada, would deter any woman from wearing the beautiful pelt derived from the seal, if only the ruthless methods employed were more widely known. One reason why the marmot has become so scarce, is the cruelty of the trappers who in ono season destro fed many hundreds by drowning them in their holes. Years ago, one set of handsome furs sufficed for even a "woman of quality," to-day, the fashionable woman has a costly set to match each costume Then again, fashions in peltry Lhange nowadays, as frequently as do those of dresses and millinery, all of wnica tends to increase the demand, and to raise the price.. Indeed furs have quite lost their former characteristics, and are used in ways that would astonish early Victorians. They can now he draped as easily as velvet and are thus employed on the huge wraps that are se much worn this winter. In fact they not only trim each other, but take the place of ordinary materials as trimmings and in combination. The Winters's Nluffs and Ilats.—Muffs this season may be divided into two cusses, the fiat, of which there are several varieties, and the round, which resemble a drum, more than anything else. Some of the newest muffs are composed (II the strangest combinations, such as rJueh dog skin and shaggy bear. Panther, badger, and monkey skies are among the novelties tee furrier has lately introduced. The hat of fur, with satin and velvet, or of fur oak, is evee more popular this winter than last, and the small hat which has become almost an obssssion, adapts itself admirably to the use of peltry of all kinds. The picture La, has however, by no means disappeared, but its place is the smart Junction, race meetings, and weddings. For every other occasion the smart hat which grows smaller every dty, is universally worn and milliners are devoting much time and trouble to new shapes and designs for the spring. The Coreelet Skirts.—Four or five years ago the corselet skirt was in high favour, then it disappeared for a season or two, but at present it is to the fore again, and has much to recommend it. It needs how- ever some skill in cutting, and is rarely successful in the hands of the amateur dressmaker. It is irreproach- able in its neatness, requires no belt, and any blouse can be worn with it. But although admirable for morn- ing and outdoor wear, it is not so suitable for evening, The newest models fit, closely to the hips, are tight at the back and slightly loose in front. Fashionable Laces.—The lightest laces are the chosen of the moment. Shadow varieties have a new finish, somewhat resembling Shetland veiling. Black lace is used in every department of dress and millinery, and the new jewelled kinds are gorgeous in the extreme. Ficelle is seen again, and a novelty in Chantilly has a coloured pattern stamped on it, but the effect is more bizarre than attractive. Small lengths of real or good imitations of the best laces should never be despised ; they can be used in a multitude of different ways. Mounted on black ribbon velvet, slightly wider than the lace, a charming neckband results, that would bean expensive trifle to buy ready made. Then dark strips of fur, with pieces of some light lace on eithsr side, make a desirable muff. Coatees for wearing over even- ing blouses, can also be made from odd lengths of lace bought for a mere " song " at a. sale. Front Fastening.—Dame Fashion is not always as kind to us as she might be. For example she long ago deprived us of the really useful dresspocket, and then wont on to make it as difficult as she could, for us to get in to, and out of, out shirts, bodices and blouses. To the woman who possesses a maid, it matters little how fastenings are placed, but when no servant, or friend, is at band to "do one up," the struggle in to, and out of bodices, is not a procedure calculated to improve either the condition of the garment, or the temper of the wearer. In this particular direction however, a happier condition of things is at hand. Leading modistea are causing their newest creations to fasten crosswise, sideways, down the front, or zig-zag fashion, all these devices being easily manipulated by the wearer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>LANCASHIRE INSURANCE COMMITTEE. -- DOCTORS TO DISPENSE WHERE NO CHEMIST.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE INSURANCE COMMITTEE. -- DOCTORS TO DISPENSE WHERE NO CHEMIST.</p>
        <p>At Monday's meeting of the Lancashirelnsurance Committee, the Medical Benefits Sub-committee recom- mended that no chemist should supply appliances other than those in the tariff except at the cost of the insured person. It was stated that some doctors were pre- scribing extraordinarily expensive appliances, which chemists did not ordinarily supply, and which were not on the list. Hence the necessity of the instruction to chemists not to carry out such prescriptions except at the cost of the insured persons. The Sub-Committee also recommended that when in urban districts there was no qualified chemist within one mile from the insured person's house, the doctor attending should be allowed to dispense and receive payment in accordance with the chemist's scale. A scheme for the constitution and appointment of district insurance committees was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>"TRADE AND COMMERCE IN THE MIDDLE AGES."</head>
        <p>"TRADE AND COMMERCE IN THE MIDDLE AGES."</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY MR. J. DALBY.</p>
        <p>Continuing his lectures on " Early English Trade Unions " at the Socialist Hall, Wilderspool Causeway, on Monday evening, Mr J Dalby took for his subject "Trade and Commerce in the Middle Ages." He pointed out that in the Middle Ages, the towns were little islands of craftsmanship in a great sea of agricultural life ; therefore trade and commerce, in anything like the modern sense, did not exist in England. The medireval economic conditions represented a condition of trade which demanded little or no capital beyond the tools of the craft, generally worked to supply a demand which was local and fixed, undisturbed more- over by any such bewildering changes of demand as modern " fashion " bad brought about. In the Middle Ages they had no sort of conception of unrestricted competition. Full as they were of communal ideas and communal feelings, they regarded such a scramble as barbaric. Neither had they, generally speaking, a clear idea of the principle, accepted by many Socialists, of an equal reward for all and still less of the higher principle: from each accord- ing to his ability, to each according to his need. They had a very strong belief in a just price which existed for every article and ought to be given to the work- man who made thit article. Whether for good or bad, they had not enthroned what was called " political economy " as an absolute monarch whose word to its subject "economic man " should be law. Theology was then the queen of sciences, and it behoved men to pay the fair reward to the labourer on the principle of doing to others as they would that they should di unta them. Of course, behind their notion of a fair reward of labour was public opinion as to the stand ird of comfort recognized as appropriate to that class of labourer. They saw there how unprogressive, how stationary the Middle Ages were. People then did not raise the question as to the existing standard of comfort for teat particular class, why there should be classes, on what principle one set of craftsmen were recognized as having a right to a higher standard of comfort than anotner. Accepting the status quo, the existing condition of affairs, as right in the main ' they wished to preserve it, on the one hand by diving it the sanction of religion and morality and on the other ' by removing obvious abuses. It was not a critical age. But it showed up fairly well against our Own, which was content that wages should be determined by the standard of comfort which a class could manage to maintain, left to itself, or rather exposed to the com- petition of machinery and immigrant foreign labour. Mr Dalby then sketched the conditions of trade as determined by the guilds, pointing out that stringent regulations were made against any kind of deceit. Short weight, short measure, dishonest (15 eing of wool, the placing of stones in hay and wool sacks, were all offences punishable by flne. The measures of bread and ale, the two most important food commodities in the Middle Ages, were also fixed. It was not attempted to fix the weight of the loaf absolutely, but the weight was to vary according to the price of wheat, in a fixed scale. Similarly a scale was fixed,according to which the number of gallons sold for a penny (times were better then) variea with the price of barley. The average price seemed to have worked out at two gallons of ale for a penny. In terindon a butcher accused of selling bad meat was brought before the Mayor and aldermen and, if be were condemned, was put into the pillory and had the meat burnt before his face. Forestalling, or the buying up of kupplies with the object of selling them retail at a higher price, was strictly forbidden. Sometimes the goods had to stand in the market place for a certain period until each citizen had had an opportunity of buying what he wanted before any great amount could be purchased by a merchant. Mr Dalby touched upon the restrictions imposed against foreigners (included in which term were merchants from outside cities), and also dealt in an interesting way with the origin of markets and fairs. Reference was also made to the Church'S prohibition of usury or money lending and to the devices which were resorted to for the purpose of circumventing it. All through the Middle Ages (concluded Mr Dalby) the conception of trade morality was much higher than ours. We asked ourselves why it was that, this being so, the system of trade then estate lished broke down and gave us our hideous capitalistic system. The answer was, ha thought, that their attitude to the economic organization of society was largely 9 negative one. The early Christian teachers looked upon the world as, at best, a bad place, and many of them had very definite views as to its speedy dissolution. A man like Aquinas believed in theology as a divine system which was to bring all other sciences under its sway. They did not under- stand that the organization of society and the method of that organization were as important, to say the least of it, as the systematization of the truths of theology. Not understanding the sacredness of the human oody, they could nit understand the sacredness of social economic organization—which was the body of the social life. They had no enthusiasm in the Middle Ages for the right economic organization of society in and for itself. They did not understand that we must set up heaven on earth. And we to-day were faced with a slmilar problem. In the Middle Ages they did believe in ideals ; that belief was very limited to-day, and the number of people who wanted to see those ideals manifested in a corporate way, in 9 new re-organisation of society, were a small multitude indeed. Ours was the task of shaping society, or getting society to shape itself as the corporate expres- sion of highest known ideals. We had to make a neW heaven in a new earth.</p>
        <p>The settlement of Central and Northern Saskatchewan is proceeding at a rapid rate. The homestead Puttied filed at the Land Titles Office at Prince Albert aggre- gated 2,263 for the past year, as campared with 2,099 for the previous year. These entries represent, of course, only a tithe of the actual settlers who have made their way into that portion of the province, and who have purchased land in districts already open and withio easy reach of railway communication.</p>
      </div>
