      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>i , t-, &lt; aIIIMEMINEMINIMO ,:__ ... , _ , ‘ , ~..... DI ~', , =. G •‘ • '4: i 4. ' r°' '.TR I CT , ~, , t __. . ...._ . , ' 5 ;' _. ,</head>
        <p>i , t-, &lt; aIIIMEMINEMINIMO ,:__ ... , _ , ‘ , ~..... DI ~', , =. G •‘ • '4: i 4. ' r°' '.TR I CT , ~, , t __. . ...._ . , ' 5 ;' _. ,</p>
        <p>THE LYNEVI BILL.</p>
        <p>BIG MAJORITYF FOR 66uRaiL;s PROPOSALS:</p>
        <p>The opponents of the Lymm Waterworks Bill should have taken Saturday's weather as a good omen, as water was, for once, conspicuous by its absence, the beautiful atmospheric con eitions tempting ratepayers to come out to register their votes. As ore toured the district during the afternoon there was very little evidence that any momentos decision hung in the balance, there being a total absence of vehicles bearing the usual election advice "Vote for Mr . . .?". The non-existence of rival candidates with their election agents and supporters also tend to make the poll rather quiet with the result that the officers in the various polling stations were able to carry out their duties in quite an unusually easy manner. rile Presiding officers were: —Booth's Hill Ward, Nit- T Newton ; Heatley Ward, Mr Denham ; Lymru Ward, Nir H Oddie ; and Statham Ward, Mr W Mallard. The polling was concluded at 8 p.m. and within half an hour the ballot boxes had arrived at the Council Chambers and counting was in operation. Mr W G Groves, as Returning Officer, presided over the proceedings and was assisted by Mr W Other Councillors present were :—Rev G B Thurston, M.A., Messrs R H Gotobed, J Holt, F Lockyer, 0 G Smith, T Warburton and G L Welford. Amongst other gentlemen in attendance were Mr T Ridgway (Solicitor to the Council), and leaders of the opposition of the Bill. As the ballot papers were sorted and counted any student of physiogomy would have had a happy half hour, for some of the facial expressions that one saw were iudeed wonderful to behold. After the papers had been counted and checked Mr W G. Groves announced the result as Total p01l For the resolution 566 Against the resolution Majority for • 362 He then declared the resolution put before the rate- payers' meeting of January Sth as passed. He said that they had had a greiat fight and though bard words had been spoken at valious meetings he hoped all would be friends again. The Rev G B Thurston, MA, proposed a vote of thanks to the Returning Officer and expressed his desire that a friendly spirit would exist between the members of both parties. Mr 0 G Smith, in secondieg the proposition, caused some amusement by his witty rerurk "First the Church and then the State." He said that he hoped they would all accep.t the result in perfectly good temper. The proposition was carried, after which Mr W G Groves briefly replied. The doors were then opened and Mr Groves, as Returning Oi:icer, announced the result from the front of the Council Chambers to the large crowd that was eagerly waiting. The figures were received with hearty cheers and it was some time before the enthusiasm had sufficiently quietened down to allow Mr G L Welford to address the gathering. Especially prominent were the shouts, They wanted a poll and they've got it ! " Mr We:ford said that as the senior memoer of the Council he wished to say a few words. They had had aligreat fight, not that, they regretted it, but rather they welcomed it. He expressed his thanks to the electors of LyEara for the way they hsd supported them and said the members of the Council have said all along that it was to the best interests of Lymm that they should possess the waterworks. "We beiieve that this scheme is for the good of the district and that our opponents will at a future date feel gled that they lost the poll to-day." He said that the result of the poll would cause the electors to have more faith in the Council in the future. Most of the opposition were personal friends and he hoped that no ill-feeling woulo remain in their breasts. He appealed especially to certain people of the Heatley Ward that they would cleanse their minds of the pre- judice they had against the Council. Mr R H Gotobed who came next, said that as one who bad taken a prominent part in the fight he thanked those who bad voted in favour of the resolu- tion. "We desired your confidence and thank you for having given it us to-day." a hough some of them had had heated arguments he hoped that all would be friends again, and work together to further the interests of the people of the village. Mr W H Finch followed, and said that as chairman of the opposition meeting, he accepted the verdict as given by the majority of the ratepayers. He said that they too thought they were working in the interests of the ratepayers. As generous as the Council have been to them, so would we have been victorious. "Speak- ing on behalf of myself and the gentlemen who supported me, we shall throw in our united support with the verdict that has been given."</p>
        <p>Mr 0 G Smith, said that ho considered that the electors had expressed their sanity and good citizen- ship. "The Council will feel strengthened by know- ing that they have the confidence of the electors of Lymna." He hoped that the result of the vote would prove to he fruitful in blessing and advantage to the whole of the district. He thankeu the electors for their support. air T Ridgway said that he was pleased that Mr Finch had spoken in such a stiaightforward manner. He was glad that the poll had taken place, and was thoroughly satisfied that the proposals of the Council were for the good of the people of Lymm. Mr. .1 H Holt spoke hopefully of the future, and predicted a reduction of the water rate and cost of water for baths. " The ratepayers have done something to-day which they will never regret." Mr C E Cowper said that he liked a fight, and that they had had one. " I was only against the price that is to be paid, not against the purchase of the water- works." Be thought it best that the people should have their say on the matter. We have had it and we are satisfied."_ _ _ -</p>
        <p>.IVIr Hiram Driukwater proposed a hearty vote of thanks to "The ten gentlemen (the Councillors) who have worked so hard on our behalf."</p>
        <p>In seconding thehoposition Mr D Daniels said that the Council membeill did without doubt spend a lot of time on their behalf and thought that they all ought to appreciate it. The proposition was carried_ with cheers. After several calls for Mr Ratcliffe, that gentleman came forwatd and said that he hoped the contest would stimulate a greater interest in the work of the Council. Those people who had opposed the purchase of the waterworks and Lske believed that the ratepayers should have a chance of expressing their feelings. This they had done in an unmistakable manner by endorsing the action of the Council, and time alone would show whether their decision had been a wise one. He thanked both those who had supported the Council and the opposition, and said that he hoped no one would take offence at their action. THE WORK OF THF. S.P.C.E.-00 Sunday services in aid of the Society for the Propagation of Christian knowledge and the National Society were held in the Parish Church. At the morning service the special preacher the Rev R A :Cochrane, M.A., organizing secretary for the S.P.C.K., spoke about his Society being the mother of elementary education in England. He said that this educational work was now being carried on in this country by the National Society, which was one of the offsprings of the S.P.C.K. The Revs G B Thurston, ALA., and H Gray, M.A„ also took part in the service.—At the evening service Mr Cochrane dealt with the emigration work of the S.P.C.K. Offertories were made on behalf of the above two Societies. On Monday night the Rector, the Rev G B Thurston, M.A.' presided over a well- attended lecture held in the Parochial Schools. The Rev R A Cochrane said the society hoped to celebrate its 216th anniversary in March by obtaining 216 new members on or before that occasion. Dealing with the work of the society, the Lecturer spoke of its religious and secular educational work ; colleges in Loudon, Toronto, Lennoxville, Tinevelly and Madagascar.; and its efforts to introduce industrial education in the foreign fields. Medical Missionary work was also touched upon, as was the society's publishing and print- ing department. The Lecturer also spoke of the build- ing grants the Society gave, and said that they helped to build 36 churches every week. After speaking about emigration work, Mr Cochrane concluded an interesting lecture, with an appeal for the prayers and assistance of the audience.—The Rector touched upon his having been an emigration chaplain, and spoke of his trips to Canada. He announced that Mr Bailey had been com- pelled to give up the local secretaryship of the S.P.C.K. owing to advanced age, and that Mr Beckett had kindly consented to hold the office.—The lecture was enhanced by the exhibition of a number of lantern lisdes manipulated by Mr Peet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATIvE SOCIETY'S CONCERT.—Once again the Education Committee of the Warringtcn Equitable Industrial Co-operative Society Ltd have kindly pro- vided a Lyrnm audience with is splendid free concert. The large assemblage and hearty manner in which they showed their approbation should have pleased both the committee and artistes. There were many demands for encores, but owing to the limited time at their disposal only a certain number of items were allowed. Mr T Ferguson occupied the chair. The exacting duties of accompanist were cleverly carried out by Miss G R Hale, L.T.C.L., who also played a pianoforte solo. , HEATLEY BOY SCOUTS.—The Heatley troop of the P. Scouts held a social in the Oughtrington schools last Saturday. After doing justice to a splendid tea, in which some of the members of the High Legh troop Participated, the scouts and their friends listened to a Most interesting lecture by Scoutmaster T Nash on " A trip to Holland," which was illustratad by about 130 Slides clearly shown through the lantern by Mr H Resketh. Additional interest was attached to the , lecture inasmuch as it was a record of the holiday that many members of the Heatley troop enjoyed last summer under Vie guidance of Scoutmaster T Nash. Short addresses on Scoutcraft were given by Scout- masters Surridge and 3 Webb, jun. An exhibition of handbell ringing, and a pianoforte and kettle drum duet by S Jones and A Brown also formed part of the pro- ceedings. A vote of thanks to Scoutmaster T Nash and llr H Hesketh was proposed by Mr W G Jones, seconded by Mr .7 Webb, sen., and carried in a characteristic manner.</p>
        <p>197F:STZXAN SUNDAY SCHOOL PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.— "6 scholars of the Wesleyan Sunday School held their annual tea, concert, and prize distr:bution in the schoolrooin on Wednesday. If the future year's work tlin any way dependent upon this gathering it should 7 most successful for the event passed off in a 13,ry, pleasant manner, over one hundred persons the down to tea. Thiq was followed by 'ue concert end prize distribution which was presided Over by Mr H S Brown. Mr A H Hudson, the hon. secretary, read the annual report which was of a most °nceuraging nature. Mrs Waterworth presented the Prizes, Which consisted of about sixty handsome books. The whole of the concert proganoree consisted of items Provided by the scholars and were performed in a 'banner that reflected credit upon both the per- formers and teachers. Recitations were given by M w°.Peland, Ii Dodson and Ethel Linley. Miss , instanley's cars contributed the action songs "Little ousemaids." " Granirtio';her's Dream," and "Three zrodest Quakeresses " ; and the dialogue " Difficult to Please." Other dialogues were " Mother's Help," by the. Frank Brown's class ; "Servant and Shop Girl," by Or Ogden and B Lord ; end " Lucy's Birthday," by the Band ot Hope members. The Primary Department taeg " Under the Old Umbrella," and M Skidmore the 2") "The Big Drum." TERRITORIALS ENTERTAINED.—The Lymm Detach- i&amp;ent of the sth (Earl of Chester's) Battalion of the ,„"e,shire Regiment have not yet been called upon to ,z'ale the citadels of Europe, or to withstand the ViEnPact of el:numbered odds in equatorial regions, bat i,°”" are a fine looking body of men, and impress one as the quite capable of sustaining the best traditions of Lie British Army, when and whenever called upon to do ;°,• Their ciuties on Friday evening were of a less ' ,`Tenhous character, but were performed with that ,i'flanimity of purpose, and consummate attention to detail, which forms part of the eqeipment of every teod soldier. Owitag to the generosity of Lieutenant • W C Hartley, the local Detachment mustered ta full force 'at lee Drill Hall, when the supper catered by Mrs H Clare was promPLIY attacked and demolished, after which the loyal gast was submit ted and honoured. Lieutenant attleY performed the duties of chairman Other officers Present were :—Lieut.-Col. GroVes, Captain Alexander, Orgeant G Holt, and ex-Sergeant A Clare, and W sirkPatrick. Lieut.-Col. Groves gave a brief address eln the Year's working and commended the men for the ,:aY they ying one. He expressed had braved the last camp which had been a 10,1'Y tr his desire that they treld get more recruits.—Lieut. Hartley speaking on th stre neth of the detachment said he was anxious tha" theY should increase their numbers and hoped that ey would reach the total of 60 men before the next xvitl79l/. If they did so he would present a cup l• • leh was to be shot for and won outright. illeutenant-Colonel Groves presented Sergeant George jr,°2t with the cups for the best shooting. Other shoot- prizes were awarded to Privates H Clare, A re.-:°,l"noseo and J Watson. Private fisher won the for the best recruit.—A smoking concert was also in which the humorous element was provided „a Manchester artiste. Songs were given by tr''ergeant Arthur Clare, and Privates H Clare, J arcival, H Reid and A Thomas. Private W Kirkpatriak teentributed a clarionet solo. The proceedings rn'nsted with a vote of thanks to Lieutenant Hartley, proposed by es-Sergeant Kirkpatrick.</p>
        <p>ly URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. The month meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council wal held in the Council Chamber on Thursday present Rev G evening, Mr 'CV G Groves presiding. There were also Smith, G L ti Thurston, Messrs T H Warburton, S Davies, Pt, H Gotobed, W Gould, F Lockyer, 0 G surveyor) Welford, W 'Mullard • clerk), D Mort (, W L Donaldson (gas manager), Dr De Jong (Medical officer.</p>
        <p>TIIE PARLIAMENTARY tau- The Ch • saman said he should like to thank the bers for the extraordinary amount of time they had tov,,n last week in connection with the poll in regard ,t,Wae proposed purch i ase of the waterworks and n fr 'ending the meetings which had contributed to the :rand result obtained. He also included Mr Mullard -aent(l; he would like to congratulate the Council on the pii;:en of the ratepayers in refusing at the meeting to h " 4 VOte 0* no corlidence in the Council. He felt 2re that Sat'utdav's result would place the Council in a ..auc ha, u stronger position for they knew now that they Vile confidence of the ratepayers. " Clerk formally reported the result of the poll.</p>
