      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>PRINIROSE LEAGUE.—A successful concert, whist drive and dance was held in connection with the above on ThurFday evening in thc Church of England school- room. Mr G Mather presided, and a speech was delivered by Lady Gerard in which, of course, the iniquities of the present Government were fully exposed. Songs were rendered by Miss Roderick and Messrs F Turner and E Bate. In each case an encore was demanded and granted. The Misses Gorton were well received in a pianoforte duet. Mr F Blackwell acted as accompanist. A hearty vote of thanks was carried to chairman, speaker and artistes,the spokesmen being Messrs P H Bennett, F Blackwell and E Lunt. The winners of prizes in the whist drive were:—Ladies, 1, Miss Povey ; 2, Mrs Cook. Gents, 1, Mr E Stringer; 2, Mr James Warburton. The M.C.'s were Messrs Pomfrey and MT Houghton. Dancing was indulged in until about 2 a.m, to the playing of Mr W Wainwright. Messrs J Critclaley and T Antrobus, jun., being the M.C.'s. WESLEY GUILD.—A debate has become quite a regular feature in the annual syllabus and the subject for this year—" Is the present system of party govern- ment desirable ? " was discussed on Monday evening. The Rev E Morgan presided and the debate was opened for the affirmative by Mr H B Mason, while the chief spokesman for the negative side was Mr Burgess. Messrs Birchall, Lunt and Duckworth spoke in favour of the present system. Upon a division an equal number voted for the ayes and the noes and, as the Chairman did not give his casting vote,the result was a draw. A vote of thanks was accorded to Messrs Mason and Burgess on the proposition of Mr Rosoollor, seconded by Mr Proffitt,</p>
