      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, Februaryl, 1 913, i STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, Februaryl, 1 913, i STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>OLD PEOPLb'S TREAT. The old people's treat was held at the Stcckton Heath Institute last N 1 onday under very happy circum- stances. 'The treat had been organised by a committee foamed from members of the four local churches— St Thomas', the Wesleyan Methodist, Independent Methodist end Hill Cliffe Baptist. Contributions were asked for at each house in the district and a-substantial sum was collect,d, which enabled the committee to provide an admiraole tea and to make gills. All who bad reached the age of 60 were invited. About 140 assembled for tea and thoroughly enjoyed the repast. After tea a lantern entertainment was conducted by Mr John Hallows, pictures of local celebrities and scenes of the old village being approp7iately chosen. At seven o'clock the Vicar of the Parish, Rev. J Collins, took the chair, supported by Mr. and Mrs. Vero Davies, Rev. G Curtis Mr. Arthur 141m:infield and Mr. J Miller. The artistes at the concert were Miss May Isherwood, Madam Lily Fairhurst, Mr and Mrs W H Wounds, Mr E Whitehouse, and Mr E A Sanders, Mr F Banner being accompanist. The entertainment concluded with a humorous sketch " Mixem's Matri- monial Mart" in whicy,h the following took part : Miss S Ealey, Miss Ada Grounds and Messrs.J Roughs- edge, G Moss, P Moss and G Ault.</p>
        <p>MR. JADIES LAWTON, aged 5.9, who has lived in the village all his life</p>
        <p>At the commencement of the entertainment, a little ceremonial took place which giently pleased the guests. It was discovereu that the oldest guest was Mr James Lawton, who has almost completed his 89th year. He was invited to the platform and duly crowned " king" of the assembly. Mrs Vera Davies carried out the crowning ceremony by placing on his head an elaborate smoking cap, and Mr Vera Davies presented a silver- mounted pipe. Mr Lawton was duly enthroned and made a picturesque figure-head to the platform. He was further honoured by the singing of" God save the king." At the close of the entertainment Mr Curtis thanked the subscribers, Mr A Mounfield eXpressed indebtedness to the artists, and Mr J Hallows thanked the Chairman. As the old people left tobacco was presented to the men and a packet of tea to each woman.A mince pie was also given to each. Many of the old people were conveyed to their homes in cabs and motors and these who were unable to leave home (of whom there were a large number) were presented with packages of groceries. Toe treat was moat successful in elery way and gave great pleasure to the workers as well as to the numerous guests.</p>
        <p>- It ought to he mentioned that the use of the Institute with crockery, &amp;c., Ras generoukly granted lree of charge.</p>
        <p>but we want to be put on equal terms with the foreigner." Every now and again, he concluded, they read that there was a crisis in the Unionist party and that the Government were coming out" Don't believe it "be added. "This Government will not come out till they are forced out. They will stand anything. They quarrel among themselves ; they are log rollers, but they are not coming out. They are fastened down securely by screws' and there is no chance of moving them."</p>
        <p>Mr W Peter Rylands went out of his way to call attention to the fact—a hich Liberals have repeatedly emphasised—that the representatives of the Govern- ment are in a hopeless minority in the House of Lords. Therefore, he added, they could not force their opinions down the throats of the Opposition by the remorseless operations of the division bell. On the question of Home Rule he expressed concern that Ireland, left to manage her home affairs, would not enjoy the social ieforms which fell to the lot of Englishmen, and in this connection he instanced the benefits of Old Age Pensions and National Insurance of which Ireland would, under Home Rule, have been deprived. Songs were given by Mr Harry Gibbon and Mr T S Bryden, the accompaniments being played by Miss Esme Callender.</p>
        <p>Mr Torn Boothroyd's baud played for dancing, Mr Fred Evans acting as M.O.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENH ALL.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH. HONOURING A VETERAN.</p>
        <p>Thursday last was the time of great rejoicing at the above place of worship. It was the 85th anniversary of the birthday of Mr Joseph Sanderson, and a social evening and concert was given to celebrate tbei event. The whole proceedings had been arranged and g ven by Mr and Mrs R Nelson. Mr Nelson, who is president of the Warrington P.S.A., brought with him the P.S.A. orchestra, which, under the able conductorship of Mr J Hannan, added immensely to the evening's entertainment. Refreshments were pro- vided at the interval, during which the orchestra rendered very pleasing items. councillor Henshall presided and after stating that the proceeds would go to the new chapel fund, wished Mr Sanderson on behalf of the church and members many happy returns ' and hoped that God would spare him to be with them for many years to come. (Applause.) The following items were then rendered, and judging by the ready applause, gave every satisfaction :—Tbe orchestra, " Tanhauser " ; Mr Warburton, a song, "The Admiral's Broom " • Miss Blanche Lbwis, a recitation entitled, "Papa's letter" ; Miss Whitmore a song, " When the heart is young" ; the Misses Blanche Lewis and Maggie Fairhurst, a dial ouge entitled, " Hunting for a servant " ; selection by the orchestra, " Batiste's Andante in G " ; a Lancashire sketch entitled "Courtin neet," by Mr R Nelson; Mrs Warburton, a song entitled " Swallow," (encore) "Who's that tapping at the garden gate" ; Mr and Mrs Hannan were encored for the duet "The keys of heaven." The veteran guest of the even- ing then entertained the audience with a recitation "The land of my birth," and a reading entitled "The old gardener" ; Mr and Mrs Wurhurt on a duet "When the wind cometh in from the sea "; Mr Hannan a song, "Good company," (encore) " Absent "; Miss Whitmore, song " Golden days "; Mr R Nelson a humorous sketch " Tha'rt welcome, bonnie brid " ; Mrs Hannan a song, " Down the vale." Mr H Harding's rendering of the</p>
        <p>Lost chord "as a cornet solo- was excellent. The accompanists were Miss Sbakeshaft, Miss Edith Watts end Master E Hannan.Mr Sanderson thanked all present for the very hearty manner in which they had received him ;such a happy evening would live in his memory to the end of his days. Below we give a photograph and a brier sketch of Mr Sanderson's carreer.</p>
        <p>Ehoto, MR. JOSEPH SANDERSON.</p>
        <p>Although a native of Yorkshire, the subject of our sketch has been a familiar figure in and about Warrington for the past 12 years. To sit and chat with one who has weathered 85 winters is an oppor- tunity whereby one can gather items of interest recalling the methods and customs of past generations first band. Mr. Sanderson began work in the woollen mills of Benjamin Shaw and Co. in Honley 77 years ago. He was then eight years of age. None know the details of a woollen worker's trade much better than our old friend. He started as a "billy piecer, " and from that to the "devil hole" and then he rose from " slubber " to manager. Mr Sanderson tells how he used to stand at the warehouse door and watch the old stage coach pass ithrough the then little village en route for London. It is decidedly interest- ing to hear him talk of his father making a light with the flint-box and steel when calling his lads to work at 5 o'clock in the morning. Those were the times when 12 hours' work per day had to be put in. is recollections of the Coins Valley flood are very vivid This disaster was caused by the bursting of the Holmflrth Reservoir; the water dashed into Colne Valley and did incalculable damage, and 70 people were drowned. Mr. Sanderson assisted in the recovery of the bodies.—Mr Sanderson goes on to tell many interesting stories, but space will not allow of their repetition. The old mania bale and hearty, enjoying the use of nearly all his faculties. He reads well without glasses ; he can do a good day's gardening ; be can entertain at a concert, and his only drawback is deafness, although this is but slight. He is now resident with his daughter and son-in-law, Mr. and Mrs R.Nelson, of Castlemere, Grappenhall. Ile is lock- ing forward, in his own words, to still many years of useful life. Our readers, we feel sure, wi,h this cheer- ful veteran of industry, many years of comfort and well-earned happiness.</p>
        <p>• •.• . , . •••••-•-: . ••:. • -• • .• ••••••••• • . „ . • ••••• • `. , • . • • • • •••;.1 . • •.• , . • . • , , • • • • • • •-• • • •• . • . • •• •. • . . ••• • . , • . . • . " • •• • . , . . •V . . " , . • .. .• • ••• • • •• . •• .• . • .. • . : . • . • : ••• .. • • • •••,, . • . : • •-•• •••• • ......</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MR. KEBTY-FLETCHER, M.P., AT LYME</head>
        <p>MR. KEBTY-FLETCHER, M.P., AT LYME</p>
        <p>ABUSE OF THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>The Primrose Leaguers of Lymrn were much elated on Wednesday evening, for their annual social gather- ing in the Drill Hall was made Lotable by the presence of the Member for the Division, Mr Kebty-Fletcher. Otherwise the proceedings followed the usual lines—a judicious blending of tea, politics, and patriotic songs, concluding with that most popular feature of all—a dance. There would be an attendance of about 500, Mrs Cornwall Legh (Ruling Councillor) presiding over the meeting and concert. Among others on the plat- form were : Mrs Callender (bon secretary), the Hon Mrs Tollemache, Mrs 'glands, Mr and Mrs W Peter Rylands, Mr Callender, Mi s Burrowes, Mrs Moles- worth, Miss Boumphrey, Miss Esme Callender, Mrs K M Tweedale, Messrs J R Newton, W E Hiller, F Evans, Newton Worthington, H S Saville, and R Oliver. As to the vocal items, there were the approved ditties about our fighting navy, but quite the tit-bit of the evening, was a sort of vccal war-cry from the pen of Mrs Callender, the burden of the refrain being Keep Kebty-Fletcher at the top of the poll, and you're all right, you know." Of course, the writer could not resist the _opportunity of a dig at the much-maligned Insurance Act, and the insured worker was asked to conjure up a touching vision of the fate of the patient, in the lines:</p>
        <p>"If you are very nearly dead and to the doctor go You'll find 300 other chaps all waiting in a row." The Ruling Councillor predicted that, owing to a cold, the gentleman who undertook the singing of this choice number would probably sound "a bit like a crow" but, as a matter of fact he did very well, and sent the faithful into ecstacies of delight. Mr Kebty-Fletcher's most devoted admirers would hardly class him as a serious politician. His chief stock-in-trade appears to consist of a somewhat feeble flow of invective, and practically the whole of his speech at Lynim was given over to extravagant state- ments and steady abuse of the Government, without a semblance of real argument. He trotted out tee familiar bogey about Ulster losalism and Home Rule, which led him to lament that " at present here is no House of Lords." Technically, he added, there was such a place, but it had no more power than a Lymm Debating Society. The House of Lords could not alter a single sentence, line or word in the present Bill, and it must go through. Alter this unequivocal statement, however, Mr Fletcher curiously added the qualification : If it becomes law, the present Government will have to enforce it on the people of Ulster. They would not have it, he declared, except by force of arms and it became a very serious thing in these days when our own people had to be coerced and dragooned into leaving the Union Jack and living under the Irish Nationalist flag. Mr Fletcher next turned attention to the land question and predicted that house rent was bound to rise, because the "people's budget" and form 4 had killed the building trade and practically no cottages were being built to meet the increased ' demand for housing. Quite oblivious of the fact that agricultural land is untaxed, Mr Fletcher coolly accused the Government of having helped to make food dearer by taxing the land which was " the raw material of food." Next he advanced the Protectionist pro- posals by which the foreigner is to relieve us of part of the unpleasant duty of paying taxes, but, if tariffs do not increase the prices of commodities, why should Mr Fletcher have gone out of his way to state that "the last thing you should tax is raw material " ? The Welsh Church Bill, of coui se, came in for castigation, Mr Fletcher indalging in delicate sarcasm at the expens3 of Sir Alfred Mond, whom he described as "that fine spsoimen of a Welshman " and stating that the Welsh Church, with the wboie of its present endowments, was poorer than "the Brunner, Mend's. " Disenoowrnent, he added, was only a long word for theft and be charged the Government with "robbing the roar curate who is not able to to defend hirte,elf." The Diseadowment Bill would recoil on the Government and with double force on the Nationalists, who had aided and abetted the robbery, when the Radicals again threw the schools into the melting pot and the only defenders of those schools would be the Unionist Party. The only utility that Mr Fletcher can discover in the land taxes is that they "provide Radicals with highly paid posts." Can it be that Mr Fletcher studies the speeches of Mr Harold Smith' to provide himself with ammunition ? He made a great point of the methods which Mr Smith has recently been telling his supporters that certain Radical clubs in London resort to, in order to raise the wind. These entertainments were said to include music hall turns and boxing matches on Sunday, and Mr Fletcher added that most of the Radical clubs were kept open by their sale of "dirty whisky." Mr Fletcher nest made statements which could only be 'regarded as accusations of jobbery on the part of members of the Governnment, but he took care to add that "I am not suggesting anything, but there you are !" With regard to tariff "reform" he said "We don't want Protection at all,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CROFT.</head>
        <p>CROFT.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR JOSHUA BALL,J.P.—At a whist drive held by the Croft and District Farmers' Associa- tion in the Council School on Saturday evening, the opportunity was taken to present Mr Joshua Ball, the president of the Farmers' Association, with an illumin- ated address, congratulating him on his appointment as a county magistrate. The Rector (Rev H I Wadeson) presided and Lord Wolmer, M.P. made the presentation</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>LABOUR CLUB.-At the fortnightly meeting of the Peoketh Labour Party held on Wednesday in the above club room, the members and friends were favoured with a visit by Mi Lowe of Warrington, who gave an interesting address on " What is Socialism." The speaker dealt with it in a fair and broad-minded way and he was listened to with close attention by a very good company. Several questions were asked at the close of the address by the members. Mr Lowe has promised to pay another visit to the club at some future date. Mr Clay of Warrington presided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>DENTH OP MR. RALPH LAWTON.—The death of Mr Ralph Lttwton, of Brookside Farm, Orford, and formerly of Wellfield-street, Warrington, took place on Monday morning after comparatively short ilhss. He had been ailing for some time, but only took to his bed a fortnight ago. Dr Naden, and eventually Dr Fox, were summoned, but he passed away as stated. Deceased was in his 6Gth year, and leaves a widow and family of five—three sons and two daughters. The funeral will take place this (Saturday) afternoon at the Cemetery</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>GREAT SAN KEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAN KEY.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED INCENDIARISAI.—At the Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday, Jame,: Gilbert Whitley (32), a labourer, who was charged with setting fire to a hayrick at Sankey, on itiovember 13, was found insane and unfit to plead.—His Lordthip ordered that the prisoner should be detained during his Majesty's pleasure. PAROCHIAL TEA.—In connection with St. Mary's Parish Church the annual parochial tea party was held in the Sankey Schools, and proved to be very successful, there being a record attendance. Besides the tea the party enjoyed themselves with dancing, and a concert was arranged in the small room under the superintend- ence of Mr D R Griffiths, Head Master of the Council Schools, Penketb. The following were the artistes : Mrs Wilfred Dwerryhouse. Misses Hilda II A Murray,M IMur.ay,Messrs Gonlden,and %V H Ireland;Miss Standish I was the pianist. The music for dancing was supplied by Messrs Ray Bros., and the M.C.'s were Messrs E J Dumbell and H Plumpton. SUNDAY SCHOOL SCHOLARS'. TREAT.—The senior scholars of the St Mary's Sunday school and Penketh Mission, had their annual treats on Saturday last. The officers and teachers had made excellent arrangements for the scholars to have a pleasant evening and they certainly were fully repaid for their trouble, as all seemed to enjoy themselves to the full. The usual games were indulged in. The Vicar was in attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—On Wednesday evening last Mr SA Elliot gave a paper entitled "An evening with Keats." Mr Elliot's visits to the Guild at Moore are always keenly looked forward to and consequently there was a very good attendance. %Ir. K. H. Meldrum made an excellent chairman. Contrary to common practice, in dealing with the biography of this youngest of the English Poets who have become famous, Mr Elliot commenced by giving a graphic description of the death of Keats, at the early age of twenty-five in Rome whither he had gone on a fruitless quest for health. Then reverting to the time of his birth he outlined in a very interesting manner the life (alas all too short) of John Ke,its. During the evening many beautiful selections from the poet's works were quoted and Mr Elliot possesses such elocutionary powers that many of the selections brought forth well merited applause. At the conclusion of the meeting Miss Dutton voiced the very sincere thanks of the members to Mr Ell jot for his most interesting paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>BLAMED THE CAT.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, Philip Paylor, of Norley, was summoned for trespassrng in search of game. Albert Edward West, as3istant gamekeeper to Sir Richard Broolse,said he saw Taylor setting rabbit traps in a field in th,?. occupation of Mr Charles Percival. Defendant said the cat had brought home the rabbit trap found on him. He did not know how to set a trap. It was just a chance that be bad the trap in his pocket. He was fined 10s, and advised to turn the cat out when next it brought a trap home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr J Spruce presiding.</p>
        <p>ASSISTANT NURSE.</p>
        <p>Miss C Roughsedge wrote resigning her position as assistant nurse at the Union Infirmary. The resignation was accepted and it was decided that a successor be advertised for.</p>
        <p>THE MENTAL DEFICIENCY BILL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Wharfedale Union forwarded the following resolution passed by them and asked the Guardians to give it their support. "That His Majesty's Government be urged to re-introduce at the earliest possible moment the Mental Defficiency Bill and use every endeavour to have the measure passed into law."</p>
        <p>The Chairman (to the clerk) : What do you recommend? The Clerk: You cannot tell in what form the Bill would be introduced. It was compulsory- Mr Linak er : I think if we had a rest from legisla- tion for a time it would be better.</p>
        <p>Mr Linaker, jun., moved that the letter lie on the table.</p>
        <p>Mr G Pilling in seconding said: " It would be as well ; it might affect many of us." (Laughter.) NATIONAL CHILDREN'S HOME.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the National Children's Home and Orphanage wrote stating that the cost of maintenance would in future be 78 per week. He stated that the boy Wallace in their custody attended the Elementary Day School. He had progressed very well and was nearing the end of a year in which he has been in Standard V. The medical report stated that the boy was developing well. The boy, when 14 years of age would be able to take up work on the farm and garden. The boy was well fitted to do the work. Mr Linaker suggested that the Board should consider the advisability et removing the boy to the Dutton Homes. (Hear, hear). _ _</p>
        <p>Mr WoOdvvara asked on what ground the boy would be taken away ?_</p>
        <p>The Clerk : On the ground of expense. You can just as well.look after him at your own homes.</p>
        <p>After having spoken at some length in favour of the work which the Children's Home did, Mr Wood said he thought the publio ought to give the Board credit for saving the rates of the Union to such a great extent as they did, by taking children in the homes, other than those who bad passed through the Workhouse. Mr Roylance said he was afraid it would cost them more than is per week.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the question of the advisability of transferring the boy to the Dutton Homes be con- sidered.</p>
        <p>THANKS FOR TREAT.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Visiting Committee, which were adopted, showed that Miss Mary Hannah Potter was recommended for appointment as laundress at the Workhouse at a salary of £2O per annum. Mr Spruce proposed that the Board tender thanks to the Ladies Visiting Committee for the preparations they made on the occasion of the treat to the inmates. He was only sorry that tbe Board was not more fully represented at that gathering. Alderman Norman seconded, and siid the treat was equal to any they had had befoie. Messrs Davies and Baxter supported. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton, on Tuesday. Mr C E Linaker presiding.</p>
        <p>WHOSE ROAD.</p>
        <p>The County Architect wrote raking the Rural Council to permit the construction of a cartway crossing at the Stockton Heath Police Station, across the footpath to the ambulance entrance gates. It was pointed out that the road in question was Grappenhall-road which was controlled by the County Council and it was decided that the Clerk write to the Clerk of tbe County Council stating that the Runcorn Council did not control the road and that it was the County road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>INSURANCE Co 11:34 I TTEE.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE Co 11:34 I TTEE.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Insurance Committee, having written asking the Council to neminate representatives to serve on the Runcorn Urban and Rural District Committees, it was decided that. Messrs c, peed, Davies and Hallows be appointed on the Rural District Committee and Mr Chadwick on the Urban District Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr Antwis : They have got a good job. (Laughter.) The Chairman ; We shall soon want salaries. GRAPPENHALL SEWERAGE.</p>
        <p>It a, ports of the various committees which were adopted included that of the Grappenhall &amp;c Sewerage Scheme, which stated that the committee met the previous day and instructed Messrs Wright and Harnlyn to inspect and report on the b--st means of dealing with the open sewer in Mayfield-road, Grappenhall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY of the Local Government Board wrote respecting the quality of the water supply in the Parish of Antrobus. it was stated that it was not good and he was to enquire what action the Council have taken or propose to take to provide satisfactory supplies within the parish ? It was observed that supplies derived from wells in the Parishes of Alvanley, Antrobus, Bartington and Kingswood are stated to be adequate but inconvenient" in this connection, and whether it applied to all supplies in the parishes. The CLERK was instructed to formulate a suitable reply stating the position of the parishes referred to, also that the" inconvenient" applied to the distance.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>ThA MEDICAL OFFICER (Dr. Adams) submitted his report which was adopted. It showed one case of scarlet fever and one of diptheria from Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST HELENS CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner of the St Helens Recreation Cycle Club was held on Saturday night at the Royal Raven Hotel, about sixty members and friends being present. Mr Geoffrey Pilkington presided. The toasts of the N.C.U. and the St Helens Cyclists' Club were proposed by Mr W Taylor. Mr W Inman, chairman of the centre responded. Mr I Little responded for the St Helens Club, and thanked the Recreation Club for their kind- ness in allowing the club the free use of their cycle track for supplementary sports. In response to the toast of Messrs Pilkington Bros." Mr Pilkington said he was sure that working men living in towns like that wou:d find great benefit from the pastime of cycling. for it would improve their health and make them happier in every way. ALLEGED INCENDIARISNI. ST. HELENS COAL DEALER SENT TO THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>ASSIZES.</head>
        <p>ASSIZES.</p>
        <p>John Lloyd, coal deal;:,-1-I,—fork-stre3t, St Helens, was charged on Monday with wilfully setting fire to his stables. Mr Quinlan). who prosecuted for the Hearts of Oak Fire and General Insurance Company, pointed out that, after completing an insurance on his shop and stables and two horses on January 8, accused paid 5s 9cl premium, and received his policy. Within a few days the police found the man's stables, in which there were two horses, on fire. The fire sergeant noticed a strong smell of paraffin and the circumstances were so suspicious that Lloyd was arrested, and was now charged with setting the place on fire by means of paraffin. Mr Donnellan, for the defence, denied the imputations of the prosecution, and said that the magistrates were invited to decide against the prisoner simply on a number of assumptions. The bench com- mitted Lloyd to the Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>CRAZY WITH ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>CRAZY WITH ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>Gratitude from a man who was cured by Dr.</p>
        <p>Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>One of the most troublesome features of</p>
        <p>Eczema .is the liability</p>
        <p>of the disease to break</p>
        <p>out again after apparently</p>
        <p>aucceisful tr eiim eni.</p>
        <p>.. . . . . . ::::!:;,i'.:::::".;,,:•-• ::::_,',--:,:::,'.,:-.:::,'•., . .. . • • • - -+-- . .•• .: ....• - • • ::i''.,:', • •• . • ... . • ••• .-- iz:.:::i! '?i'" . . .. MR. J. S. BIRCH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>because</head>
        <p>because</p>
        <p>mischief is</p>
        <p>so _ deep-</p>
        <p>seated and becan'se ordinary treatments</p>
        <p>afford only partial relief. To get at the root and</p>
        <p>cans; of Eczema it is</p>
        <p>necessary to purify and refresh the system with . .</p>
        <p>good pure blood—the rich red blood that is</p>
        <p>created by Dr. Williams</p>
        <p>Pink Pali. To make this point clear the case of Mr. Joseph Samuel Birch. proprietor of the Stores, 2 Harding Terrace, Northampton, is here furnished. "Some l ime ago," related Mr Birch, "I had an itching burning feeling between my fingers. This went on for a few hours, then the skin began to break. Then " blebs' formed all over my hands almost like nettle-rash. In a short time my arms were covered too, and I endured misery such as I should'at like even a dog to know. "The rash broke and became one huge sore, and I nearly went mad from the scalding, itching pain. felt as if I could tear the flesh from my bones. took medicine, and my wife made me some ointment. One doctor said that mine was s bad case of Weeping Eczema; he gave me cooling lotions and medicine. But the Eczema would break out again and again,and steadily spread to my back and legs. My arms and hands were so bad that my wife used to pour the ointment over my limbs and draw a pair of stockings over my hands up to my shoulders, to protect my arms, I had six doctors at different times, also treatment from hospital.</p>
        <p>WITH BOTH ARMS IN SLINGS.</p>
        <p>" For months together I walked about with both arms it., slings, and, of course, work was out of the question. I had fearful nights ; when warm in bed the irritation was enough to drive me crazy. "For 12 years I suffered like this. Then a niece persuaded me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. I took several boxes of the pills before noticing much difference ; still the irritation steadily kept going down, and I could sleep better at nights. As I persevered with Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, the sores dtied up leaving the flesh underneath as clean and healthy as could be. In time, except for a scar or two, no one could have told that I had had Eczema. My cure took place two years ago, and I've never had the least sign of skin trouble since. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills thoroughly cleansed my system, filled my veins with good pure blood, and banished all the. Eczema."</p>
        <p>Sufferers should write to 46 Holborn Viaduct, London, for free booklet" Diseases of the Blood."</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People have cured not only innumerable cases of Eczema, but also Anfemia, Indigestion, Rheumatism, General Weakness, and varions Disorders of the Blood and Nerves. They are obtainable of dealets ; but if in doubt send direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 40, Holborn Viaduct, London, enclosing 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six. Common pink pills will not do, you must have Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Burning Fatality.</head>
        <p>Burning Fatality.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the Wa;rington Coroner's Court, on Wednesday morning by Mr F A Jones (deputy coroner), touching the death of Elizabeth Leigh, wife of William Leigh, a wire rope maker, of Bostock-street.—The husband stated that his wife was. .54 years of age. On the 10th of January he was at work, when he was informed that she had been burned Be returned home and found she had been badly scorched on the body, chest and arms. She told him that her Deice asked her to get a pencil which was on the mantel shelf over the kitchen fire. She stood on the fender to get it, and in reaching over, her apron caught fire. The flames travelled up her dress and her blouse became ignited. She tried to put out the flames by wrapping herself in an old macintosh and by pouring cold water over herself. She was successful in her endeavour and then rushed out into the yard screaming. A neighbour came to her assistance and she was taken to the Infirmary. She appeared to be recovering, until Saturday, when she became worse and died early on Sunday morning.—The jury returned a verdict of 'Accidental death."—Mr R Roberts watcbed the proceedings in the interests of the relatives.</p>
      </div>
