      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>El</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am afraid that "Phil Squod" has failed to grasp the nature of my last letter. Its purport was to demonstrate what should be the marks or characteristics of the True Church established by Christ. The question of doctrines taught by that or any other Church is quite anoher matter and beyond the scope of the argument. I have .not yet attempted to expound any Catholic doctrine, although quite willing to do so if requested. As to "Phil Squod's" alleged perplexity as to what Church or branch of a Church I refer to., I think there can be no doubt in the minds of your readers generally, as the heading of the letters is sufficiently significant. I don't refer 'to any branch, but to the Roman Catholic Church, which is either the one True Church or nailing. Our friend urges, in his last letter, that this Church is corrupt, and if such is the case it has not a claim to be evert a branch of the true Church. The wickedness of individuals within her pale is one thing however, and the alleged 'corruption of the Church and her doctrines quite another. But to refer to his idea of u Church. He states that "a true Church should expound the Scrip- fures, teaching nothing but what is contained therein." Such a statement clearly implies an authority to teach, and for a man to be teaching he must necessarily be explicitly and definitely ex- pounding truths which there is no gainsaying, for if he. is merely expressing his own opinion he is not teaching. Now, the Catholic Church teaches among other things the Unity and Trinity of God; the Incar- nation, Death .and Resurrection of Our Saviour; Purgatory, Immaculate Conception, etc. Whether these doctrines are true or not is not the question. The point is that she teaches this perfect and definite code of doctrines explicitly,- emphatically, unitedly, and universally. Thus I maintain that even if her doctrines are not true (which I don't admit), she possesses the elements necessary for a true teacher, viz., certainty, confidence, and unity. Moreover, as God's Church must be universal, she also possesses that distinction. This I asserted in my last letter, and as it has not been questioned, it is therefore admitted that such is a fact. Now, if the Roman Church is wrong and bad, there must exist somewhere the true one, and which must necessarily have better credentials than that which is untrue. Then what. I ask "Phil Squod" to tell us is : What teaching body outside the Roman Catholic Church propounds for man's belief a definite code of doctrines? Which is that "Church of which any preacher of such Church can say to his congrega- tion : "You must believe this or that dictrine which I deliver to you, because I am one of the preachers of that authoritative body which Christ established for the expounding of the Gospel? And what I deliver to you is the same as is taught by my fellow preachers throughout the world."</p>
        <p>If "Phil Squod" can bear testimony to a Church having such or bettek credentials, he will give us cause for serious reflection.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>JOHN RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>86 Buttermarket-street, January 29th, 1913</p>
