      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>IMPORTS OF MANUFACTURES 1903-1912.</head>
        <p>IMPORTS OF MANUFACTURES 1903-1912.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln Warrington as elsewhere. we are eon:</p>
        <p>vinced that after the ten years' ding-dong of Tariff Reform we are all heartily sick of the sub- ject. I hesitate therefore in writing anything that bears any relation to it. But leaving out of con- sideration the inconsistencies, sectional appeals, economic confusion which have all along charac- terized this movement, it is well that we should carefully watch the tendency of our trade with the outside world which this ill-started movement was designed to alter. And here we pass from the region of theory to that of fact. For the pur- pose of ascertaining the growth of our imports of fully-manufactured goods ready for use I have as below extracted from the Board of Trade re- turns the figures for 1903 and 1912. The period is of special interest, because it covers the time in which the fiscal controversy has been prominently before the country. To ascertain the balance for home consumption, the re-exports are deducted: It must be acknowledged that the term fully- manufactured goods ready for use is difficult to apply. It is impossible to tell in many of the items what proportion is subject to further manu- facture, and hence difficult to determine to what class these properly belong. For example, in the case of. silk manufactures, which form nearly a third of our fully-manufactured imports, a pro- portion of these imports are in the form of broad- stuffs, which although as such are fully manufac- tured must necessarily go to our workshops to be made up into articles of clothing. Again, ma- chinery although not subject to further manufac- ture is almost entirely us-e'd by and benefits our manufacturers. For this reason I have thought it proper to omit it from the manufactured goods ready for home use. The accompanying list (although the selection is necessarily of an arbit- rary character) contains the items in our imports which can be most appropriately designed as above. And inasmuch as comparisons are made of the same goods for both years, the totals show fairly accurately the progress made in supplying our increasing requirements for manufactured articles ready for use. THE IMPORTS OF WHOLLY-MANUFAC- TURED GOODS. 1903. 11,784,117 Cutlery. Hardware and</p>
        <p>1912. 11,727,094</p>
        <p>Silk Manufactures</p>
        <p>Scientific Instruments 2,545,895 3,938,931 Watches, Clocks &amp; Parts 1,342,824 1,429,305 Cotton Manufactures</p>
        <p>(Yarns. omitted) Woollen Manufactures</p>
        <p>6,310,027 8,339,032 8,078,086 5,723,656 881,066 800,919</p>
        <p>Boots and Shoes</p>
        <p>Hats and Bonnets</p>
        <p>(trimmed &amp; untrimmed) 367,316 903,300 Printed Paper Fancy Goods Soap Toys and Games Carriages, Cycles, Motor</p>
        <p>Cars-and 'Cycles Glass and Glassware</p>
        <p>2,113,600 1,528,7.39 3,613,414 3,209,408 245,835 225,884 2,086,371 2.233,050 1,292,913 914,205 83,569 87,097</p>
        <p>Basket &amp; Basketwares...</p>
        <p>Leather Manufactures ... Musical Instruments ...</p>
        <p>Oilcloth</p>
        <p>Wholly-manufactured... 14,367,283 44,166,434 Semi-manufactured .... 71,960,704 112,147,434</p>
        <p>116,327,987 156,313,868</p>
        <p>IMPORTS. Prom the above we get the following :- 1903 1912 mill. mill. Dec. p.c</p>
        <p>Wholly manufactured goods Semi-manufactured Incr.</p>
        <p>goods The amount of semi-manufactured goods is ar- rived at by deducting the fully-manufactured goods as per list from the total imports of goods classed in the Board of Trade returns as "Goods wholly or mainly manufactured." These semi-manufac- tured goods are mainly composed of 'blooms, bil- lets, and slabs, sheet bars, tinplate bars, and the like; copper, lead, tin, zinc in a semi-manufac- tured state; chemicals including materials for making dyes, drugs, colours, and tanning extract; leather; paper; motors and motor-cycle parts; paraffin wax, oil cake,-starch, etc.; stones and slates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>EXPORTS.</head>
        <p>EXPORTS.</p>
        <p>We now trim to the eporta of our manufactures of a similar character to the goods enumerated above. We find that while the imports of these have slightly diminshed, our exports in the similar classes have increased by £04,000,000. The total exports of manufactures have increased .U. 51,000,000.</p>
        <p>It is only right to say that the general increase in values has effected semi-manufactured more than fully-manufactured goods, beCause these are nearer to the raw material. In manufactured goods, while also dependent on raw material costs, the ' proportionate dependence is less, hence to</p>
        <p>ascertain the exact increase or decrease it would be necessary .to enter into elaborate calculations beyond our present capacity. But a comparison of the quantities imported in the contrasted years shows that while the correction would be required, it would not in any way invalidate the deduction which can be fairly drawn from the figures. This deduction is as follows During the last ten years, under our system of free imports, our nianufac- tures are tending more and more to supply the natural increase in the demand .for the finished articles for home consumption, and our increase of imports of an-called manufactured 'goods is only in those things Which supply our manufacturers with what to them is raw material. CRARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, January 30th, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 1, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 1, 1913.</p>
        <p>DVANCED EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>should like to express iilyself upon a sUbject which some superior person signing him- self "Astonished" seems to' speak with authority, viz., the instruction given at the local Secondary School. _ _ _</p>
        <p>I was a scholar at • this institution for four years, and can therefore claim to know a little of the education which is received there. I have been an "Old Boy" for nearly six years, and have had time to test, in some degree, the value of the instruction received. Others there are who were students at the school with myself and they, too, will testify that the instruction given in those days was thorough, and the methods employed by the staff were.quite as modern, and as successful, as those of any other school. The list of suc- cesses gained by the students is one of which the town should be proud, and these distinctions have been gained by the efforts of the staff, coupled with-the -industry of the pupils. Evidently "As- tonished" is so utterly overwholmed by his child's inability to keep up with the rest of the form that-in his paternal blindness he fails to reoognise his child's duty towards the schooT. • Moreover, if he had a genuine desire to see his child succeed, he would have interviewed the Principal, or the "offending" teacher, and, not have ignorantly rushed into print. May I suggest to "Astonished" that he kindly leaves the education of the children to those who have been trained expressly for this purpose? The duty of a right-minded parent is to stimulate his child to vigorous co-operation with his tutor, and not by sneers to make it impossible for the youngster to respect; and so derive benefit from the efforts of the teacher. He May as well take his child away from the school now that the teacher's good name has been held up to ridicule. The education at the Secondary School is "very advanced" for tho small-minded and unsympathe- tic prig; but to any one who will realise his duty to his school, it is not above his capabilitieq, and will stand him in 'good stead in later life.—Yours, etc., "W.:.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>VETERAN POSTMAN.</head>
        <p>VETERAN POSTMAN.</p>
        <p>Photo Burrcit AIR. WILLIAM TAYLOR HUNT,</p>
        <p>a well-known Warrington postman, who retired yesterday after thirty-two years' service. He began work at the Warrington Post Office in 18,30 as a night mail messenger and later was appointed a let:er del ivei er, having held that position up to his retirett,eut horn the service. He has served under eight postmasters and has enjoyed the confi- dence and esteem of the staff and public, by whom his conscientious work has been much appreciated.</p>
        <p>• . 1 •••••••• • • : *P. /,,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>MISS GORNALL'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>MISS GORNALL'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The charity concert, given in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening by Miss Mary Gornall for the benefit (f the Royal Albert Institution Lancaster,was distinctive in being conceived on a novel plan involving the free admission of the audience and the taking up of a silver collection during the iteerval. There was quite a large gatherit g of baluonably dressed people in the hall, and a very attractive, but all too long, programme of vocal and instrumental music and recitations was given by a number of capable amateur and pro- fessional artistes most of whom have appeared on past occasions at Miss Gornall's matinee concerts. We understand that the Royal Albert Institution has claims upon the public of Warrington inasmuch as some half-dozen at least of the inmates of that admirable harbour Lf refuge are from this neighbc.ur- hood. It is gratifying therefore to be able to announce that what is regarded as a very satisfactory money contribution will be made to the funds of the institu- tion as the outcome of the self-sacrificing efforts of Miss Gorman and her friends. As the circumstances under which the concert was given preclude anything in the way of detailed criticism, it must suffice to say that a very enjoyable evening was spent in listening to an entertainment the only fault to be found with which was its inordinate length. Otherwise the programme was as nicely diversified as song, recitation, and instrumental music both of the solo and concerted kind, could make it. The songs by Miss Hilda Cragg-James and Mr Mann were mostly both intrinsically good and well sung, and, beirg in their nature and character as widely apart as the poles, the effect of each was thereby enhanced. One could not but feel grateful that the first named singer included amongst her contributions the finely emotional but too much neglected "Spirit's song" f Haydn. To such an item and to Teresa del Riego's "Sink, red sun," some rollicking and good- humoured songs by Mr. Mann of which "The little grey home in the West" and "The Ringers of St. Peter's" are specimens, served admirably as a foil, and served also in lifting into more cheerful surroundings much else in the programme that was perhaps of too gloomy a character. Mrs. Ridgway's two recitations "The happy Prince" and" Dora" were given most feelingly and with great restraint, but, though they were listened t., with deep attention, one could not help feeling that the inclusion of both these big things resulted in unduly prolonging an already overloaded programme. Amongst the instrumental music were a Fantasia (Chopin) by Miss Mabel Fairclough, and an adaptation for two pianos of a piano concerto 17 Mendelssehn in which Miss Goruall took the more °nen us and responsible part. There were also a number of more or less interesting violin and 'cello solos by Miss Amethe Leadbetter and Miss Gladys S. Stewart and a trio (the that movement of the Mendelssohn D minor) in which these two ladies were associated with Miss Gornall.</p>
        <p>Military Dinner The Warrant Officers and Sergeants (past and present) of the Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) held their annual dinner in the Lecture Room at the Drill Hall on Saturday evening. Sergeant-Major W Powcer presided over a gathering of over SO. Lieutenant-Colonel B Fair- clough, commanding the 4th Territorial Battalion, as present. An excellent dinner was served by Mrs Atkinson, from th, Cafe, Bridue-street--Referring to the presence of Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough, the Chairman said he was an old friend of the Ist Battalion, having served with them during the South African war with ,the Volunteer Service Company.—Colcnel Prendergast, commanding the 2nd Battalion, who said he was pleased to be present, read a letter from Colonel Richardson (who takes a keen interest in the club) wishing them every success. (Applause.) The com- bined bands of the depot, The King's and South Lancashire Regiments, played an excellent selection of music.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>REV T T JAMES ENTERS UPON HIS PASTORATE.</p>
        <p>The Rev T T James, M.A., will enter upon his pastorate at Wycliffe Congregational Church to-morrow (Sunday), when he will (,ccupv the pulpit at the morning and evenieg services. Special music has been arranged, incluGiug the anthems " 0 for a closer walk with God " and 'Hear ray prayer " which the choir sil render at the morning and evening services respectively. It is expected that there will be le.rge congreg,tions, so that Mr J;.rnes may inatuurate his ministry in the town u. der the most ef.couraging and hel i,il auspice... 'Cho six yeas' h..ppy and successful mini‘try of the v T T James bt Wycliffe Church. .7.l.ltilield, came to an cud lat Su,day evening, when he preached from the we, "1 kin whom I have believed." Referring to his dep,irture he said that he should carry 'away with him Irian that church many happy memories. •</p>
        <p>At a pub. _ meeting Vir and mes were the recipients of handsome presentations. It is worthy of note that Mr James brought into exi.tence not only the brotherhood, which is the second largest in Sheffield, but the sisterhood and in-titute coni•edted with the church, and the entire work of the chinch has grown aid prispered during his stay in the city. Mr F Skelton, who presided it the meeting, said they had a great deal to he thankful for in the six years of Mr JAnes's mit istry and in the good work he had done, not only by his preaching but by everything le had takr n in hand. The Rev J F Matthews (Baptist, formerly of War- rington), s.rid that all the Free Churches of Sheffield regretted Mr James's leaving, for they were 10-ing a genitl, painstaking, versatile, and accomplished minister and a true friend. He was going to one of the finest churches in Lancashire, which had wonder- ful traditions, and in which he would have splendid opportur ities. The 11ev. E V Everard, vicar of St, Philips's, said it had been a pleasure to be a neighbour of Mr James He had known him on the cricket field, and if he had not been stumped by Mr Trar ter be had been bowled by Mr James. (Laughter.) They all liked mu,cular Christians. (Hear, hear.) The Rev E H Titchmarsh spoke on behalf of the Sheffield Congregational Aw=ociation, and Councillor Crowther the Sheffield Federation of Brotherhoods, of which he is the president. The latter referred with pleasure to the presence of Mr Everard. He said that the pulpits of Nonconformity were open to Nonconformists. When churches recognised their duty to the people around them, a 4 Wyciffe had done, the masses wculd &amp;cy,rl be gathered in.</p>
        <p>Several speeches followed by representatives of various departments of the chunh. Mr A Jefferson, secretary of the chuich, handed to Mr James a gold watch, and to Mrs James a dressing case. The Pastor, in reply, thanked the church for the freedom of speech they had allowed him in the pulpit. He said that, in his opinion, every church existed to deal with people who lived at its doors, and that had been his ideal in Sheffield. Christianity was not for a select few, but for everyone.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the Rev T T James will pre-ide at the annual congregational tea party at Wycliffe Memorial Hall.</p>
