      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODRO-NIE.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODRO-NIE.</p>
        <p>"Queen of Lilliput." - Such is the title which has been bestowed upon Lady Little, the smallest woman in the world, at the sight of whom, the large audiences at the Hippodrome this week have been perplexed and astonished by her exceeding diminutiveness. Although over 20 years of age she is only 23 inches high, and does not turn the scale at eleven pounds. She is accompanied by her director, Mr Harold L. Hilliard, who, although only ordinarily tall, appears to be some giant besides the dainty little doll-lady. In child-like tones she tells the audience interesting facts about her life, and not a few of the audience are given the oppor- tunity to shake the hand of this little artiste. Lady Little has an enormous amcunt of valuable jewellery which, as is explained, she has received from Royal personages the world over. We are also humorously told that in view of the single blessedness which Lady Little enjoys, many offers of mart iage have been received from War- ringtonians. Someone evidently wants a pocket edition entertainer. The premier all-round instru- mentalists, Cavalotti Bros are highly successful in a refined and clever musical turn, playing most skilfully on numerous wind instruments. It is indeed a beautiful ten minutes with the great com- posers. Conway George is a vocal and character impressionist of no mean older, his child mimicry being very well clone.fhe music hall favourites of our fathers and of the present day are presented by Graham, the human marionette. We see the music hall of 1870 and the" stars" of that period as well as the elaborate vaudeille stage of 1912. His representa- tions cf Barry Lauder and George Lashwood evoked much laughter. Hal Bryan is a popular vocal comedian with a bright style and greatly pleases in his popular number" You can't judge a woman by her clothes." Ragolio is a rag artist and lightning sketch artist, for whom these is a fair amount of applause. Frank and Beryl Harmeiltre clever drawing room entertainers in burlesque comedy and harmony, Little Connie Edmonde, a juvenile serio comedienne also appears.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The great attraction next week will be the presentation by Fred Kano of his latest laughing creation "The Hydro." Syd Chaplin will appear as Nick Sharp. There will be a real swimming bath with a bevy of beautiful bathing girls. Other items will be :—Belle Davis and Clacker Jacks in Southern pastimes; Charles Torbay, comedy in the shade; Jack Stocks, the man of mirth; Elsie Ellis, charming comedienne; and John Earl, the Hatedian.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>CRINIE IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CRINIE IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable of Warrington (Mr. Martin Nicholls) has issued his sixth annual report dealing with the state of crime in the borough and the work of the police force for the year just ended. The number cf crimes reported during the year was 304, being an increase of 98 over the previous year. From these offences 195 persons were pro- ceeded against, an increase of 41 on the figures for 1911. The value of property stolen amounted to £646 10s lid and the amount recovered was £325 19a 11d. There was also an increase of 9 in the number of juvenile offenders charged with theft and apprehended dut log the year. The number of persons proceeded against for non-indictable offences was 1,553, of which number 922 were apprehended and 631 summened. As compared with the figures of the previous year there was a decrease of 15 in the number of persons arrested, and an increase of 57 in the number of persons summoned. There was a decrease of 9 in the number of persons proceeded against for drunken- ness. The police had restored to their parents 355 children who were found wandering in the streets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>£lOO FOR THE INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>£lOO FOR THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>INSURANCE ACT AND THE "FOUNTS OF CHARITY."</p>
        <p>The following letters have been sent to us for publication by Councillor H. Roberts : , "Kingsley," Hill Cliffe, Warrington. 30th January, 1913. J. H. J. Hampson„Esq., Secretary, Warrington Infirmary. Dear Mr. Hanipson,—l have very great pleasure in handing to you a cheque for £lOO, which I have received from my friend and colleague, Dr. Markel, as a. donation to the funds of the War- rington Infirmary. I also enclose a copy of Dr.. Markel's letter to myself which accompanied his cheque, and which admirably expresses what, in my judgment, is the opinion held by the great majority of our people in regard to the National Insurance Act, the in- calculable benefits of which are only now begin- ning to operate.—Faithfully yours, H. ROBERTS. 20, Queen's Gate Terrace, London, S.W. 29th January, 1913. Dear Mr. Roberts,—l believe certain people have made the National Insurance Act a pretext for withholding; or for reducing, their contribu- tions to the hospitals. I do not know whether this has happened at W rrington, but if it has I should like to do some little towards redressing the balance. Will you (10 me the kindness of passing on the enclosed cheque for the benefit of the 'Warrington Infirm- , and, in doing so, of intimating that this donation is expressly made to commemorate the inauguration of the great scheme ofiNational In- surance, which, far from drying up 'the founts of charity, should hearten all those whose desire it, is to make systematic war with disease and distress.—With very kind regards, believe me, yours sincerely, . • (Signed) K. E. MARKEL. , A. Roberts, Esq., J.P.. "Kingsley," Hill cliffc, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Treatment of Insured Patients.</head>
        <p>Treatment of Insured Patients.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Board of Management held 3esterday afternoon, it was unanimously resolved : "That no chani2o be nu!de for the next three months in the pri4co if t% 0 Warrington Infirtnaty and Dis- pensary in thA trPatarient of patients in consequet.ce of the National Heath Insurance Act, fart that a list of insured patiel ts and t.t.e societies to which they belong be kept." The lion I reasurcr gratefully acknowledges the receipt from Dr K E Make], pc,r *Mr henry Roberts, of the sum of £lOO.</p>
      </div>
