      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>REV. A. J. BRATT. M.A.</head>
        <p>THE REV. A. J. BRATT. M.A.</p>
        <p>Tell.</p>
        <p>The impending departure from Warrington of the Rev A J Bratt, senior curate at the Parish Church, will be genarally regretted, not only by Churchpeop.le, but by members of many other denominations with whom Le Las came into contact during his ministations in the town. Mr Bratt has accepted the position of clerical organizing secretary of the Church of England Waifs and Strays Society for the dioceses of New- castle, Durham and Carlisle, and will enter upon his new duties shortly after EA ster. An M.A. of Durham University, Mr Bratt received his theological education at St Aidan's Theological College, Birkenhead. Having held curacies at Huddersfield, Portsmouth (where he worked in the parish of St. Agatha's Landport, which will ever be associated with the name of Father Dolling) and Tamworth, Mr Bratt came to Watrington four-and a- half years ago. Bis gifts as a preacher ate exceptional, for be combines a happy facility of expression, with a directoess, originality and wealth of homely illustra- tion, which appeal to all. Moreover, he makes a point of keeping in touch with the latest developments in the social and scientific worlds and he has always given his hearers the benefit of his studies. Mr Bratt is also an energetic wotker and the remarkable success of the Men's Bib!e Class is due to his splendid leadership and initiative. Useful work has also been done in the parish by Mr Bratt's sister, Mrs Hopkins.</p>
      </div>
