      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <p>--</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR MD NONCONFORMITY.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT TAKEN TO</p>
        <p>TASK.</p>
        <p>In his breezy and interesting little publication "The Sunrise Annual," Alderman Bennett, in the course of a review of the events of the year 1912, has the following reference to the Welsh Church Bill:</p>
        <p>We —are not very much concerned about Disestabliehment, and, as Mr. Arthur hlounfield has shown in his interesting history of "Warring- ton Nonconformity," now appearing in the "War- rinktna Examiner," a good share of the original endowments of the Church belong, by right, to the churches which now represent the brave ejected ministers who, two hundred and fifty years ago, to the tune of some two thousand, gave up home and all they held most dear, and went into the wilderness sooner than betray their consciences. One of them, we recollect with pride, was Robert Yates, the Rector of Warrington. . • • If the Government had proposed to allocate the huge endowments which had been accumulated up to 1662 amongst the various sects that sprang from the great schism, we should recognize a sort of rudimentary justice in the Bill ; but to. take them from the Church which is, at present, making Filch good use f them, and not to hand the spoil to those who might legitimately claim a share, but to the County Councils, seems to us to be both mean and foolish. We, at any rate, as Nonconformists, will not countenance the plan. Speaking at the annual Couversazione in connection with the Warrington Church Society held in the Patten Hall on Thursday evening, the Rector (Rev Canon Willis) referred to the above expression of opinion. He said Alderman Bennett told them that the Non- coi4,rmist ministers who were the descendants of the ministers during the Rebellion had a right to the endowments. Let them think calmly and quietly over what really took place. Those Presbyterian ministers i had only been n office seventeen years—from 1015 to 1662—and then, to their honour, they resigned because they could not agree to the terms. But they were treated with gi eat leniency compared with the way in which thttr predecessors of seventeen years before had been timed out with t'ie greAtest cruelty. If it were true that there were 2,000 Nonconformist ministers who resigned, it was also true that there were three or four times as many rightful occupants who were turned out by the Nonconformists. It was simply a matter of history. Tne Rector also said he was supposed to have a hankering after the Roman • Church. (Laughter.) Nothing could be further from the truth than that. What he longed for was that the Roman Church should give up the Papal supremacy which was comparatively modern. The Eastern Church would never acknowledge the Papal supremacy and, as Anglicans, they wanted to be one with the Eastern Church, as well as with the Church of Rome, on right terms—and by right terms he did not mean for one moment, the recognition of Anglican Orders by ROMP, but he did not mean the giving up of Papal supremacy. The Rector further defined the Holy Catholic Church as meaning the whole Eastern, Roman and Anglican Churches, and all Nonconformists who had been baptised. Baptism was the one and only entrance into the Catholic Church. On the question of religious education, the Rector referred to the discussion in the Education Committee on the proposed transfer of teachers and scholars from Cairo-street to Holy Trinity school. He had every respect for those who cared for sanitary Fonditions, for the health of the body and for good instruction for the minds of little children, but what distressed him was that, apparently, because men differed so ranch in their religious views, they would prefer to banish definite religious instruction altog.ethT% Nothing could be better than the secular education in the Council schools, find, so far as it went, the religions instruction of an undenominational character was good, but why the Education Committee should refuse to have undenotninational religious instruction in the Council Secondary School when they had it in the elementary schools;he could not conceive. While pleading with the audience to do their utmost to secure religious instruction in the Secondary School, the Rector said the enly other course to take was to make their Sunday schools as efficient as possible, not for one moment being content with reading stories and amusing the chiloren, but seeing that they bad definite religious instruction spiritually and lovingly imparted. (Applause.) A most enio3 able min ice] programme had been arranged by AlrWB Randall (organist of Holy Trinity Church). It included songs by Madame Sara Sileoek, Miss L Davey, Mr C H Ford and Mr .1 B Poole, humorous interludes by Mr II M Ashton, a violin solo by Mr F H Randall and a pianoforte solo by Mr W B Randall (who also acted as accompanist).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>"IN THE HAND OF THE POTTER." Rev. J. E. Ratten bury on Mission Work in London.</head>
        <p>"IN THE HAND OF THE POTTER." Rev. J. E. Ratten bury on Mission Work in London.</p>
        <p>"The Hand of the Potter " is the title of a striking book in which Harold Begbie shows what remarkable moral reformations have been effected through the agritcy of Christianity. It is well known that Mr Begbie found mcst of the material for ,his book in the great Christian and social work of thPsWest London Mission, of which the Rev J E Rittenbury is the superintendent. On Thursday Mr Rattenbury paid another of his welcome visits to Warrington and lectured in Bold-street Wesleyan Church on the ever- fasoinating theme of the West London Mission and its rescue operations. There was a large congregation, Mr C E Parker presiding. Mr Rattenbury is the inheritor of the traditions and spirit of the mission's great founder, the Rev Hugh Price Hughes. The Mission began as the West London Mission in 18S7 and it had its home for many years in St. James' Hall. On the demolition of this hall the Mission found a temporary headquarters in the Lyceum Theatre, while the new Kingsway Hall was being built. This building lately opened occupies a commanding position in the broad new thoroughfare. It has accom- modation for over 2,000 people and is crowded every Sunday. Surrounding the Central Hall are various rooms for meetings. There are all the accessories associated with the idea of an institutional church and these are already in full use. The dominant note of the Mission has ever been evangelical Christianity, coupled with what might be called the Social Gospel. But Mr Rattenbnry would claim that this was not a tacking on of the Gospel but an integral part; he would probably say that the message of the Mission was " Conversion for the individual, righteousness for the State, and a call to all for service and for sacrifice." The CHAIRMAN, in the course of a brief speech, said this was a great age, and everything that affected human beings collectively seemed to he assuming larger pro- portions Having referred to the tendency to amal,,,,a- [nation in industrial affairs, Mr Parker touched upon the enormous waste of physical, mental and spiritual activity involved by the many divisions in our denom- inational system. He pictured numerous little congregations engaged in singing about the Church marching on to war and " hell's foundations trembling" at the attack, but added that one was almost inclined to think that if hell's foundations did tremble as the result of our split-up denominational methods, thrse foundations must have been put together with uncommonly poor cement. (Laughter ) The large missions connected with the Wesleyan society were in contrast to the stagnant conditions which held good in connection with the ordinary efforts of almost every denomination, proving a great success, and it was to that branch of the Church's work that they looked with most hope for the future. (Applause). In his powerful address Mr Rattenbury emphasised the fact that the conversion of the individual was the dominant note of the mission from the beginning. He combatted the idea that conversion belonged to a differ- ent age, and was only applicable to particular individuals and races.He contended that the experience gained in the Mission established beyond question the applicability of the Gospel to every human being, and this for all time and all races, and gave many striking illustrations to prove this point. In dealing with the world-wide influence of the Mission, Mr Rattenbury spoke of several instances which came to his notice in his recent visit to America. He told how on board ship, after public meetings, in private interviews, in the States and in Canada of the many who had come to him with particulars of individual personal experience of the good they had received at the West London Mission. The rev gentleman also emphasized the sadness and dangers of the loneliness of London, especially to the many young people who come up from all parts of the country as shop assistants and the like. He described how at the Mission they had made a special effort to provide a home for these lonely ones, and told that in his membership there were over 300 young shop assistants, mostly from the country. One of the most impressive parts of the teverend gentleman's address efts his reference to the work of the Sisterhood, first organized as a Methodist Institution by the founder of the Mission and subjected at its formation to a consider- able amount of suspicion and distrust. Mr Rattenbury, in a very earnest and graphic passage, described tho heroic work of Sister Mildred in the rescue of fallen women, a heroism as great and greater than had been manifested by the heroes of early Methodism. Mr Rattenbttry claimed the support of the provinces for the Mission on the ground of the national and cosmo- politan character of its ministry and service. The audience responded to the appeal in a very practical way, the collections at the afternoon service and evening meeting amounting to £37.</p>
      </div>
