      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>TURTLE AND INFLUENZA.</head>
        <p>TURTLE AND INFLUENZA.</p>
        <p>At this season of the year, when influenza is rife, the danger of infection with this debilitating and dangerous disease is ever present. When we are tired or cold—particularly when both tired and cold—the natural disease-resisting forces of the body at e at low ebb, and we are at the mercy of the influenza germ. What the system needs at such moments is nourish- ment that can be assimilated rapidly enough to restore vitality at once: ordinary tonics and stimulants are useless, as they reduce the vital forces further in pro- ducing a fictitious and temporary strength. The finest and rn,}st rapid restorative food is turtle : it abounds in the phosphorous the system needs, in such easily digestible form that it is completely assimilated and its benefit felt within half-an-hour of taking it. Turtle has long been known to doctors for its rapid restorative effects, and they have always prescribed it in cases of nervous debility, exhaustion, and in con- valescence, for those who could afford it. In the past turtle has been extremely dear. To-day the enterprise of Bellis's, the largest firm in the turtle importing trad,% has brought turtle within reach of all. In Bellis's Turtle Cup you have finest turtle in con- centrated form, fully prepared so that all you need to do is to add boiling water to it in order to prepare the most nourishing and delicious food drink imaginable. Bellis's Turtle Cup exercises to the full the invigorat- ing and revitalising effects of turtle. Ask your Doctor, You can obtain Bellis's Turtle Cup from any grocer, in shilling bottles ; or direct from Turtle Cup (1911), Ltd.. Dept. 53, 15 Bury-street, St. Mary Axe London. Be sure you get the genuine Bellis's Turtle Cup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "Mother Goose" Pantomime.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "Mother Goose" Pantomime.</p>
        <p>In its gorgeous and beautiful setting, the comic pantomime "Mother Goose," presented by Mr Gar Kiddie's Company at the Royal Court Theatre this week, has attracted large " houses " at both perform- ances: The story of the goose which laid the golden eggs is too well known to relate here, and all that need be said of the pantomime version is that nothing of any material value is " potted "so as in any way to lessen the humorous element which prevails,iu a large measure, throughout. The various characters are well sustained, Mr Arthur Carlton being excruciatingly funny in the title role. Kar and Kooney era delight- fully humorous as Parchment and squire Swank. They indulge in the most eccentric typo of mirth-making, keeping the audience in one continual roar. It is due in a large degree to their energies that the pantomime goes with the swing which has made it so popular in Warrington. The Three Merry Wags introduce a speci- ality for which they are loudly applauded. Miss Dorothy Dare is a dashing comedienne and male impersonator, portraying the character of Archie, the Squire's son, in quite a manly manner. Her songs are breezy and catchy and her bouyancy thoroughly grips the audience who join in the choruses with great gusto. The character of the two policemen Robert and Timothy are in the able hands of Frank Langford and Harry Hughes who aid in keeping the ball of humour rolling, as well as proving themselves capable harmonising vocalists. Miss Brenda White is a captivating Marjory and along with her lover Jack, a character undertaken by Blanche 1114 r-ion, she scores a decided success. Bert Rex as Billy is very popular, his drollery being crowned with wit and irresistible humour. Miss Anna Rutland is a captivating Benevo- lence and Mr Bert DlArcy a cunning and unscrupu- lous Avarice. There is an extensive ballet, the groupings being carried out with much taste for the artistic. The music is enchanting, Mr G Gerrard Sinclair being an able musical director. There will be a matinee at 2.30 to-day and two performance* to-night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
